        "theory": "s and p - Block Elements\nTeaching\n25\nAssessment\n3\nWeightage %\n22\nAfter studying this unit, students will be able to:\nRecognize the demarcation of the periodic table into s-block, p-block, d-block and f-block.(Understanding)\nDescribe how physical properties like atomic radius, ionization energy, electronegativity, electrical conductivity and melting and boiling points of elements change within a group and within a period in the Periodic Table. (Analysing)\nDescribe reactions of period 3 elements with water, oxygen and chlorine. (Applying)\nDescribe physical properties and acid-base behaviour of oxides, chlorides and hydroxides of period 3 elements. (Applying)\nDescribe reactions of oxides and chlorides of Period 3 elements with water. (Applying) Explain the trends in physical properties and oxidation states in Groups I, II, IV and VII of the periodic table. (Analysing)\nDescribe reactions of group I elements with water, oxygen and chlorine. (Applying) Explain effect of heat on nitrates, carbonates and hydrogen carbonates of Group I elements. (Applying)\nDescribe reactions of Group II elements with water, oxygen and nitrogen. (Applying) Discuss the trend in solubility of the hydroxides, sulphates and carbonates of Group II elements. (Analysing)\nDiscuss the trends in thermal stability of the nitrates and carbonates of Group II elements. (Analysing) Differentiate beryllium from other members of its group. (Analysing) Describe reactions of Group IV elements with water. (Applying)\nDiscuss the chlorides and oxides of Group Velements. (Applying)\nNe\nExplain the relative behaviour of halogens as oxidizing agents and reducing agents Applying) Compare the acidity of hydrogen halides. (Analysing)\nDistinguish between an oxide and a peroxide. (Understanding)\n30\nWrite representative equations for the formation of oxides and sulphides. (Applying) Compare the outermost s and p orbital system of an element with its chemical properties. (Analysing)\nCc 9\nFr Ra\n1\nK\nHT Pb Bi Po At Rn\nNh Fl Mc\nMc Lv Te Oa\nIntroduction\nTo easily understand the properties of the elements in the period table, the elements are placed in vertical columns and horizontal rows. The vertical columns are called groups or families and the horizontal rows are called the periods. The elements in the periodic table are divided into s, p, d and f blocks. Those elements in which last electrons occupy &#x27;s subshell (orbital) are called s-block elements. The s-block elements are at the extreme left of the periodic table and placed in Group IA (alkali metals) and Group IIA (alkaline earth metals) elements. Those elements in which last electrons occupy p subshell are called p-block elements. The p-block elements are placed in Groups IIIA, IVA, VA, VIA, VIIA\nand VIIIA.\nBoth s-block and p-block elements are normal elements. s-block elements consist of only metals but p-block elements include both metals and non-metals.\nMain-group elements\ns-block\nIA\n+15+\nΠΑ\nTransition elements d-block\n3-IIIB\nIIIB IVB VB VIB VIIB VIIIB\nIB IIB\n3d-\n58-\n4d-\nSd\ntd\nInner-transition elements f-block\np-block\nVIIIA\nIIIA IVA VA VIA VIIA\n-ls,\nSp\nFigure 13.1 Demarcation of the Periodic Table.\nIn previous grades, you have learnt about the periodic classification of elements in groups and periods. You have also learnt the basics about the 1&quot;, 2&quot; period elements. In this unit you will study more detail about the periodic trends and some properties of period (Na to Ar), Groups IA, IIA, IVA and VIIA elements.\n2\n*\n13.1 Period 3 (Na To Ar)\nNOT FOR SALE\nPeriod 3 elements consists of sodium (Na), magnesium (Mg), aluminium (Al), silicon (Si), phosphorous (P), sulphur (S), chlorine (CI) and argon (Ar). Sodium and magnesium are metals and belong to s-block. Aluminum (metal), silicon (metalloid), phosphorus, sulphur, chlorine and argon are non-metals and belong to p-block.\n13.1.1 Physical and Atomic Properties of the Elements\nThe properties of the atoms of the elements are called atomic properties. On moving from. left to right, in a period and from top to bottom, in a group of the periodic table, atomic properties show a regular variations. Hence, these properties, are also called periodic properties. Certain important periodic and physical properties of period 3 elements have been discussed in this unit.\n13.1.1.1 Electronic Structure\nIn third period of the periodic table electrons are successively filled in 3s and 3p orbital of atoms of elements (Na to Ar). The electronic configurations of these elements are given below where [Ne] represents the electronic configuration of neon.\nTable 13.1 Elements, their Symbols and electronic configurations.\nName of element\nSymbol with atomic\nnumber\nElectronic configuration\n[Ne] 3s1\nSodium\n&quot;Na\nMagnesium\n12Mg\n[Ne]3s\nAluminium\n13AI\n[Ne]3s3p\nSilicon\n14Si&lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 460 210&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;s and p-Block Elements&quot; style=&quot;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #dbeafe;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0&quot;&gt;&lt;rect x=&quot;120&quot; y=&quot;55&quot; width=&quot;220&quot; height=&quot;105&quot; fill=&quot;#eff6ff&quot; stroke=&quot;#2563eb&quot; stroke-width=&quot;3&quot;/&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M175 55V160M230 55V160M285 55V160M120 108H340&quot; stroke=&quot;#60a5fa&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;152&quot; y=&quot;92&quot; font-size=&quot;18&quot;&gt;s block&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;243&quot; y=&quot;92&quot; font-size=&quot;18&quot;&gt;p block&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;176&quot; y=&quot;140&quot; font-size=&quot;16&quot;&gt;periodic-table regions&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;",
