        "id": "13.5",
        "title": "13.1.1.5 Trend in Electrical Conductivity",
        "theory": "lodine is found primarily in seawater and in the soil especially in coastal areas. At high elevations, iodine is often deficient in the water and soil. Lack of iodine can cause an abnormal enlargement of the thyroid gland, known as a goitre.\nA goitre is an abnormal enlargement of thyroid gland. Thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland located at the base of neck. The most common cause of goitres worldwide is a lack of iodine in the diet. Iodine deficiency may be made worse by a diet high in hormone-inhibiting foods, such as cabbage, broccoli and cauliflower.\nOur body does not naturally make iodine, so the only way to get this nutrient is through diet. Adults typically require 150μg per day. Pregnant and breastfeeding women need 200μg per day.\n13.1.3 Physical Properties of the Oxides\nThe oxides of period 3rd elements are following.\nNa,O\nMgO\nAl,O,\nSiO2\nP010\nSo,\nCI,O,\nPO\nSo,\nC12O\nThe oxides in the top row are the oxides where period 3 elements are in their highest oxidation state. In these oxides, all the outer electrons in period 3 elements are being involved in bonding from just one in sodium to all seven outer electrons of chlorine in Cl2O,. The oxides (high oxidation state) of sodium, magnesium, aluminium, silicon, phosphorous are in solid state and those of sulphur and chlorine are in liquid state. The oxides having low oxidation state of sulphur and chlorine are in gaseous state.\n10\n13.1.3.1 Structure\nSodium, magnesium and aluminium oxides consist of giant structures containing metal ions and oxide ions. Magnesium oxide is similar in structure to sodium chloride. The crystalline aluminium oxide is known as corundum, in which oxygen ions form nearly hexagonal close-packed structure with the aluminium ions.\nNOT FOR SALE\nTidbit\nCorundum, naturally occurring that is, after diamond, the hardest aluminum oxide mineral (Al,O,) known natural substance.\nSilicon dioxide is covalent oxide having giant structure. There are three different crystal forms of silicon dioxide; the most suitable structure is similar to that of diamond. Phosphorus, sulphur and chlorine form molecular oxides. Some of these molecules are simple, while others are polymeric. Thus, the trend in structure in period is\nGiant metallic ionic oxides\n13.1.3.2 Melting and Boiling Points\ngiant covalent oxide\nmolecular oxide.\nThe giant structures (the metal oxides and silicon oxide) have high melting points and boiling points because a lot of energy is needed to break the strong bonds (ionic or covalent) operating in their lattices. The oxides of phosphorous, sulphur and chlorine consist of individual molecules, some small and simple, others polymeric.\npresent in\nThe attractive forces between these molecules will be dispersion forces and dipole-dipole interactions. The strength of the attractive forces depend on the size, shape and polarity of various molecules but are always much weaker than the ionic or covalent bonds a giant structure. These oxides are gases, liquids or low-melting solids. Table 13.8 Melting and Boiling Points of Period 34 Oxides\nOxides of period 3rd Na,0\nMelting point (°C)\n1610\n-17\n-92\n45\n82\nMgO AL,O, SIO, PO, SO, CIO, 1280 2900 2040\n340\nBoiling point (°C) 1950 3600 2977 2230 360\n13.1.3.3 Electrical Conductivity None of the period 3 oxides have any free or mobile electrons. It means that none of them will conduct electricity when they are in solid state. The ionic oxides can, however, conduct electricity in molten state because of the movement of ions towards the opposite electrodes.\n13.1.4 Acid-Base Behaviour of the Oxides\nGoing across period 3, from left to right the nature of the oxides changes from basic (Na,O,MgO) to amphoteric (Al,O,) and then to acidic (SiO,,P,O,P,O,SO,/SO,). The acidity of the oxides increases across the period.\n11\n13.1.4.1 Trend in Acid Base Behaviour\nAs we move in 3rd period from left to right, acidity of oxides increases, ranging fr strongly basic oxides on the left to strongly acidic on the right side, with an amphote oxide (aluminium oxide) in the middle.\nThis trend applies only to the highest oxidation of the individual elements (see the top n of the table). For example, sulphur trioxide reacts with water to form sulphuric acid. Th however, also react with bases such as sodium hydroxide to form salts such as sodi sulphate.\nTable 13.9 Trend in Acid Base Behaviour of Period 3 Oxides.\nOxide of period 3\nNa2O\nMgO\nA1,0,\nSiO, P2O10 SO, Cl2 (P,0.) (SO2) (Cl\nNature of Basic\noxide (alkaline)\nBasic\n(weakly Amphoteric Acidic Acidic Acidic Acie alkaline)\n13.1.4.2 Reactions of Oxides with Water, Acids and Bases\nThe period 3 consists of elements Na to Ar. There occurs metals, metalloids. non-metals. Oxides of Period 3&quot; elements are basic to amphoteric to acidic.\nReaction of oxides with water\nThe oxides of period 3&quot; include oxides of metals such as Na2O and MgO, which are in nature. They combine with water to form bases.\nNa2O+H2O\nMgO + H2O@\n2NaOH(aq)\nMg(OH)2(24)\ne in\nAl,O, does not react with H2O, since the strength of ionic bonds holding molecules lattice together is too much strong.\nSilicon dioxide does not react with H2O. The covalent bonds holding the silico atoms together in the 3-dimensional lattice are too strong to be broken down water molecules.\noxygen\nOxides of non-metals such as oxides of sulphur, phosphorous and chlorine react with to form oxyacids.\nOxides of the phosphorus such as P4010, react with water to produce acids.\nP4010(s)+6H2Oα)\n4H,PO40\nSulphur dioxide (SO2) and sulphur trioxide (SO3) react with water to produce sulp acid and sulphuric acid respectively.\n12\nC\nW\ny,\nm\nSO2(g) + H2O) SO3(g) + H2O(1)",
