        "id": "13.11",
        "title": "13.2.4 Reactions with Chlorine",
        "theory": "In Group - IA the lithium carbonate decomposes on the same way as the carbonates of Group-IIA. Group-IIA carbonates tend to decompose on heating to produce metal oxide\nand carbon dioxide.\nIn Group - IA, lithium carbonate behaves in the same way, producing lithium oxide an\ncarbon dioxide\nLi, Cos\nLi2O + CO20)\nThe rest of the Group - IA carbonates do not decompose at low temperature, although higher temperature this becomes possible. The decomposition temperatures again increas\ndown the group.\n22\nEffect of Heat on Hydrogen Carbonates\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe hydrogen carbonates of Group - IA are stable enough to exist as solid, although they do decompose easily on heating. For example,\n2NaHCO\nNa,CO+CO+H2O\nExplaining the Trend in Terms of the Polarizing ability of the Positive ion\nA small positive ion has a large amount of charge packed into a small volume of space. An ion with a high charge density has a clear distorting effect on any nearby negative ions. A. larger positive ion has the same charge spread over a larger volume of space. Its charge density is therefore lower and it causes less distortion to nearby negative ions. Polarizing the Carbonate ion\nThe molecular structure of carbonate is given below:\nThis figure shows two carbon-oxygen single bonds and one double bond, with two oxygen atoms each carrying a negative charge.\nb.com\n0\n0-\nIn carbonate, bonds are identical and the charges are delocalized.\n2-\nThe positive ion attracts the delocalized electrons in the carbonate ion towards itself. The carbonate ion becomes polarized.\nThe delocalize\nelectrons are pulled towards the positive ion\n2-\n2+\nnd of the\nay to break away anx\nThis oxygen atom is well on the way to become an oxide ion\nFigure 13.2 Molecular structure of carbonate.\nWhen it is heated, the carbon dioxide gets free, leaving a metal oxide. The amount of heat required depends on the polarization of the ion. If the positive ion has only one positive charge, the polarizing effect is reduced. This is why the Group - IA compounds are more thermally stable than those in Group - IIA.\n23\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe smaller the positive ion, the higher the charge density and greater the effect on the\nless effect on the carbonate ions near them. In other words, carbonates become more\nthermally stable down the group.\nExtension to Nitrates and Hydrogen Carlunate-\nThe phenomena of polarization is exactly the same for nitrates and hydrogen carbonates. The small positive ions at the top of the group polarize the nitrate or hydrogen carbonate ions to a greater extent than the larger positive ions at the bottom of the group. Again, the Group - IA compounds need more heat than those in Group - IIA because the Group - IA ions are less polarizing. Thus, thermal stability of anion of Group - I and Group - II increases down the group. The reason is increasing size and decreasing charge density of metal ions.\n13.2.6 Flame Test: Origin of Flame Colours\nThe Group-IA elements and their salts impart a characteristic colour to the Bunsen flame. These colours differ from one another as shown below.\nTable 13.17 Characteristic Colour to Bunsen Flame of Group - IA Elements\nElements\nLithium\nLi\nSodium Na\nPotassium\nRubidium\nCesium\nK\nRb\nCs\nFlame Colour\nCrimson red\nGolden yellow\nBlue-\nLilac\nRed-violet\nviolet\nOn heating Group - IA elements or its salt (especially chlorides due to its more volatile nature in a flame), the electrons are excited easily to higher energy levels because of absorption of energy. When these electrons return to their ground states, they emit extra energy in the form of radiations, which fall in the visible region, thereby, imparting a\ncharacteristic colour to the flame.\n13.3 Group - IIA Elements\nGroup-IIA elements are commonly called alkaline earth metals. They do not exist free in nature. They exist in combined form. Magnesium and calcium are very abundant in\nthe rocks of Earth&#x27;s crust.\nElements of Group-IIA (alkaline earth metals) possess two electrons in the outermost (valence) s-orbital. They form di-positive ions by loss of both the electrons. The elements\nand their atomic number etc. are given in Table 13.18 below.\n24\nNOT FORSALE\nTable 13.18 The Electronic Configuration of Group - IIA Elements\nElements symbol\nwith atomic No\nBe\n12 Mg\n20 Ca\nElectronic configuration\n1s2, 2s2\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2\n[He]2s2",
