        "theory": "2Na(s) + 2H2Oα)\n2NaOH(aq) + H2(g)\nMagnesium reacts slowly with cold water but burns in steam. A clean coil of magnesium dropped into cold water eventually is covered with small bubbles of hydrogen, which floats it to the surface. Magnesium form a very thin layer of magnesium hydroxide on the surface, which tends to stop it from further reaction.\nMg(x) + 2H2Oα)\nMg(OH)2(aq) + H2(g)\nMagnesium burns in steam with its typical white flame to produce white magnesium oxide and hydrogen.\nMg) + H2O)\nMgO) + H2(g)\nAluminium powder when heated in steam produces hydrogen and aluminium oxide. This reaction is relatively slow with bulk because of the formation of strong aluminium oxide layer on the metal and build-up of even more oxide during reaction.\n2Al(s)+3H2O(g)\nAl2O3(8) + 3H2(g) Silicon react with steam at red heat to produce silicon dioxide and hydrogen.\nSi(s) + 2H2O(g)\nSiO2() + 2H2(g)\ne in\nChlorine dissolves in water to some extent to give a pale green solution. A reversible reaction takes place to produce a mixture of hydrochloric acid and chloric (1) acid (hypochlorous acid), which decompose to HCI on exposure to sunlight.\nCl2(g) + H2Om\nHOC)\nNet reaction: Cl2(g) + H2Om\nHCl(aq) + HOCl (4) HCl(aq) + 1/202(4) 2HC1(4)+1/2020\nThere is no reaction between argon and water.\nii.\nReactions of the Period 3rd Elements with Oxygen\nSodium burns in a limited supply of air (oxygen) with a pale yellow flame to produce a white solid of sodium oxide. In sodium oxide, the oxidation state of oxygen is -2.\n4Na(s) + O2(g)\n2Na2O\n8\nX\nSodium burns in an excess of air (oxygen) with a flame to produce a pale yellow solid of NOT FOR SALE sodium peroxide. In sodium peroxide the oxidation state of oxygen is -1.\n2Na() + O2(g)\nNa2O2(s)\nWhen sodium peroxide react with oxygen at high temperature and pressure, it produces a yellow-orange solid of sodium superoxide (NaO2). In sodium superoxide the oxidation. state of oxygen is -12.\nNa202(1) + O2(2)\n2NaO2(s)\nMagnesium burns in oxygen with an intense flame to give white solid magnesium oxide.\n2Mg) + O2(2)\n2MgO(s)\nAluminium burns in oxygen, if it is powdered, otherwise, the strong oxide layer on aluminium tends to inhibit the reaction.\n4Al()+302(8)\n2A1,000\nSilicon burns in oxygen if heated strongly enough. Silicon dioxide is produced.\nSi(s) + O2(a)\nSiO2(3)\nWhite phosphorus catches fire spontaneously in air, burning with white flame and produces clouds of white smoke. A mixture of phosphorus trioxide (phosphorus (III) oxide) and phosphorus pentaoxide (phosphorous (V) oxide) is formed.\nFor phosphorus trioxide\nP40)+30202)\nFor phosphorus pentaoxide\nPa(3) +502(s)\nP ̧06(s)\nP101062)\nSulphur burns in air or oxygen on gentle heating with a pale blue flame. It produces colourless sulphur dioxide gas.\nS() + O2(g)\nSO(g)\niii. Reactions of the Period 3rd Elements with Chlorine\nSodium burns in chlorine with a bright orange flame. White, solid sodium chloride is produced.\n2Na(s) + Cl2(g)\n2NaCl)\nMagnesium burns with its usual intense white flame to give white magnesium chloride.\nMg(s) + Cl2(g)\nMgCh\nAluminium reacts with chlorine by passing dry chlorine over aluminium foil heated in a long tube. Aluminium burns in stream of chlorine to produce pale yellow aluminium chloride.\n2Al()+3Cl2(2)\n2AICL60\n¥\n9\nNOT FOR SALE\nIf chlorine is passed over silicon powder, in a heated tube, it reacts to produce a colourless\nliquid silicon tetrachloride.\nSi(+2Cl2()\nSiCl40\nWhite phosphorous burns in chlorine to produce a mixture of two chlorides. Phosphorus trichloride (Phosphorus (III) chloride) and phosphorus pentachloride (phosphorus (V) chloride). Phosphorus trichloride is colourless fuming liquid and\nP4() + 6Clip)\nphosphorous pentachloride is a straw-coloured solid.\nP40 + 10Cl2(g)\n4PC10)\n4PC156)\nIf a stream of chlorine is passed over some heated sulphur, it reacts to form an orange, foul smelling liquid disulphur dichloride (S,CI,).\n256) + Cla\nSociety, Technology And Science\nSC120)\nBody need a certain amount of iodine in order to make a chemical known as thyroid hormone. Thyroid hormone controls body metabolism and other important functions in the body.",
