        "theory": "NaCl and MgCl2 are ionic and solids with high melting and boiling points. Large amount of heat is needed to break the strong ionic attractions.\nAICI, is solid at room temperature, has an ionic lattice with a considerable covalent character. At 180-190°C (depending on the pressure), aluminium chloride converts to molecular form (dimeric form), Al,CI,-\n*\n14\nNOT FOR SALE\nPCIs is a white solid, which sublimes at 163°C. As the temperature goes higher, the PCI dissociates (splits up reversibly) to give PCl3 and Clz.\nPCl5(s)\nThe rest are low melting point liquids.\n13.1.5.3 Electrical Conductivity\nPC130+ Cl2(g)\nNaCl and MgCl, are ionic in nature. They do not conduct electricity in solid state. But in molten state and aqueous solution these compounds conduct electricity.\nAICI, and PCI, do not conduct electricity in solid state, as the ions are not free to move. In the liquid state, they are converted into a covalent form and hence do not conduct electricity.\nThe chlorides of rest of the elements of period 3&quot; do not conduct electricity because they do not have any free ions or any free electrons to conduct electricity.\n13.1.5.4 Solubility in Water\nNaCl dissolves in water and dissociates into ions to form a neutral solution of sodium chloride.\n༨\nNaCl(s)\nH2O\nNa*(aq) + Cl(aq)\nMgCl dissolves in water and dissociates into ions to form a solution of magnesium chloride\nMgCl2()\nH,0\nMg2+(aq) +2C1 (24)\nAICI is hydrolysed (broken down by water) to give an acidic solution containing the complex species hexaaqua aluminium (III).\nAICI)+6H2O\n[Al(H2O)]*(aq) + 3CH(aq)\nThe acidity is caused by the high charge density of aluminium ion (Al3) polarising the water ligands (attached molecules), releasing free hydrogen ions into solution:\n[AI(H2O)]*()\n[AI(H,O),OH]+H\nSilicon tetrachloride (SiCl4), reacts very rapidly with water to form a strongly acidic solution. During the reaction, white fumes of hydrogen chloride gas are formed which dissolves in water forming hydrochloric acid.\nSiCl4 + 4H2O\nSi(OH)4(aq) + 4HCl(g)\nsilicic acid\nBoth of the phosphorus chlorides i.e. PCI, and PCl5, violently react with water giving trongly acidic solutions. During the reaction, white fumes of hydrogen chloride gas are formed which dissolves in water forming hydrochloric acid.\n15\nNOT FORSALE\nPC130\n+3H2O\nPCl5(s)\nPCl5+4H2O\nH2PO3(aq) +3HCl(g) H,PO4(4)+5HCl(g)\n13.1.6 Hydroxides of the Period 3rd Elements\nThe word &quot;hydroxide&quot; refers to a compound which contains either a OH ion or an -OH hydroxides are simple basic hydroxides due to the presence of hydroxide ions. These group covalently bonded to the atom of an element. The sodium and magnesium\nAluminium hydroxide, Al(OH),, like aluminium oxide, Al,O,, is amphoteric, it reacts with\nhydroxide ions react with hydrogen ions of acid to form water.\nboth acids and bases.\nAll the other elements e.g. Si, P, S and Cl, which have - OH groups covalently bonded to the atom of period 3 are acidic. They range from very weakly acidic silicic acid to the very\nstrong sulphuric acid or perchloric acid.\nOH\nHO-S-OH\nOH\northo salicic acid\nOH\nH0-5=0\nHO-P-OH\nOH\nphosphoric acid\nOH\n0=C=0\na\nT\na\nsulphuric acid\nperchloric acid\nTH\nfra\nsin\naz e in\n13.1.6.1 Sodium and Magnesium Hydroxides Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is a white solids having soapy touch. Skin contact with concentrated caustic soda can cause serious chemical burns. This is highly hygroscopic. Sodium hydroxide is only slightly soluble in alcohols. It is one of the most soluble substances in water evolving a considerable amount of heat.\nMagnesium hydroxide, unlike sodium hydroxide, it is classified as a weak base, so it has no exothermic reaction with water. Magnesium hydroxide is obtained as a white precipitate when caustic potash (KOH) solution is added to a soluble magnesium salt. Both hydroxides react with acids to form salts. For example, with dilute hydrochloric acid,\ncolourless solutions of sodium chloride or magnesium chloride are obtained.\nNaOH + HCl()\n50\nvic\nThe\nshe\nTal\nE\nNaCl(aq) + H2O(1)\n16",
