        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 7",
        "theory": "Mg(OH)2(aq) + 2HCl()\nNOT FOR SALE\nMgCl2(aq) + 2H2O\n13.1.6.2 Aluminium Hydroxide\nAluminium hydroxide is amphoteric in nature. Like sodium or magnesium hydroxides, Al(OH)3 reacts with acids which shows its basic nature. The Al(OH)3 reacts with dilute ydrochloric acid and forms a colourless solution of aluminium chloride.\nAI(OH)3(4)+3HCl()\nAIC13 (4) + 3H2O\nluminium hydroxide also shows acidic properties. It reacts with sodium hydroxide. lution and forms a colourless solution of sodium tetrahydroxoaluminate (III).\nAI(OH)3(aq) + NaOH(aq)\n8.1.6.3 Other Hydroxides\nNaAl(OH))\nSodium tetrahydroxoaluminate (III).\nher elements do not contain hydroxide ions but -OH group is covalently bonded to the oms of period 3 elements. These compounds are acidic in nature because they can loss\nfrom-OH group.\nthosilicic acid is very weak acid. Phosphoric acid is a weak acid but stronger than acetic d. Sulphuric acid (H,SO,) and perchloric acids are very strong acids.\ne strength of an acid depends on the stability of it&#x27;s anion. Higher the stability of an on, higher will be the acidic strength of compound.\n3.2 Group - IA Elements\nelements lithium (Li), sodium (Na), potassium (K), rubidium (Rb), caesium (Cs), and cium (Fr) constitute group - IA of the periodic table. These are known as alkali metals e they form oxide and hydroxides which combine with water to produce alkaline tion. Only the first three of these are safe to keep in the school laboratory. The rest are ently reactive.\ncharacteristic feature of the sub-group- IA is the ns&#x27; arrangement in the outermost\nThe elements symbol and their electronic configuration are given in Table 13.11. e 13.11 Elements Symbol with Atomic No and their Electronic Configuration\nment symbol with\natomic No.\n3Li\nElectronic configuration\n1s2, 2s1\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s1\n[He]2s&#x27;\n11Na\n[Ne]3s1\n19K\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p6, 4s\n[Ar]4s\n17\nNOT FOR SALE\n37Rb\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d 4p, 5s!\n[Kr]5s1\n55Cs\n1s2, 2s2, 2p6, 3s2, 3p6, 4s2, 3d10, 4p6, 5s2, 4d10,\n5p6, 6s\n[Xe]6s1\n87Fr\n[Rn]7s1\n[Kr] 4d1o, 5p6,6s2,4f45d6p,7s\nLike all metals, they are good conductor of heat and electricity. They are softer than other metals. They can be cut with a knife. They are lighter than other elements and have low melting points, boiling points and densities.\n13.2.1\nAtomic and Physical Properties\nThe elements in a group behave in similar way but they also show trends of variations in properties as given below.\n13.2.1.1 Trend in Atomic Radius\nAs we move in group from lithium to caesium, an extra shell of electrons is added to next. element. The addition of extra shells increases the atomic radius from Li to Cs.\nTable 13.12 Trend in Atomic Radius in Group IA Elements\nElements\nLi\nNa\nK\nRb\nCs\nAtomic Radius (pm)\n152\n186\n227\n248\n265\nto the\n13.2.1.2 Trend in First Ionization Energy\nAs we move in group from lithium to caesium the ionization energy decreases, due addition of extra shells from Li to Cs. So the removal of electron become easier. Table 13.13 Trend in First lonization Energy in Group IA Elements\nElements\n1&quot; Ionization Energy\n(kJ/mol)\nto the\n1\nLi\nNa\nK\nRb\nCs\n520\n496\n419\n403",
