        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 9",
        "theory": "Reactions with Air or Oxygen and the formation of Normal Oxides,\nPeroxides, Super Oxides and their Stability\nWhen the group - IA elements are heated with excess of air or oxygen, lithium forms normal oxide, Li2O; sodium forms peroxide, Na,O,, whereas potassium, rubidium and caesium form superoxides having general formula MO,\n4Li)+02)\n300°C\n2Na) + O2(g)\nK(+026)\n2Li20)\nlithium oxide\nNa2O2\nsodium peroxide\nKO2\npotassium superoxide\ne in\n“Rb&quot; and &quot;Cs&quot; catch fire in air and produce superoxide such as RbO, and CsO, respectively. Thus, the reactivity of alkali metals with oxygen increases down the group.\n13.2.3.2 Reactions of Oxides with Water and Dilute Acids\ni. Reactions of Oxides with Water\nOxides of group - IA elements react with water to produce the respective bases. Group IA elements react with oxygen to form normal oxides, peroxides or superoxides. These oxides react with water in different ways.\n⚫ Normal oxides\nNormal oxide reacts with water and produce metal hydroxide. For example, Li2O reacts with water and form lithium hydroxide.\nLi2O+H2O\nX\n2LiOH,\n20\nPeroxides\nNOT FOR SALE\nPeroxides (M,O,) react with water and produce metal hydroxide and hydrogen. peroxide. For example, Na,O, reacts with water and form sodium hydroxide and hydrogen peroxide.\nNa200+2H2O(1)\nSuper oxides\n2NaOH + H2O2(0)\nSuper oxides (MO,) react with water and produce metal hydroxide, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas. For example, KO, reacts with water and forms potassium hydroxide, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas.\n2KO 200+ 2H2O\nii. Reactions of Oxides with Dilute Acids\n2KOH+H2O2 + O2(8)\nOxides of group - IA elements react with dilute acids to produce the respective salts. Oxides of group - IA i.e. normal oxides, peroxides, or super oxides react with dilute acids as follows.\ndes\n⚫ Normal oxides\nNormal oxides such as Li2O and Na,O react with dilute sulphuric acid (H2SO,) and produce the respective salts and water.\nLi2O+H2SO\nNa2O +H2SO404\n⚫ Peroxides\nLi2SO4(4) + H2O\nNa,SO4(4) + H2O\nPeroxides, such as Na,O,, react with dilute sulphuric acid (H,SO,) and produce sodium sulphate and hydrogen peroxide.\nNa20200 +H2SO4(4)\nSuper oxides\nNa2SO1(aq) + H2O2(\nSuper oxides such as KO,, react with dilute sulphuric acid (H,SO,) and produce potassium sulphate, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas.\n2KO 26 +H2SO40\nK2SO4 + H2O) + O2()\n13.2.4 Reactions with Chlorine\nGroup - IA elements react vigorously with chlorine to form metal chlorides. These metal chlorides are ionic crystalline solids. For example, sodium burns with an intense orange flame in chlorine in exactly the same way as it does in pure oxygen.\n21\n2Na+ Cla\n2NaCl\n2KCL\nNOT FOR SALE\n2K) + Cl2(g)\nReactivity of alkali metals with particular halogens increases from Li to Cs.\n13.2.5\nEffect of Heat on Nitrates, Carbonates and Hydrogen-Carbonates explaining the Trend in Terms of the Polarizing Ability of the\nPositive Ion\nCompounds of Group - IA elements are more stable to heat than the corresponding compounds of Group - IIA elements with the exception of lithium compounds.\nEffect of Heat on Nitrates\n-\nIn Group – IA the lithium nitrates decomposes on the same way as the nitrates of Group-IIA. Group - IIA nitrates tend to decompose on heating to produce metal oxide, nitrogen dioxide (brown fumes) and\noxygen.\nTidbit\nThe ability of a cation to distort\nan anion is known as its polarization power and the tendency of the anion to become polarized by the cation is known as its polarizability.\nLithium nitrate on decomposition produces lithium oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen.\n4LINO 300\nA\n2Li,O+4NO3+02\nThe rest of nitrates from sodium to caesium, however, do not decompose completely at low temperatures. At high temperature, the nitrates of these elements produce the respective metal nitrite (MNO,) and oxygen, but no nitrogen dioxide. (Where M = Na, K, Rb and Cs)\n2MNO\n2MNO2(3) + 03\nAs we go down the group, the decomposition of these metal nitrates becomes more difficult and higher temperature is required.\nEffect of Heat on Carbonates\navtal",
