        "sciNote": "In Group - IA the lithium carbonate decomposes on the same way as the carbonates of Group-IIA. Group-IIA carbonates tend to decompose on heating to produce metal oxide\nand carbon dioxide.\nIn Group - IA, lithium carbonate behaves in the same way, producing lithium oxide an\ncarbon dioxide\nLi, Cos\nLi2O + CO20)\nThe rest of the Group - IA carbonates do not decompose at low temperature, although higher temperature this becomes possible. The decomposition temperatures again increas\ndown the group.\n22\nEffect of Heat on Hydrogen Carbonates\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe hydrogen carbonates of Group - IA are stable enough to exist as solid, although they do decompose easily on heating. For example,\n2NaHCO\nNa,CO+CO+H2O\nExplaining the Trend in Terms of the Polarizing ability of the Positive ion\nA small positive ion has a large amount of charge packed into a small volume of space. An ion with a high charge density has a clear distorting effect on any nearby negative ions. A. larger positive ion has the same charge spread over a larger volume of space. Its charge density is therefore lower and it causes less distortion to nearby negative ions. Polarizing the Carbonate ion\nThe molecular structure of carbonate is given below:\nThis figure shows two carbon-oxygen single bonds and one double bond, with two oxygen atoms each carrying a negative charge.\nb.com\n0\n0-\nIn carbonate, bonds are identical and the charges are delocalized.\n2-\nThe positive ion attracts the delocalized electrons in the carbonate ion towar"
