        "id": "13.12",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 11",
        "theory": "[Ne]3s2\n[Ar]4s2\n38 Sr\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p6, 4s2, 3d10,4p6, 5s2\n[Kr]5s2\n56 Ba\n1s2, 22, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d, 4p, 5s2,4d, 5p, 6s2\n[Xe]6s*\n88 Ra\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d, 4p, 5s2, 4d10, 5p, 6s2, 4f14, 5d1o, 6p, 7s2\n[Rn]7s2\nAll Group - IIA metals except beryllium are white in colour. They are quite reactive and tarnish in air. The values of their densities, melting points and boiling points are higher than those of alkali metals.\n13.3.1\nAtomic and Physical Properties\n13.3.1.1 Trend in Atomic Radius\nThe atomic radii increase down the group due to the successive addition of extra shell of electrons to each element from Be to Ba.\nTable 13.19 Trend in Atomic Radius in Group - ILA Elements\nElements\nAtomic radius\n(pm)\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\n112\n150\n197\n215\n217\n13.3.1.2 Trend in First Ionization Energy\nThe first ionization energy of these elements decreases with increase of atomic radii from Be to Ba.\nTable 13.20 Trend in First lonization Energy in Group IIA Elements\nElements\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\n1st Ionization energy\n899.5\n737.7\n589.8\n549.5\n502.6\n(kJ/mol)\n13.3.1.3 Trend in Electronegativity\nThe electronegativity values of these elements are small and decrease from Be to Ba.\n25\nx\nTable 13.21 Trend in Electronegativity in Group IIA Elements\nNOT FOR SALE\nElements\nElectronegativity\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\n1.57\n1.31\n1.00\n0.95\n0.89\n13.3.1.4 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points\nMelting point: If we exclude the magnesium, there is a general decrease in melting point going down Group - IIA. However, if you include magnesium, you will see that its melting point is lower than the melting point of calcium, the next element. If you include magnesium, there is no obvious trend in melting points\nBoiling point: There does not appear to be a trend in boiling points going down the group. Again magnesium is an anomaly.\nTable 13.22 Trend in Melting and Boiling points in Group - IIA Elements\nElement\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\nMelting point (&quot;C) Boiling point (°C)\n1278\n651\n843\n769\n725",
        "content": "[Ne]3s2<br>[Ar]4s2<br>38 Sr<br>1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p6, 4s2, 3d10,4p6, 5s2<br>[Kr]5s2<br>56 Ba<br>1s2, 22, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d, 4p, 5s2,4d, 5p, 6s2<br>[Xe]6s*<br>88 Ra<br>1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d, 4p, 5s2, 4d10, 5p, 6s2, 4f14, 5d1o, 6p, 7s2<br>[Rn]7s2<br>All Group - IIA metals except beryllium are white in colour. They are quite reactive and tarnish in air. The values of their densities, melting points and boiling points are higher than those of alkali metals.<br>13.3.1<br>Atomic and Physical Properties<br>13.3.1.1 Trend in Atomic Radius<br>The atomic radii increase down the group due to the successive addition of extra shell of electrons to each element from Be to Ba.<br>Table 13.19 Trend in Atomic Radius in Group - ILA Elements<br>Elements<br>Atomic radius<br>(pm)<br>Be<br>Mg<br>Ca<br>Sr<br>Ba<br>112<br>150<br>197<br>215<br>217<br>13.3.1.2 Trend in First Ionization Energy<br>The first ionization energy of these elements decreases with increase of atomic radii from Be to Ba.<br>Table 13.20 Trend in First lonization Energy in Group IIA Elements<br>Elements<br>Be<br>Mg<br>Ca<br>Sr<br>Ba<br>1st Ionization energy<br>899.5<br>737.7<br>589.8<br>549.5<br>502.6<br>(kJ/mol)<br>13.3.1.3 Trend in Electronegativity<br>The electronegativity values of these elements are small and decrease from Be to Ba.<br>25<br>x<br>Table 13.21 Trend in Electronegativity in Group IIA Elements<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Elements<br>Electronegativity<br>Be<br>Mg<br>Ca<br>Sr<br>Ba<br>1.57<br>1.31<br>1.00<br>0.95<br>0.89<br>13.3.1.4 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points<br>Melting point: If we exclude the magnesium, there is a general decrease in melting point going down Group - IIA. However, if you include magnesium, you will see that its melting point is lower than the melting point of calcium, the next element. If you include magnesium, there is no obvious trend in melting points<br>Boiling point: There does not appear to be a trend in boiling points going down the group. Again magnesium is an anomaly.<br>Table 13.22 Trend in Melting and Boiling points in Group - IIA Elements<br>Element<br>Be<br>Mg<br>Ca<br>Sr<br>Ba<br>Melting point (&quot;C) Boiling point (°C)<br>1278<br>651<br>843<br>769<br>725",
        "rules": [],
        "sciNote": "[Ne]3s2\n[Ar]4s2\n38 Sr\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p6, 4s2, 3d10,4p6, 5s2\n[Kr]5s2\n56 Ba\n1s2, 22, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d, 4p, 5s2,4d, 5p, 6s2\n[Xe]6s*\n88 Ra\n1s2, 2s2, 2p, 3s2, 3p, 4s2, 3d, 4p, 5s2, 4d10, 5p, 6s2, 4f14, 5d1o, 6p, 7s2\n[Rn]7s2\nAll Group - IIA metals except beryllium are white in colour. They are quite reactive and tarnish in air. The values of their densities, melting points and boiling points are higher than those of alkali metals.\n13.3.1\nAtomic and Physical Properties\n13.3.1.1 Trend in Atomic Radius\nThe atomic radii increase down the group due to the successive addition of extra shell of electrons to each element from Be to Ba.\nTable 13.19 Trend in Atomic Radius in Group - ILA Elements\nElements\nAtomic radius\n(pm)\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\n112\n150\n197\n215\n217\n13.3.1.2 Trend in First Ionization Energy\nThe first ionization energy of these elements decreases with increase of atomic radii from Be to Ba.\nTable 13.20 Trend in First lonization Energy in Group IIA Elements\nElements\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\n1st Ionization energy\n899.5\n737.7\n589.8\n549.5\n502.6\n(kJ/mol)\n13.3.1.3 Trend in Electronegativity\nThe electronegativity values of these elements are small and decrease from Be to Ba.\n25\nx\nTable 13.21 Trend in Electronegativity in Group IIA Elements\nNOT FOR SALE\nElements\nElectronegativity\nBe\nMg\nCa\nSr\nBa\n1.57\n1.31\n1.00\n0.95\n0.89\n13.3.1.4 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points\nMelting point: If we exclude the magnesium, there is a general decrease in melting point going down Group - IIA. However, if you inc"
