        "theory": "a) Variation in a Group\nAs we move in a group from top to bottom, there is an increase in the atomic size, nuclear charge and shielding effect. However, the combined effect of increase in atomic size and shielding effect dominates over the effect of increase in the nuclear charge. Hence, force of attraction of the nucleus for the electron to be added decreases resulting in a decrease in electron-affinity values.\nHowever, it has been seen that fluorine has lower electron affinity than chlorine because fluorine has very small size and high charge density which opposes the electron to be added. Electron affinity generally decreases in the following order (I&lt;Br&lt;F&lt;CI).\n38\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 13.27 Trend in Electron Affinity in Group-VIIA Elements\nHalogen\nF\nCI\nBr\nElectron Affinity (kJ/mol) -328.0\n-349.0\n-324.6\n-295.2\n13.5.1.4 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points\nThe melting and boiling points of Group - VIIA increases down the group due to increase\nin strength of the Van der Waals forces. This trend is given below.\nTable 13.28 Trend in Melting and Boiling Points in Group-VIIA Elements\nHalogen\nF\nCI\nBr\nI\nMelting point (&quot;C)\n-220.0\n-101.0\n-7.2\n+114.0\nBoling point (°C)\n-188.0\n-35.0\n+58.8\n+184.0\n13.5.1.5 Bond Enthalpies\nBond enthalpy is the amount of heat required to break one mole of covalent bonds to form individual atoms. It begins with the original substance in a gaseous state and ending with gaseous atoms.\nاصلی\nCI-Cl\n2C\n13.5.1.5.1 Bond Enthalpies in Halogens\nBond breaking is an endothermic process and bond enthalpy involved is given positive sign. The bond enthalpies of halogens are given below.\nTable 13.29 Bond Enthalpies in Halogens\nBond\nF-F\nCI-CI\nBr-Br\nI-I\nBond length (pm)\n144\n199\n228\n267\nBond enthalpy (kJ/mol)\n159\n243\n193\n151\nThe abnormal behavior in F-F bond is due to its smaller size and strong repulsion amongst the non-bonded electrons (lone pair) of fluorine atoms. Hence bonds become weak, though bond is short. Thus, enthalpy of fluorine is less than the other halogens.\n39\n13.5.1.5.2 Bond Enthalpies in Hydrogen Halides\nNOT FOR SALE\nDown the group the halogen atoms get bigger, the bonding pair of electrons get more and more distant from the nucleus, the attraction is less and the bond becomes weaker. So the bond enthalpies of hydrogen halides decreases down the group. The bond enthalpies are very important in the thermal stability of hydrogen halides.\nH-F and H-Clare stable to heat and they do not decompose easily. H-Br decomposes slightly into hydrogen and bromine on heating and H-1 decomposes to an even greater extent. As the bonds get weaker, they are more easily broken. Bond enthalpies of halides are given below.\nTable 13.30 Bond Enthalpies in Hydrogen Halides &amp;xidize: Co confibine with\noxygen or other wise\nMake an oxide\nH-I H-Br\nHydrogen Halide\nH – F-\nH-CI\nBond enthalpy (kJ/mol)\n+568.2\n+431.9\n+366.1\n+298.3\nbecues reducd.\nanother: in so doing, it An oxidizing agent is a substance that removes electrons from other substances during a\nAny Substance that oxidizes, or\nreceives elections from redox reaction. The oxidizing agent takes these electrons for itself, thus gaining electrons and getting reduced. An oxidizing agent is thus an electron acceptor. The halogen accepts electron during the reaction and acts as oxidizing agent.\n13.5.2 Strength of Halogens as Oxidizing Agents\n2Na + Cli",
