      "Na,CO+CO+H2O",
      "Explaining the Trend in Terms of the Polarizing ability of the Positive ion",
      "A small positive ion has a large amount of charge packed into a small volume of space. An ion with a high charge density has a clear distorting effect on any nearby negative ions. A. larger positive ion has the same charge spread over a larger volume of space. Its charge density is therefore lower and it causes less distortion to nearby negative ions. Polarizing the Carbonate ion",
      "The molecular structure of carbonate is given below:",
      "This figure shows two carbon-oxygen single bonds and one double bond, with two oxygen atoms each carrying a negative charge.",
      "b.com",
      "0",
      "0-",
      "In carbonate, bonds are identical and the charges are delocalized.",
      "2-",
      "The positive ion attracts the delocalized electrons in the carbonate ion towards itself. The carbonate ion becomes polarized.",
      "The delocalize",
      "electrons are pulled towards the positive ion",
      "2-",
      "2+",
      "nd of the",
      "ay to break away anx",
      "This oxygen atom is well on the way to become an oxide ion",
      "Figure 13.2 Molecular structure of carbonate.",
      "When it is heated, the carbon dioxide gets free, leaving a metal oxide. The amount of heat required depends on the polarization of the ion. If the positive ion has only one positive charge, the polarizing effect is reduced. This is why the Group - IA compounds are more thermally stable than those in Group - IIA.",
      "23",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "The smaller the positive ion, the higher the charge density and greater the effect on the",
      "less effect on the carbonate ions near them. In other words, carbonates become more",
      "thermally stable down the group.",
      "Extension to Nitrates and Hydrogen Carlunate-",
      "The phenomena of polarization is exactly the same for nitrates and hydrogen carbonates. The small positive ions at the top of the group polarize the nitrate or hydrogen carbonate ions to a greater extent than the larger positive ions at the bottom of the group. Again, the Group - IA compounds need more heat than those in Group - IIA because the Group - IA ions are less polarizing. Thus, thermal stability of anion of Group - I and Group - II increases down the group. The reason is increasing size and decreasing charge density of metal ions.",
