      "The nitrides of Group - IIA elements are ionic and solid in nature except beryllium nitride (Be,N,) which is covalent and volatile.",
      "i.",
      "13.3.4 Trend in Solubility of the Hydroxides, Sulphates and Carbonates",
      "Solubility of the Hydroxides",
      "Group-II metal hydroxides become more soluble in water as we go down the group.",
      "Magnesium hydroxide appears to be insoluble in water. However, if it is shaken in water and filtered, the solution is slightly basic (pH = 9.0). This is because some magnesium hydroxide has been dissolved.",
      "water will",
      "Calcium hydroxide is partially soluble in water. A suspension of calcium hydroxide in appear at more alkaline (pH = 11) than magnesium hydroxide as it is more soluble so there will be more hydroxide ions present in solution of magnesium hydroxide.",
      "Barium hydroxide is soluble enough to produce a solution with a concentration around 0.1 mol dm3 at room temperature. The hydroxide ions present would make the solution",
      "27",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "strongly alkaline.",
      "но",
      "Ba2+",
      "Ba(OH)2(8)",
      "+20H",
      "(aq)",
      "Tidbit",
      "ii.",
      "Magnesium hydroxide is used in medicine (in suspension as milk of magnesia) to neutralise",
      "excess acid in the stomach and to treat constipation.",
      "Mg(OH)2 + 2HC",
      "MgCl2(g) + 2H,Om",
      "An aqueous solution of calcium hydroxide is called limewater and can be used as a",
      "test-reagent for carbon dioxide.",
      "Solubility of the Sulphates",
      "Group – IIA sulphates become less soluble as we move down the group. BaSO, is the least soluble. This simple trend is true provided that hydrated beryllium sulphate is considered, but not for the anhydrous beryllium sulphate. The anhydrous beryllium sulphate, BeSO, is insoluble, whereas the hydrated form, (BeSO, 4H,O), is soluble. Solubility of magnesium sulphate and calcium sulphate also depends on whether the salt is hydrated",
      "or not.",
      "iii.",
      "Solubility of the Carbonates",
      "The carbonates become less soluble down the group. All the Group - IIA carbonates are sparingly soluble, while all dissolve in acids. The trend to lower solubility is, however, broken at the bottom of the group.",
      "Barium carbonate is slightly more soluble than strontium carbonate. As we move downward in the group, the energy required to break the lattice of carbonates decreases, because the positive ions get larger. The larger the ions, the more distance there is between ions. Thus, the forces holding these ions together become weaker. This increases the solubility. On the other hand, when the size of the positive ions become larger downward in the group, the energy released by the ions during hydration also falls because bigger ions are not so strongly attracted to the water molecules. This decreases the solubility of carbonates. The effect of decrease in hydration energy is greater than decrease in lattice energy in case of carbonates of IIA down the group. Thus, the solubility of carbonates decreases.",
      "13.3.5 Trend in Thermal Stability of the Nitrates and Carbonates",
      "i. Thermal Stability of the Group-IIA Nitrates",
      "All the nitrates of this group undergo thermal decomposition to the metal oxide, nitroger dioxide and oxygen gas. Thermal decomposition means the splitting up of a compound by",
      "28",
      "NOT FOR SALE heating. The following reactions describe the thermal decomposition of the nitrates (Where M = Be, Mg, Ca, Ba and Sr).",
      "2M(NO3)200 2Ca(NO3)26)",
      "2M0+4NO+O",
      "2CaO+4NO) +020",
      "The nitrates become more thermally stable down the group, i.e. they require more heating to decompose.",
      "ji. Thermal Stability of the Group - IIA Carbonates",
      "All the carbonates of this group undergo thermal decomposition, to the metal oxide and carbon dioxide gas.",
      "The following reactions describe the decomposition of the carbonate (Where M = Be, Mg, Ca, Ba and Sr).",
      "MCO",
      "CaCO",
      "200",
      "n",
      "MO+CO CaO +CO",
      "The carbonates become more thermally stable down the group, they require more heating to decompose.",
      "13.3.6 How Beryllium Differs from other Members of its Group?",
      "Beryllium differs from other Group-IIA metals in many respects. This is partly due to its small size and partly due to its high electronegativity.",
      "Beryllium is the lightest of all the Group - IIA metals.",
      "Beryllium has higher melting and boiling point compared to other elements in Group - IIA.",
      "Beryllium unlike other Group - ILA metals is not easily affected by dry air",
      "Beryllium does not react with water or steam at red heat.",
      "The oxide of beryllium, BeO is amphoteric whereas, other oxides of Group - IIA metals are strong bases.",
      "Oxides, nitrides, chlorides etc. of Group-IIA elements are ionic, while those of beryllium are covalent.",
      "⚫ Salts of beryllium do not impart any colour during flame test unlike its other family",
      "members.",
      "Beryllium oxide is insoluble in water unlike oxide of other members of its family. Beryllium does not liberate hydrogen from acids as observed in other Group – IIA",
      "metals.",
      "29",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "13.3.6.1 Why is Beryllium Chloride Covalent and not lonic?",
      "Beryllium at the top of the group has high electronegativity (1.57) compared with the rest of the group members. High electronegativity means that it attracts a bonding pair of electrons towards itself more strongly than the rest of the Group - IIA elements. This gives covalent character to BeCl,.",
      "13.3.6.2 Amphoteric Beryllium Hydroxide",
      "Beryllium hydroxide can act both as an acid and as a base.",
      "L Beryllium Hydroxide as an Acid",
      "Beryllium hydroxide reacts with concentrated solution of sodium hydroxide to produce sodium tetrahydroxoberyllate.",
      "As an acid: Be(OH)2+2NaOH()",
      "Beryllium Hydroxide as a Base",
      "Na,[Be(OH),",
      "sodium tetrahydroxoberyllate",
      "Beryllium hydroxide reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid producing beryllium chloride as neutralization product.",
      "As a base: Be(OH)2(aq) + 2HCl(aq)",
      "BeCl2 + 2H2O",
      "13.4",
      "Group-IVA Elements",
      "The Group-IVA of the periodic table is often referred to as the carbon group. This group consists of five elements carbon (C), silicon (Si), germanium (Ge), tin (Sn) and lead (Pb). This group is present in the middle of periodic table forming a link between more electropositive elements of Group - IA to IIIA and more electronegative elements of Group VA to VIIA. Each element in this group contains four valence electrons. The elements of group - IV A evidentially exhibit an intermediate character. The general electronic configuration of Group - IVA elements in valence shell is ns, np2, where n varies from 2 (for carbon) to 6 (for lead), for example, the electronic configuration of carbon is 1s2,252, 2p2.",
      "13.4.1 Physical Properties: Melting and Boiling Points",
      "The melting point of carbon is extremely high. The melting and boiling points of silicon and germanium are very high because these elements have a very stable arrangement of solid structure (diamond-type lattice). Sn and Pb have a lower melting point because only two electrons are involved in bond formation instead of four, due to inert pair",
      "effect.",
      "30",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
