        "title": "14.1.3 Binding Energy",
        "theory": "upto Group - IIB. For example, in 3d transition series binding energy increases upto vanadium and then decrease upto zinc.\n14.1.4\nVariable Oxidation States Most of the transition metals show variable oxidation states and form compounds showing more than one (variable) oxidation states because energy levels of ns and (n-1)d are very close. The involvement of the d-electrons in addition to &#x27;ns&#x27; electrons in bond formation also take place. The oxidation state of 3d series is from +2 to +7 (except Cr and Cu).\nTidbit\nValency\nThe combining capacity of an atom is known as its valency. Some elements differ in their Variable Valency\ncapacity to combine with other elements depending on the nature of the reaction; this property is called\niron in ferrous oxide shows a variable valency. For example, the\nvalency of +2, whereas in ferric oxide, it has a valency of +3.\n51\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe lowest oxidation states of these elements are +1 or +2 which is due to their 4s\nelectrons.\nAs the number of unpaired electrons in 3d orbital increases the number of oxidation state also increases form Sc3 to Mn\n7+\nAfter Mn, the number of oxidation states decreases as the electrons starts pairing in 3d orbital and hence fewer unpaired electrons are available for bond formation, so oxidation states decreases from Fe to Zn. From iron to zinc only the weakly held electrons of &quot;4s&quot; subshell are involved in oxidation state.\nTable 14.3 Commonly Occurring Oxidation States of the 1 Transition Series\nElement\nname\nAtomic\nnumber\nElectronic configuration\nOxidation states\nScandium(Sc)\n21\n[Ar]4s2, 3d1\n2 3\nTitanium(Ti)\n22\n[Ar]4s2,3d2\n23 4\nVanadium(V)\n23\n[Ar]4s2, 3d\n2\n345\nChromium(Cr)\n24\n[Ar]4s&#x27;, 3ds\n2\n3 456\nManganese(Mn)\n25\n[Ar]4s2,3ds\n1\n2\n3\n4567\nIron(Fe)\n26\n[Ar]4s2, 3d\n1\n2\n3\n456\nCobalt(Co)\n27\n[Ar]4s2,3d&#x27;\n23 4\n5\nin\nNickel(Ni)\n28\n[Ar]4s2,3d\n23\n4\nCopper(Cu)\n29\n[Ar]4s&#x27;, 3d10 1 2 3\nZinc(Zn)\n30\n[Ar]4s2,3d10\n2\n14.1.5 Catalytic Activity\nMany transition metals and their compounds are used as catalysts, which influence the\nrate of chemical reaction.\nSome common examples are platinum (Pt), nickel (Ni), iron (Fe), chromium (Cr) and vanadium pentoxide (V2O,) etc. They are used as catalyst either because of their ability to change oxidation state or in the case of metals adsorbs other substance on their surface and activate them in the process.\n52\n3208",
