        "title": "14.1.5 Catalytic Activity",
        "theory": "12\nقابل\n72stha NOT FOR SALE Transition metals have variable oxidation states and hence are capable of forming\n+ Ivers intermediate compounds, which help in the formation of desired product. For example, finely divided iron is used as a catalyst in Haber&#x27;s process for the synthesis of ammonia, while V2O, is used in the conversion of SO2 to SO3 for the manufacture of H2SO, by contact process. TiCl4 is used in the polymerization of ethene to polythene polymer.\nThe application of nickel (Ni), palladium (Pd) or platinum (Pt) in the catalytic hydrogenation of unsaturated hydrocarbons and the use of copper (Cu) in the oxidation of ethanol to acetaldehyde are example of catalytic properties of transition elements. 2F a2 + $2082 - (y) 2502 + 2 Festo\n14.1.6Magnetic Behaviour\nSubstances show two different types of behaviours when subjected to an external magnetic field. If a substance is weakly attracted by a strong magnetic field, it is called paramagnetic substance, whereas a substance weakly repelled by magnetic field is called diamagnetic substance.\nParamagnetic behaviour of a substance is due to interaction of magnetic field produced by spinning of one or more unpaired electrons in an atom, ion or molecule with external magnetic field.\nIn diamagnetic substances the electrons are paired up due to which magnetic field of oppositely spinning electrons cancel each other.\nMetals like Fe, Co and Ni possess very high paramagnetic nature and attain permanent magnetic moment hence, they are ferromagnetic as shown by Fe and Mn2+ ions which have five unpaired electrons each.\n14.1.7 Alloy Formation 4 mixture containing two or more metallic Elements metallic and nonmetallic elements\nAn alloy is a mixture of a metal with another element, either métal or non-metal.\nusually fused together Transition metals have another great characteristic that they easily mix with each other. This is because all the d-block metals have similarity in their atomic sizes. Some transition metal atoms allow to replace easily in crystal lattice.\nAn alloy in which one of the components is mercury is known as amalgam. In alloys the chemical properties of the component elements are retained but certain physical properties are improved. Alloys are more shiny, stronger and harder than the pure metal. They are rigid, light weight, durable, thermally more stable, less reactive and corrosion resistant as compared to its component elements.\n0 dissolving into each other when molten.\nAn alloys of where 153 with another Metal (usually Selver) dentists to fill cavititios\nusedl\nMetals dissolve\nмежин\nٹانگا\nTable 14.4 Important Alloys and Their Uses\nNOT FOR SALE\nAlloys\nCompositions\nBrass\n)\nCu + Zn\nBronze\nCu + Sn\nGerman Silver Cu + Zn + Ni\nRolled Gold\nGun Metal\nDuralumin\nSolder\nCu + Al\nCu + Sn+Zn+Pb\nAl + Cu + Mg + Mn\nPb+Sn\nUses\nاورا\nIn making utensils\nIn making coins, bell and utensils.\nIn making utensils.\nIn making cheap ornaments.\nنفتی و نظار\nگاڑی کا اور ساڑی کا نیا بنداق\nil\nIn making guns, barrels, gears and bearings.\nFor making utensils.\nFor soldering.\nBell metal\nCu + Sn\nStainless steel\nNickel steel\nFe + Cr+Ni+C\nFe + Ni\nچاقو رہنے کا شد. For casting bells and statues\nFor making utensils and surgical cutlery.\nFor making electrical wire, automobile parts.\n14.2 Coordination Compounds\nCoordination compounds are those compounds in which a central atom or ion is attached with a fixed number of groups or molecules (ligands) through coordinate covalent bonds.\nin\nThese compounds can be divided into two classes depending on their behaviour in aqueous solution.\nZ\n1. Those which Lose their Identity in Solution L.c. Double Salts These compounds exist as stable compounds only in the solid crystalline state and break up into constituent units in aqueous solution. For example, when crystals of potash alum are dissolved in water, Al3+, K+ and SO4 ions are produced. This aqueous solution shows the same physical and chemical properties as a mixture of aqueous solution of aluminium sulphate and potassium sulphate. Some examples of double salts\nare,\nMohr&#x27;s salt:\nPotash alum:\nFeSO, (NH),SO,.6H2O Al,(SO),.K.SO.24H2O\nThese compounds lose their identity in the solution.\n54\nظام ہونا\n2. Coordination or Complex Compounds\nNOT FOR SALE\nمقید کرنا\nThese compounds exist as a stable compounds in the solid state and retain their identity in the solution. For example, when potassium ferrocyanide is dissolved in water, the aqueous solution does not show the characteristic test of Fe and CN ions.\nThe physical and chemical properties of the aqueous solution is entirely different from those of free Fe2* and CN ions.\nبالکل پورا کا پورا سر آسم\nIn coordination compounds, a central metal atom or ion is bonded to the surrounding electron-pair donors called ligands, through a coordinate bond. These compounds are also known as metal complexes because of the presence of complex metal ions. Some common examples of coordination compounds are,\nبخار بن کر اڑ جانا",
