        "theory": "Tin\nTungsten\nStannate\nTungstate\nZinc\nZincate\n7. Some coordination compounds are called by their common names. For example, [Fe(CN)6]3 and [Fe(CN)6] are named ferricyanide and ferrocyanide respectively and Fe(CO)s is called iron carbonyl.\nExamples: The systematic names for some coordination compounds are,\n59\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\n1.\nComplex Formula\n[Cr(NH,),(H2O),]CI,\n[Pt(NH,),CI]Br\nKa[Fe(CN)6]\nNa,[NICI,]\nPH(NH,),CH,\nFe(CO)\n(NH,),[Ni(C,D,),(H,O)]\n[Pt(H2NCH,CH2NH2),CI,]CI,\nComplex Name\ntriamminetriaquachromium(III) chloride\npentaamminechloroplatinum(IV) bromide\npotassium hexacyanoferrate(II)\nsodium tetrachloronickelate(II)\ndiamminetetrachloroplatinum(IV)\npentacarbonyliron(0)\nammonium diaquabis(oxalato)nickelate(II)\ndichlorobis(ethylenediamine)platinum(IV) chloride\n[Co(H2NCH2CH2NH2),1,(SO), tris(ethylenediamine)cobalt(III) sulphate\n14.2.3 Shapes of Complex Ions with Coordination Number 2, 4 and 6 Coordination compounds have definite geometrical shapes because the bond formed between central metal atom and the ligands are directional in nature. Hence, the ligands are arranged around central metal atom in a clear spatial arrangement. This arrangement depends on the number of ligands (co-ordination number) and the relative sizes of the metal ion and the ligands. As the ligand gets larger, fewer of them can coordinate to the metal ion. Thus, iron(III) is able to coordinate to six fluorides in [FeF6] but to only four chlorides in [FeCl4]&quot;\nin\nThere are various geometries for the coordination complexes. The most common geometries for coordination complexes are tetrahedral geometry, square planar geometry and octahedral geometry. Complexes in which the coordination number is 4 have two geometries-tetrahedral and square planar. Complexes with a coordination number of 6 always have an octahedral geometry. The coordination complex ions with coordination number 2, 4 and 6 is discussed here in detail.\n2-Coordinated Complex Ions\nThis arrangement is not very common for first\nrow transition metal ion complexes. These [H,NAgNH, complex ions can have only two possible\narrangements, i.e. linear or bent. The linear\nFigure 14.3 Coordinated complex.\narrangement is adopted by the central ion with two hybrid orbitals and is most commonly shown by complexes of Cu(I), Ag(I) and Au(I). For example, [CuCk], [Cu(NH3)2]*, [Ag(NH3)2], [Au(CN)2], etc.\n60\nNOT FOR SALE\nBent arrangement is found in case of [Ag(SCN)] which exists as a polymer in the solid\nstate.\nii. 4-Coordinated Complex Ions\nThese are far less common and they can take up one of two different shapes.\na. Tetrahedral Complexes\nTetrahedral geometry is the most\ncommon geometry adopted by\nCI\n2-\nCu\nCo\n[CuCl ]\ncomplexes of transition as well as non-transition elements. For example, [CuCl4], [CoCl2, etc. The copper (II) and cobalt (II) ions have four chloride ions bonded to them rather than six, because the chloride ions are too big to fit any more around the central metal ion. b. Square Planer Complexes\n[CoCl2\nFigure 14.4 Tetrahedral ion geometry.\nOccasionally a 4-coordinate complex turns out to be square planer. There is no easy way of predicting that this is going to happen. The only one you might possibly come across at this level is cisplatin (cis-diamminedichloroplatinum (II)), which is used as an anti-cancer drug.\nCI\nila\nH,N-Pt-:Cl\n↑\nNH,\nPt(NH,), Cl2\nH,N\nNH,\nPt\nCI\nCI\nCisplatin Cis-diamminedichloroplatinum (II)\nFigure 14.5 Square planer complex.\niii. 6-Coordinated Complex lons\nThese are complex ions in which the central metal ion is forming six co-ordinate covalent bonds or in simple words the central metal ion is attached to six ligands. These ions have an octahedral shape. Four of the ligands are in one plane, while the fifth one above the plane and the sixth one below the plane. The shapes of four examples of 6 - coordinated complex ions.\n61\nH2O.\nH2O\n• Fe\nH2O: 1\nH2Ö\n[Fe(H2O).]&quot;",
