        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 2",
        "theory": "elements.\nIn order to maintain a rational classification, the elements of group IIB and group IIIB are referred to as Non-Typical Transition Elements and the elements in the remaining transition series\nare called Typical Transition Elements.\nIn previous unit, you have studied the s-block and p-block elements. In this unit, you will study the d-block elements, their general features, coordination compounds and chemistry of some important transition elements in detail. The transition elements,for the sake of convenience, are divided into two main categories.\ni. the main transition elements or the &#x27;d&#x27; block elements\nii. the &#x27;f&#x27; block elements (inner transition elements)\nHere, we will discuss the main transition elements or the &quot;d&#x27; block elements only.\n14:1\n14.1.1\nGeneral Features\nواقع\nMeans\nChanging gone\nstate to another\nGeneral Features of Transition Elements\nThe General Features of Transition Elements are,\nThey are all hard metals.\nVstate.\nDestructive energy from\nthe release &amp; atanic\nEnergy\nThey have high melting and boiling points. This is due to high nuclear charge\nand small atomic radius.\nThey form alloys with one another and also with the elements of other blocks. They are good conductors of heat and electricity.\nMost of these elements show variable valencies or oxidation states.\nThe compounds and ions of transition elements are mostly coloured in solid state and in solution respectively. Colour is due to the incomplete &quot;d&quot; orbitals in which electronic transition is possible.\nاحب گریب\n48\nNOT FOR SALE Some of the transition elements form paramagnetic compounds because of the presence of unpaired electrons.\nThey have the ability to form complexes because of the involvement of vacant\nd-orbitals.\n14.1.2\nElectronic Structure\nپے کار\nمشکل معاملہ\nخالی\nلی کام کا دمی اگر\nTidbit Aufbau Principle\nbits 04 | UP\nThe word &#x27;Aufbau&#x27; is derived from the German language and can be translated as construct or build up.\nAufbau principle states that electrons are\nGenerally, the electronic configuration of transition elements is (n-1) d1-&quot;ns&#x27; Scandium (Z=21), the first transition metal in this series, has the configuration [Ar]3d&#x27;,4s2. Addition of electron to the 3d sub-shell continues until, at zinc (Z=30), the 3d sub-shell is filled, with the 4s sub-shell still containing two electrons. However, there are two regularities, one at chromium (Z=24) and the other at copper (Z=29). The electronic configuration of chromium (Z = 24) is [Ar]3d3, 4s and not [Ar]3d,452 as we might expect. A similar break in the pattern is observed for copper, whose electronic configuration is [Ar] 3d10,4s&#x27;, rather than [Ar] 3d,4s2.\n121\nرہ دیکھنا\nLooked Expected forward configuration tas\nمعلوم\nObserved configuration\nCr (Z=24)\n3d\nincreasing order of orbital energy level. According to the Aufbau principle, the available atomic orbitals with the lowest energy state are occupied before those with higher energy state.\nfilled into atomic orbitals in the\nCu (Z = 29)\n11_\n# # # # 1 #\n3d\n1 11 11 11 11\n3ds\n4s1\n3d10\n4s1\nThe reason for these irregularities is that a slightly greater stability is associated with the half-filled (3d) and completely filled (3d10) subshells.\nWhen the first-row transition metals form cations, electrons are removed first from the 4s orbitals and then from the 3d orbitals.\nThe second, third and fourth transition metal series correspond to the filling of the 4d, 5d and 6d sub-shells respectively.\nچوڑ ہونا\n49\nمطابق ہونا\nexchay. SUB\nMessage\n3d-elements\n4d-elements\nElements\nElectronic configuration\nElements\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 14.1 Electronic Configuration of Three Series of d-block Elements\n5d-elements\nElectronic configuration\nElectronic configuration\nElements\nBy",
