        "theory": "anion\n[Co(NH),]Cl, is named hexaamminecobalt(111) chloride\ncation\nanion\n2. In naming the complex ion, the ligand names are written in alphabetical order, before the name of the central metal ion. There should be no space between the name of the ligand and the name of the central metal ion.\n3. Numerical prefixes di, tri, tetra etc., are used for indicating the number of the same kind of ligand. Thus, the ligands in the cation [Co(NH3)4Cl] are &quot;tetraamminedichloro&quot;. If the ligand already contains a Greek prefix (e.g. ethylendiamine) if it is polydenate ligands the prefixes, bis-, tris-, tetrakis-, pentakis- are used instead. Numerical prefixes are not considered for determination of alphabetical order.\n*\n57\n[Fe(CN),] or\n[Co(NH,)] or\nNOT FOR SALE\nhexacyanoferrate(II) ion\n6 CN ligands\nhexaamminecobalt(III) ion\n6 NH, ligands\nThe oxidation number of the central metal ion is indicated in parentheses by Roman numerals following the name of the metal. For example, the Roman numeral III is used to indicate the +3 oxidation state of chromium in [Cr(NH3)«Cl2]&#x27;, which is called tetraamminedichlorochromium(III) ion. No\nis provided between the name of the metal and the parentheses.\nhexaamminecobalt(III) hexacyanoferrate(II)\nspace\nferrum oxidation -iron number 2\nindicates\nan anion\n(metal oxidation name number 3\n5. The names of anionic ligands end with the letter &#x27;O&#x27;, for example, hydroxo (OH), carbonato (CO), whereas the names of neutral ligands are written as such. The exceptions are H2O (aqua), CO (carbonyl), and NH3 (ammine). Table 14.5 lists some common ligands.\nTable 14.5 Names of Common Ligands in Coordination Compounds\nName of Ligand in Coordination Compound\nLigand\nBromide, Br\nBromo\nChloride, CI\nChloro\nCyanide, CN\nCyano\nHydroxide, OH\nHydroxo\nOxide, 0°2\nОхо\nCarbonate, CO2-\nCarbonato\nNitrite, NO,\nNitro\nOxalate, C20,2\nOxalato\nAmmonia, NH,\nAmmine\nCarbon monoxide, CO\nCarbonyl\nWater, H2O\nAqua\nX\nwaxin\n58\nNOT FOR SALE\nAfter naming the ligands, name the central metal. If the complex ion is a cation, the metal is named same as the element. For example, Co in a complex cation is called cobalt and Pt is called platinum. If the complex ion is an anion, the name of the metal ends with the suffix -ate. For example, in K4[Fe(CN)6] the anion [Fe(CN), is called hexacyanoferrate (II) ion. For example, Co in complex anion is called cobaltate and Pt is called palatinate. For some metals, the Latin names are used in the complex anions (e.g. Fe is called ferrate and not ironate). The Roman numeral II indicates the oxidation state of iron. Table 14.6 gives the names of anions containing metal atoms.\nTable 14.6 Names of Anions Containing Metal Atoms\nMetal\nName of Metal in Anionic Complex\nAluminate\nAluminum\nChromium\nCobalt\nCopper\nGold\nCONT\nChromate\nCobaltate\nCuprate\nAurate\nIron\nFerrate\nLead\nPlumbate\nManganese\nManganate\nMolybdenum\nMolybdate\nNickel\nNickelate\nSilver\nArgentate",
