        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 13",
        "theory": "The net reaction is given as,\n3CH, CH, +2MnO; +4H,O\n2CH-CH2+ 2MnO, + 40H-\nOH\nOH\nDark brown precipitate\n3CH, CH, + 2Mn0, + 20H\nT\nOH\nOH\nDark brown precipitate\nii.\nCH,\nIn the Oxidation of Aromatic Side Chains Alkaline potassium manganate (VII) solution oxidizes any hydrocarbon side chain attached to a benzene ring back to a single -COOH group. Prolonged heating is necessary. For example: as shown in the figure.\nIn case of the ethyl side chain, carbon dioxide is also produced but\nCH,CH,\nin each case, the main product is benzoic acid.\nAlkaline MnO,\nHeat\nawa\nCOOH\nBenzoic acid\n14.3.3.3 Potassium Manganate (VII) as an Oxidizing Agent in Titrations KMnO, is a strong oxidizing agent with intense dark purple colour. During reduction, the purple permanganate ion changes into colourless Mn ion.\nPotassium manganate (VII) solution is used to find the concentration of all sorts of reducing agents. It is always used in acidic solution.\nFor example, it oxidizes\n70\nin\nNOT FOR SALE\nIron(II) ions to iron(III) ions\nFe\nacidic\nFee\nHydrogen peroxide solution to oxygen\nH2O,\nSulphite ions (IV) to sulphate ions (VI) ions.\nSo+H2O\n02+ 2H + 2e\nSO+ 2H + 2e\nIn each case, the half-equation for the manganate (VII) ions in acidic solution is:\n14.3.4\nMnO;+8H+ Se\nIron\nMn2+ 4H,O\nIron is the second most abundant metal in the Earth&#x27;s crust. Concentrated deposits of iron ore are found in many localities, thus making it easy to mine. Iron is a transition. metal with a symbol &quot;Fe&quot; and atomic number 26 and its atomic mass is 55.845 a.m.u.\n14.3.4.1 Oxidation States\nMost common oxidation states of iron are +2 and +3 but it can also exists in the form of Fe+, Fe and Fe+. The +6 oxidation state of Fe is very rare. The Fe2+ and Fel oxidation states are stable states.\n◆ Iron in the +2 oxidation state is known as the ferrous ion. This ion is pale green in colour and is very easily oxidized to ferric ion. Even traces of dissolved. oxygen in solution will complete this oxidation. Solution of ferrous ion is sometimes used as reducing agent.\nMost solutions containing ferric ions are usually yellow or yellow-brown due to formation of [Fe(H2O),(OH)]. Fe3) yields a dark blue precipitate called Prussian blue, Fe,[Fe(CN),], is formed, when treated with potassium ferrocyanide, K,[Fe(CN).&quot;\n14.3.4.2 Iron as Catalyst in Haber&#x27;s Process\nIn the Haber process, hydrogen reacts with nitrogen to produce ammonia. Without catalyst, the process would proceed too slowly to be economically viable. The reaction takes place according to the following steps:\nN2+ H2\nN2H2+ H2\n2\nN2H2\nN2H1\n71\nNOT FOR SALE\n2NH,\nN2H, + H2\nHowever, breaking the nitrogen triple bond to form N,H, is very difficult and resulting compound is highly unstable and is likely to dissociate as soon as it is formed. The formation of N,H, is highly endothermic. So the above reactions do not proceed easily\nwithout the use of catalyst.\nThe hydrogen and nitrogen are adsorbed on metallic iron surface.\nNa\nN2000\nN2(d)\nThe hydrogen almost immediately splits into its component atoms by sharing or exchange of electrons with the catalyst surface\nH\n2H (art)\nThe nitrogen molecules split into their component atoms and they lock into the surface of the iron catalyst. This is the slowest step and so limits the rate of the entire process.\nNa\n2ad)\n2N(\nThe hydrogen atoms migrate across the surface of the catalyst and react with nitrogen and produce ammonia molecules that are still bound to the surface.\nN(t) + Hat)\nNH (nd)\n+ H\n+ Had)\nNH 20\nNH\nNH 20",
