      "oxidation Number: The Net sum of the negative, Transition elements are those elements, which have partially filled d or forbitals either",
      "Less The positive, charges on andton. in their atomic or in any common oxidation states. The transition elements occupy the central part of the periodic table, bridging the gap between the s-block metals of Groups-IA and IIA on the left and the p-block metals metalloid and non-metals of Groups-IIIA to VIIIA on the right (Figure 14.1).",
      "Generally, these elements have partly filled (n-1)d subshell in the ground state of their free atom or in their common oxidation states. The elements of groups IB to VIII B (groups 3-12) are known as d-block elements. Each d-subshell consists of five orbitals and can accommodate 10 electrons, so each transition series consists of 10 elements. The first series extends from scandium to zinc (Sc-Zn). These elements have partially filled 3d orbitals. The second series runs from yttrium to cadmium (Y-Cd). These elements have partially filled 4d orbitals and the third series runs from the element lanthanum (La) and the elements hafnium through mercury (HfHg). All these elements have partially filled 5d orbitals. In addition, there is a fourth transition series starts up from the unstable, radioactive elements actinium (Ac), which also includes Rutherfordium (Rf) through the recently discovered element 112, copernicium (Cn). These elements have partially filled 6d orbitals.",
