      "14.2 Coordination Compounds",
      "Coordination compounds are those compounds in which a central atom or ion is attached with a fixed number of groups or molecules (ligands) through coordinate covalent bonds.",
      "in",
      "These compounds can be divided into two classes depending on their behaviour in aqueous solution.",
      "Z",
      "1. Those which Lose their Identity in Solution L.c. Double Salts These compounds exist as stable compounds only in the solid crystalline state and break up into constituent units in aqueous solution. For example, when crystals of potash alum are dissolved in water, Al3+, K+ and SO4 ions are produced. This aqueous solution shows the same physical and chemical properties as a mixture of aqueous solution of aluminium sulphate and potassium sulphate. Some examples of double salts",
      "are,",
