      "3. Ligand",
      "CH 2100",
      "The atoms, ions, or neutral molecules, which surrounds the central metal ion and donate electron pairs to it, are called ligands. They may be anions or neutral molecules. For example, K1[Fe(CN)6], [Ag(NH3)2]CI. In these examples, the CN is anionic and NH is the neutral ligand.",
      "Depending upon the number of donor atoms (or site), the ligands may be classified into various categories as follows.",
      "i.",
      "Monodentate or Unidentate Ligands",
      "The ligands which possess only one donor site are called monodentate or ligands. Some examples are CN, F, CI, OH, CO, NH,, H2O etc.",
      "II.",
      "Multidentate or Polydentate Ligands",
      "unidentate",
      "The ligands, which possess more than one donor sites are called multidentate, or polydentate ligands. Depending on the number of coordinating donor atoms, these are called bidentate (two), tridentate (three), tetradentate (four), pentadentate(five) and hexadentate (six) ligands. For example, ethylene diamine tetra acetic acid EDTA is an important hexadentate ligand.",
      "4. Coordination Number",
      "It refers to the total number of coordinate covalent bonds formed by the central metal ion with the ligands. For example, in [CoCl2(NH3)4]Cl, [Cu(NH3)6]Cl2, K3[Cr(C2O4)3] and K4[Fe(CN)6], six coordinate covalent bonds are formed between the ligands and the central metal ion and the coordination number is six, while in [Cu(NH3)4]SO4, four coordinate covalent bonds are in between the ligand NH3 and the central metal ion and the coordination number is four.",
      "5. Coordination Sphere",
      "The central atom along with the surrounding ligands are enclosed in a square bracket and forms coordination sphere. It may be anionic, cationic or neutral. For example, K4[Fe(CN)6] [Cu(NH3)4]SO4 and [Ni(CO)4]. In these examples, [Fe(CN)6], [Cu(NH3)2+,",
