      "The bonding of a polydentate ligand to a metal ion through more than one site is called chelating ligand. These ligands. produce a ring like structure called chelate. The polydentate ligand is called a chelating agent, the process of chelate formation is called chelation and the compounds formed are known as chelate compounds. Chelation increases the stability of complex. For example, ethylene diamide acts as a bidentate chelating ligand.",
      "14.2.2 Nomenclature of Coordination Compounds",
      "ethylene diamide",
      "The systematic names of complex compounds are based on rules recommended by the Inorganic Nomenclature Committee of International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC). The rules for naming the coordination compounds are as follows: 1. The cation is named before the anion, as in other ionic compounds. For example, in the Ka[Fe(CN)6] and [Co(NH3)4Cl2]Cl compounds, we name the K* and [Co(NH3)4C12] cations first, respectively.",
      "K[Fe(CN),] is named potassium hexacyanoferrate(II)",
      "cation",
      "anion",
      "[Co(NH),]Cl, is named hexaamminecobalt(111) chloride",
      "cation",
      "anion",
      "2. In naming the complex ion, the ligand names are written in alphabetical order, before the name of the central metal ion. There should be no space between the name of the ligand and the name of the central metal ion.",
      "3. Numerical prefixes di, tri, tetra etc., are used for indicating the number of the same kind of ligand. Thus, the ligands in the cation [Co(NH3)4Cl] are \"tetraamminedichloro\". If the ligand already contains a Greek prefix (e.g. ethylendiamine) if it is polydenate ligands the prefixes, bis-, tris-, tetrakis-, pentakis- are used instead. Numerical prefixes are not considered for determination of alphabetical order.",
