      "Figure 14.6 Geometrical shapes of complexes.",
      "Most of the compounds of the transition metals are coloured in solid or solution state. In transition elements, the d orbitals are responsible for the development of colour in the transition compound. When these orbitals form bonds with the ligands, they splits up, e.g in octahedral complexes they divide into two energy levels, one set has higher energy than the other. The electrons residing in low energy d-orbital absorb a part of the visible light and jump to higher energy d-orbital. This process is called d-d transition. When light is allowed to fall on a substance, it absorbs from it the light of a particular colour whose wavelength is in the visible region (4000 7000Å) and reflects or transmits back the remaining light which is",
      "Tidbit",
      "Complementary Colours",
      "Colours directly opposite each other on the colour wheel are said to be complementary colours. Blue and yellow are complementary colours; red and cyan are complementary; and so are green and magenta. Copper (II) sulphate solution is pale blue (cyan) because it absorbs light in the red region of the spectrum. Thus, cyan is the complementary colour of red.",
