        "theory": "0\n0\nHalo\nR-C-X\nAcid halide\n- COX\n-C-X\nCH,-C-Cl\ncarbonyl\nOF\nот\nacetyl chloride\nCH2COX\n0\n-C-NH,\nR-C-NH,\nAcid amide\nAmide\nor\n--CONH\nот\nC.H, CONH,\n0\nCH,...C-NH,\nacetamide\nEster\nO\nEster\n-C-OR (alkoxy\ncarbonyl)\nог\n-COOR\nR-C-O-R\nог\nCHCOOCH\nCH-C-OCH, methyl acetate\nAlkyl\nR-CN\ncyanide or Nitrile\n-C=N\nCyano\nог\nCH.CN\nCH,CN methyl cyanide\nHomologous Series\nav\nA series of organic compounds in which all members possess similar structural features and similar chemical characteristics, but each member is different from the next member by a methylene (-CH,-) group is called homologous series. All the members that differ from each other by methylene group, CH,-, are called homologues. Each class of organic compounds has its own homologous series which can be represented by a general formula. For example, alkanes (saturated hydrocarbons) form a series of compounds. It can be represented by a general formula CH2 where &#x27;n&#x27; shows the number of carbon atoms in the corresponding alkane.\n92\nTable 15.2 Alkane Homologous Series\nNOT FOR SALE\nMolecular\nNo. of C\nformula\nName with\nStructural formula\natoms\n(CH22)\nsuffix (-ane)\n1\nCH\nMethane CH\n2\nC2H\nEthane\nCH,CH,\n3\nC,H,\nPropane\nCH,CH,CH,\n4\nCH10\nButane\n5\nCH12\nPentane\nCH,CH,CH,CH,\nCH,CH,CH,CH,CH,\n6\nCH\nHexane",
        "content": "0<br>0<br>Halo<br>R-C-X<br>Acid halide<br>- COX<br>-C-X<br>CH,-C-Cl<br>carbonyl<br>OF<br>от<br>acetyl chloride<br>CH2COX<br>0<br>-C-NH,<br>R-C-NH,<br>Acid amide<br>Amide<br>or<br>--CONH<br>от<br>C.H, CONH,<br>0<br>CH,...C-NH,<br>acetamide<br>Ester<br>O<br>Ester<br>-C-OR (alkoxy<br>carbonyl)<br>ог<br>-COOR<br>R-C-O-R<br>ог<br>CHCOOCH<br>CH-C-OCH, methyl acetate<br>Alkyl<br>R-CN<br>cyanide or Nitrile<br>-C=N<br>Cyano<br>ог<br>CH.CN<br>CH,CN methyl cyanide<br>Homologous Series<br>av<br>A series of organic compounds in which all members possess similar structural features and similar chemical characteristics, but each member is different from the next member by a methylene (-CH,-) group is called homologous series. All the members that differ from each other by methylene group, CH,-, are called homologues. Each class of organic compounds has its own homologous series which can be represented by a general formula. For example, alkanes (saturated hydrocarbons) form a series of compounds. It can be represented by a general formula CH2 where &#x27;n&#x27; shows the number of carbon atoms in the corresponding alkane.<br>92<br>Table 15.2 Alkane Homologous Series<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Molecular<br>No. of C<br>formula<br>Name with<br>Structural formula<br>atoms<br>(CH22)<br>suffix (-ane)<br>1<br>CH<br>Methane CH<br>2<br>C2H<br>Ethane<br>CH,CH,<br>3<br>C,H,<br>Propane<br>CH,CH,CH,<br>4<br>CH10<br>Butane<br>5<br>CH12<br>Pentane<br>CH,CH,CH,CH,<br>CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,<br>6<br>CH<br>Hexane",
