        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 5",
        "theory": "7\nC,H1\nHeptane\n8\nCH\nOctane\nCH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,\nCH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,\nCH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,\nC,H20\nNonane\n10\nCH2\nDecane\nCH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,\nCH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,CH,\nAs it is clear from alkane homologous series that each member is different from the adjacent member by CH,. Similarly, this series can be expanded beyond C1, to higher alkanes. Similar homologous series can be developed for alkenes, alkynes, alcohols, ethers, amines, carboxylic acids, carbonyl compounds etc.\nCharacteristics of homologous Series\nAs mentioned, each class of organic compounds has its own homologous series having general formula.\nAll the members of homologous series have similar chemical properties and same general methods of preparation similar structural features and same functional group. The physical properties like melting point, boiling points, densities etc, increase down the series due to increase in their molecular masses.\n15.7\nDetection of elements in Organic Compounds\nThe main elements present in organic compounds are carbon and hydrogen as well as oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur and halogens. Following methods can be used to detect these elements, in the organic compound.\n93\nDetection of Carbon\nNOT FOR SALE\nAs carbon is always present in all the organic compounds, there is no need to test for it. The test is performed only to establish whether a given compound is organic or not. The organic substance is mixed with dry cupric oxide in 1:3 ratio and heated in a test tube fitted with a delivery tube. The other end of which is dipping into lime water (solution of Ca(OH),). Carbon converts to carbon dioxide which reacts with lime water to turn it milky due to formation of calcium carbonate.\nCa(OH)2+ CO20\nCaCO↓ + H2O(\ng 8\nOrganic compound + CuO-\nCa (OH)2\nNe in\nFigure 15.1 Production and detection of carbon dioxide gas.\nDetection of Hydrogen\nIf hydrogen is present in the compound, it is oxidized to water vapours which condenses in small droplets on the cooler end of the test tube. The water formation is further\nconfirmed by passing the issuing gases (water vapours) from anhydrous copper sulphate (white) that is turned blue as copper sulphate converts to hydrated form (CuSO, SH,O).\nDetection of Nitrogen, Sulphur and Halogens\nNitrogen, sulphur and halogens can be detected in organic compounds by sodium extract test or Lassaigne&#x27;s test. For this purpose, sodium extract or Lassaigne&#x27;s solution is prepared first. The substance is heated strongly with sodium metal in a fusion tube till the tube becomes red hot which is plunged into the distilled water in a china dish\n94\nNOT FOR SALE\nand filtered after boiling the contents of china dish. The filtrate is called Lassaigne&#x27;s\nsolution or sodium extract.\nDetection of Nitrogen\nTake a part of Lassaigne&#x27;s solution in a test tube and add to it few drops of sodium hydroxide and then few millilitres of freshly prepared ferrous sulphate (FeSO,) solution and boil it. After cooling, add few drops of ferric chloride (FeCl,) solution. and excess of concentrated HCl solution. The formation of Prussian blue or green colouration confirms the presence of nitrogen.\nDetection of Sulphur\nTo another portion of sodium extract add some acetic acid first and then lead acetate solution. Black precipitate of lead sulphide will be formed which confirms the presence of sulphur.\nDetection of Halogens\nBoil a portion of sodium extract with concentrated nitric acid and. then add to it silver nitrate solution. The formation of white precipitate soluble in ammonium hydroxide solution indicates black precipitate chlorine, pale yellow precipitate slightly soluble in NH OH\nformed\nindicates bromine and deep yellow precipitates insoluble in NH,OH solution shows\nthe presence of iodine in the organic compound.\nDetection of Oxygen\nOxygen can be detected by following methods.\nThe substance is heated alone in a dry test tube usually in nitrogen atmosphere. Formation of water droplets on the cooler part of the test tube obviously show the presence of oxygen.\nii. Different tests are applied for oxygen containing functional groups like alcohol (OH), carbonyl compounds like ketone (-CO-), aldehyde (CHO) and carboxylic acid (-COOH). If any one is detected, the presence of oxygen is confirmed.\n95\nNOT FOR SALE\nKey Points\nGenerally, all the compounds were classified on the basis of their origin. The compounds that were having living source (plants and animals) were called organic and those coming from non living sources (minerals) were called inorganic. According to vital force theory, organic compounds can only be obtained from living organisms and could not be synthesized in the laboratory from inorganic\nsources.\nThe study of the chemistry of the carbon containing compounds (organic compounds) is called organic chemistry.\nThe major sources of organic compounds are plants and animals as well as fossil fuels.\nThe abundance of organic compounds is due to unique behaviour of carbon as well as due to some special characteristics of these compounds themselves. Organic chemistry has applications in almost all fields and the importance of organic compounds can hardly be neglected.\nThe discovery of fullerenes (bucky balls) has significantly expanded the number of known allotropes of carbon.\nFunctional group is the active part of organic compounds which greatly affects chemistry of organic compounds.\nthe\nOrganic compounds can be classified on the basis of structure as well as on the basis of functional group.\nEach class of organic compounds have their own homologous series that can represented by a general formula.\nbe\n96.\nNOT FOR SALE\nExercise\nQI. Choose the correct one from the given options.\n1. The first organic compound prepared in the laboratory was\na. peat\nb. urea\n2. All the substances are organic except\nb. urea\na. graphite 3. The type of coal containing highest",
