        "title": "16.5 Alkenes",
        "theory": "4-methylpent-1-ene\nCH2\npenta-1,3-diene\n116\nNOT FOR SALE\n16.5.2\nStructure\nTidbit\nIn 1993, the IUPAC recommended a logical change in the positions of the numbers used in names. Instead of placing the numbers before the root name (1-butene),\nConsider ethene (CH) to illustrate the structure of alkenes. In ethene, each carbon atom has three sp2-hybridized orbitals which are coplanar. One of the sp2-hybrid orbitals of one carbon atom overlaps linearly with that of another carbon atom to form a sigma (8) bond. The other two sp- hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom overlap with Is orbital of hydrogen atoms to form C-H sigma bonds. The unhybridized p-orbitals of the two perpendicular to the plane of hybridized orbitals, overlap in a side-wise (parallel) fashion to form a pi (x) bond between the two carbon atoms. (Figure.16.3).\nTC\nthey recommended placing them immediately before the part of the name they locate (but-1-ene).\ncarbon atoms, which remain\nilab labo\nH\nCsp2sp2\nSp\nHC-\nH\nCH\nH\nH\nFigure 16.3 Orbital overlap discription in formation of sigma and pi bonds in ethene. The overall structure of ethene is planar in which H-C-C and H-C-H bond angles are approximately equal to 120°. The carbon-carbon double bond length is 134. pm and C-H bond lengths are 108 pm.\n16.5.3 Conjugation\nIf an alkene has two double bonds, it is called a diene (or alkadiene), if three double bonds, then triene (or alkatriene) and so on. When two double bonds in an alkene (diene, triene etc) are separated by just one single bond, the two double bonds are said to be conjugated and the alkene is a conjugated alkene. The interaction between the two double bonds in a conjugated dienes (or triene etc.) delocalizes the electron density and increases the stability of the molecule, and termed as conjugation. The simplest diene that can have two conjugated pi bonds is buta-1,3-diene. Similarly hexa-1,3,5-triene is a triene with three conjugated double bonds and so on.\nCH, =CH-CH=CH,\nbuta-1,3-diene\nCH2=CH-CH=CH-CH=CH2\nhexa-1,3,5-triene\n117\nLet us consider buta-1,3-diene to illustrate the phenomenon of conjugation. All the four carbon\nTidbit\nNOT FOR SALE\natoms in buta-1,3-diene are sp2-hybridized. The When the double bonds in a diene\nC-C and C-H sigma bonds are the result of the overlap of sp2-hybrid orbitals with each other\nare separated from each other by two or more single bonds, they are called isolated double bonds. Adjacent\nand with Is orbitals of the hydrogen atoms double bonds in a diene are referred respectively. All the carbon and hydrogen atoms as comulated double bonds. lie in the same plane. Additionally, each of the four carbon atom also possess an unhybridized p orbital, which are perpendicular to the plane of sigma bonds. These four porbitals overlap to form delocalized pi molecular orbitals (Figure 16.4). These delocalized pi electrons are thus attracted by all four carbon atoms. The delocalization of pi electrons is responsible for greater stability of buta-1,3-diene. As a consequence of conjugation, the C-C single bond in buta-1,3-diene is 148 pm (1.48 A) which is shorter than the normal C-C single bonds (154 pm) of alkane while the C-C double bond are 137 pm (1.37 A) which are longer than normal isolated C-C double bonds (134 pm).\nsmall amount of overlap\npartial double bond\nH\nH\n1.37Å\nH\nH\nH\nH\n1.48 A\n1.37A\nH\nH\nCum H\nH\nZe in\nFigure 16.4 Overlap of four p orbitals in buta-1,3-diene.\n16.5.4 | Relative Stability of Alkenes\nW\nVarious alkenes have different stability which depends upon the position of the double bond in the molecule and the number, nature and relative position of substituents on double bonded carbon atoms. In general, the greater the number of alkyl groups attached to the double bonded carbons, the greater is the stability of alkene. For geometric isomers of an alkene, trans-isomer is always more stable than cis-isomers (Section 16.6.2.1). For isomeric dienes, conjugated dienes are more stable than non- conjugated.\nMeasurement and comparison of the heats of hydrogenation or heats of combustion of different isomeric alkenes, give us a clue about the relative stability of these alkenes. Stable alkenes evolve small amount of heat upon combustion and hydrogenation and\nvice versa.\n118\nNOT FOR SALE\nConsider the heats of combustion of the four isomeric butylenes of molecular formula C,H,, as given below. Comparison of the heats of combustion of butylenes shows that but-1-ene evolves the largest amount of heat on combustion. In other words, but-1-ene possess the highest energy and is thus the least stable of the four butylenes. Similarly isobutylene (2-methylpropene) has lowest value of enthalpy of combustion, and the most stable of the four isomers.\nCH,\nCH2-CH=CH, + 60,\nCH,\nH\nbut-1-ene\n=\ncis-but-2-ene\nCH1\nH\n4CO2+ 4H2O + 2719kJ/mol\n+ 602\n4CO2+ 4H2O + 2712kJ/mol\nCH,\nlahom\n+ 602\n4CO, 4H,0 + 2707 kJ/mol\nH\nCH,",
