        "theory": "Br\nCCI,\nCCI\nBr\nHC=CH\nBr\n1,2-dibromoethene\nBr Br\nHC–CH\nBr\nBr\ne in\n1,1,2,2-tetrabromoethane\nav\n1,2-dibromoethene\nChlorine also react in similar way.\n16.7.5.6 Ozonolysis\nAlkynes react with ozone to form ozonides. The ozonides may be decomposed with water to give a 1,2-dicarbonyl compound, which is finally oxidized to an acid by hydrogen peroxide produced in the reaction, resulting in formation of carboxylic acids\nR-CEC-R&#x27;+03\nAlkyne\n0-0\n00\nR-C-C-R\nH2O\nR-C-C-R\n-H2O2\n0 Ozonide\n1,2-dicarbonyl\ncompound\n138\nNOT FOR SALE\nR-C-C-R 1,2-dicarbonyl compound\nH2O,\nR-C-OH +\nR&#x27;-C-OH\nCarboxylic acids.\n16.8\nBenzene and Substituted Benzenes\nBenzene is an aromatic hydrocarbon. Its molecular formula is CH. The following ring structure is usually written for benzene, where each corner represents a carbon atom. It is understood that one hydrogen atom is bonded to each carbon atom.\nH\nH\nH\nOR\nOR\nH\nH\nH\nMonosubstituted and polysubstituted benzene derivatives are formed by the replacement of one or more hydrogen atoms respectively.\n16.8.1\nNomenclature\nBenzene and its substituted compounds are named by common names which are also accepted by the IUPAC System.\n1. Monosubstituted benzene derivatives are named by putting the name of the\nsubstituent before the word benzene. For example;\nBr\nbromobenzene\nCI\nchlorobenzene\nNO,\nnitrobenzene\nSome Monosubstituted benzene derivatives have been given &quot;Special names&quot;. They must be remembered as such. Some of the examples are given below. Their systematic names are also given in parenthesis.\nCH,\nOH\nNH,\ntoluene (methylbenzene)\nphenol (benzenol)\naniline (benzenamine)\n139\n*\nCHO\nCOOH\nNOT FOR SALE\nbenzaldehyde\n(benzenecarbaldehyde)\nbenzoic acid (benzenecarboxylic acid)\nii. In disubstituted benzene derivatives, the relative position of substituents in 1,2-, 1,3- and 1,4-disubsituted can be represented prefixes ortho (o-), meta (m-) and para (p-) respectively. For similar substituents, the prefix di- is also used before the name of the substituents. For example\nBr\nBr\nBr\nBr\no-dibromobenzene",
