        "theory": "(1,2-dibromobenzene)\nBr\nm-dibromobenzene\n(1,3-dibromobenzene)\nBr\np-dibromobenzene (1,4-dibromobenzene)\nIf the substituents are different, they are named in alphabatical order. If one of the substituent is such that it gives special name to the molecule then the special name is used as the parent name. The other substituent, along with its position, is named before the parent name.\nBr\nCI\nNO2\nOH\nNH,\nCI\nn\no-bromochlorobenzene\nBr\nm-bromonitrobenzene\no-chloropnenol (1-bromo-2-chlorobenzene) (1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene) (2-chlorophenol)\nNO2\nm-nitroaniline (3-nitroaniline)\nSome disubstituted benzene derivatives are given special names. For example;\nCH,\nCH1\nCH\nCH1\nCH,\nCH,\no-xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene)\nm-xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene)\np-xylene\n(1,4-dimethylbenzene)\n140\nOH\nOH\nCH,\nNOT FOR SALE\nOH\nCH,\nCH1\no-cresol\n(2-methylphenol)\nm-cresol (3-methylphenol)\np-cresol (4-methylphenol)\niii. Polysubstituted benzene derivatives, are named similarly to disubsituted derivatives. For similar atoms/groups the prefix tri-, tetra- etc. are used before the substituents. If different groups attached, they are named in alphabetical order.\nTo indicate the position of the substituents, the ring is numbered in such a way that all the numbers used in the name give the lowest sum. If a substituent gives special name, it should be considered at position-1, and the special name is the parent name of molecule. For example;\nCL\nBr\nBr\nCI\nCI\nBr\n1,2,3-trichlorobenzene\nNO2\nBr\n1,3,5-tribromobenzene\n1-bromo-2-chloro-3-fluorobenzene\nOH\nCH1\nCI\nO,N\nNO,\nBr\n1-bromo-3-chloro-5-nitrobenzene 2-chloro-3-fluorophenol\nNO2\n2,4,6-trinitrotoluene (TNT)\nSome polysubstituted benzene derivatives have been assigned special names. Few examples are given below. Their systematic names are given in parenthesis.\nH,C\nCH,\nOH\nH,C\nCH\nO,N.\nNO,\n5\nCH,\nH,C\nmesitylene (1,3,5-trimethylbenzene)\nCH,\ndurene (1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene)\n141",
        "content": "(1,2-dibromobenzene)<br>Br<br>m-dibromobenzene<br>(1,3-dibromobenzene)<br>Br<br>p-dibromobenzene (1,4-dibromobenzene)<br>If the substituents are different, they are named in alphabatical order. If one of the substituent is such that it gives special name to the molecule then the special name is used as the parent name. The other substituent, along with its position, is named before the parent name.<br>Br<br>CI<br>NO2<br>OH<br>NH,<br>CI<br>n<br>o-bromochlorobenzene<br>Br<br>m-bromonitrobenzene<br>o-chloropnenol (1-bromo-2-chlorobenzene) (1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene) (2-chlorophenol)<br>NO2<br>m-nitroaniline (3-nitroaniline)<br>Some disubstituted benzene derivatives are given special names. For example;<br>CH,<br>CH1<br>CH<br>CH1<br>CH,<br>CH,<br>o-xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene)<br>m-xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene)<br>p-xylene<br>(1,4-dimethylbenzene)<br>140<br>OH<br>OH<br>CH,<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>OH<br>CH,<br>CH1<br>o-cresol<br>(2-methylphenol)<br>m-cresol (3-methylphenol)<br>p-cresol (4-methylphenol)<br>iii. Polysubstituted benzene derivatives, are named similarly to disubsituted derivatives. For similar atoms/groups the prefix tri-, tetra- etc. are used before the substituents. If different groups attached, they are named in alphabetical order.<br>To indicate the position of the substituents, the ring is numbered in such a way that all the numbers used in the name give the lowest sum. If a substituent gives special name, it should be considered at position-1, and the special name is the parent name of molecule. For example;<br>CL<br>Br<br>Br<br>CI<br>CI<br>Br<br>1,2,3-trichlorobenzene<br>NO2<br>Br<br>1,3,5-tribromobenzene<br>1-bromo-2-chloro-3-fluorobenzene<br>OH<br>CH1<br>CI<br>O,N<br>NO,<br>Br<br>1-bromo-3-chloro-5-nitrobenzene 2-chloro-3-fluorophenol<br>NO2<br>2,4,6-trinitrotoluene (TNT)<br>Some polysubstituted benzene derivatives have been assigned special names. Few examples are given below. Their systematic names are given in parenthesis.<br>H,C<br>CH,<br>OH<br>H,C<br>CH<br>O,N.<br>NO,<br>5<br>CH,<br>H,C<br>mesitylene (1,3,5-trimethylbenzene)<br>CH,<br>durene (1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene)<br>141",
        "rules": [],
        "sciNote": "(1,2-dibromobenzene)\nBr\nm-dibromobenzene\n(1,3-dibromobenzene)\nBr\np-dibromobenzene (1,4-dibromobenzene)\nIf the substituents are different, they are named in alphabatical order. If one of the substituent is such that it gives special name to the molecule then the special name is used as the parent name. The other substituent, along with its position, is named before the parent name.\nBr\nCI\nNO2\nOH\nNH,\nCI\nn\no-bromochlorobenzene\nBr\nm-bromonitrobenzene\no-chloropnenol (1-bromo-2-chlorobenzene) (1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene) (2-chlorophenol)\nNO2\nm-nitroaniline (3-nitroaniline)\nSome disubstituted benzene derivatives are given special names. For example;\nCH,\nCH1\nCH\nCH1\nCH,\nCH,\no-xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene)\nm-xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene)\np-xylene\n(1,4-dimethylbenzene)\n140\nOH\nOH\nCH,\nNOT FOR SALE\nOH\nCH,\nCH1\no-cresol\n(2-methylphenol)\nm-cresol (3-methylphenol)\np-cresol (4-methylphenol)\niii. Polysubstituted benzene derivatives, are named similarly to disubsituted derivatives. For similar atoms/groups the prefix tri-, tetra- etc. are used before the substituents. If different groups attached, they are named in alphabetical order.\nTo indicate the position of the substituents, the ring is numbered in such a way that all the numbers used in the name give the lowest sum. If a substituent gives special name, it should be considered at position-1, and the special name is the parent name of molecule. For example;\nCL\nBr\nBr\nCI\nCI\nBr\n1,2,3-trichlorobenzene\nNO2\nBr\n1,3,5-tribromobenzene\n1-bromo-2-chloro-3-fluo",
