        "id": "16.30",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 29",
        "theory": "OH\nNOT FOR SALE (a) Draw the structural formula of the following organic compounds.\n(a)\ni. 3-ethyl-4-methyloctane. ii. 4-isopropyldecane\niii. sec-butylcycloheptane\niv. 2,3-dimethyl-4-propylnonane\niv. 2,4,4-trimethylhex-2-ene v. cis-hex-3-ene\nvi. phenanthrene\nvii. hex-2-yne\nProject\nviii. 1,2-dimethyl-4-propylbenzene ix. cyclopropylbenzene\nSet up one small test tube in a test tube rack in the hood. Add 10 mg of a solid or 10 mL of a liquid organic compound containing carbon-carbon double bond. Add 1-3mL of distilled water (if compound is water soluble) or carbon tetrachloride (if compound is water insoluble), shake the mixtures for 2 minutes. Add bromine water reagent (5mL of bromine dissolved in 100 mL of water) for water soluble compound, or bromine in CCL, (5mL of bromine dissolved in 100mL of CCl4) for water insoluble compound, drop by drop. Do not add an excess of the bromine reagent. The bromine reagent is brown or yellow-brown in color. Observe the dissipation of the brownish color of bromine.\n161\nNOT FOR SALE\nCC\nUnit\n17",
        "content": "OH<br>NOT FOR SALE (a) Draw the structural formula of the following organic compounds.<br>(a)<br>i. 3-ethyl-4-methyloctane. ii. 4-isopropyldecane<br>iii. sec-butylcycloheptane<br>iv. 2,3-dimethyl-4-propylnonane<br>iv. 2,4,4-trimethylhex-2-ene v. cis-hex-3-ene<br>vi. phenanthrene<br>vii. hex-2-yne<br>Project<br>viii. 1,2-dimethyl-4-propylbenzene ix. cyclopropylbenzene<br>Set up one small test tube in a test tube rack in the hood. Add 10 mg of a solid or 10 mL of a liquid organic compound containing carbon-carbon double bond. Add 1-3mL of distilled water (if compound is water soluble) or carbon tetrachloride (if compound is water insoluble), shake the mixtures for 2 minutes. Add bromine water reagent (5mL of bromine dissolved in 100 mL of water) for water soluble compound, or bromine in CCL, (5mL of bromine dissolved in 100mL of CCl4) for water insoluble compound, drop by drop. Do not add an excess of the bromine reagent. The bromine reagent is brown or yellow-brown in color. Observe the dissipation of the brownish color of bromine.<br>161<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>CC<br>Unit<br>17",
        "rules": [],
        "sciNote": "OH\nNOT FOR SALE (a) Draw the structural formula of the following organic compounds.\n(a)\ni. 3-ethyl-4-methyloctane. ii. 4-isopropyldecane\niii. sec-butylcycloheptane\niv. 2,3-dimethyl-4-propylnonane\niv. 2,4,4-trimethylhex-2-ene v. cis-hex-3-ene\nvi. phenanthrene\nvii. hex-2-yne\nProject\nviii. 1,2-dimethyl-4-propylbenzene ix. cyclopropylbenzene\nSet up one small test tube in a test tube rack in the hood. Add 10 mg of a solid or 10 mL of a liquid organic compound containing carbon-carbon double bond. Add 1-3mL of distilled water (if compound is water soluble) or carbon tetrachloride (if compound is water insoluble), shake the mixtures for 2 minutes. Add bromine water reagent (5mL of bromine dissolved in 100 mL of water) for water soluble compound, or bromine in CCL, (5mL of bromine dissolved in 100mL of CCl4) for water insoluble compound, drop by drop. Do not add an excess of the bromine reagent. The bromine reagent is brown or yellow-brown in color. Observe the dissipation of the brownish color of bromine.\n161\nNOT FOR SALE\nCC\nUnit\n17",
