        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 1",
        "theory": "CH-CH2\nCH-CH2\ncyclobutane\nCH\n€€\nCH2\nCH\nCH,\nCH\nCH2\ncyclohexene\npropene\ncyclobutene\n101\nNOT FOR SALE\nCH2\nAlkynes and Cycloalkynes\nCH2\nC\nHC=CH\nH,C-C CH\npropyne\nCH2\nethyne\nCH2\ncyclohexyne\n2.\nAromatic Hydrocarbons\nThe term aromatic was derived from the Greek word aroma (fragrant), because of their pleasant smell. Aromatic hydrocarbons, were typically obtained by treatment of pleasant-smelling plant extracts. Another name for aromatic hydrocarbons is arenes. Benzene (C,H,) is the parent member of this class and hence aromatic is the term used for benzene and all those compounds that resemble benzene in their chemical properties. Over time, chemists discovered that many derivatives of benzene are, in fact, odorless. Nevertheless, the term aromatic is still used to describe all derivatives of benzene, regardless of whether they are fragrant or odourless. Examples of some aromatic hydrocarbons are given below.\nCH1\nbenzene\ntoluene\nnaphthalene\n16.2 Alkanes and Cycloalkanes\nvaz e in\nAlkanes are open chain saturated hydrocarbons, which can be represented by the\nTidbit\nAn aromatic organic compound is cyclic, planar, fully conjugated compound containing (4n+2) pi electrons; where n should be 0, 1, 2, 3 ...\ngeneral formula CH2 (where &quot;n&quot; is the number of carbon atoms), while cycloalkanes are cyclic saturated hydrocarbons represented by the general formula CH (n&gt;2). It is to be noted from the general formula of cycloalkanes, that there are two hydrogen atoms less than the corresponding alkanes. In cycloalkanes, two carbon atoms of the molecule utilize one of their valency in forming bonds with other carbon atoms for formation of cyclic ring.\n102\n16.2.1\nNomenclature\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe naming of organic compounds is called Organic Nomenclature. Nomenclature in organic chemistry is of two types: common (or trivial) and systematic (or IUPAC system). At the beginning of organic chemistry, each new compound was given an individual name, which was based on its source, some property or other trivial reason. For example, the compound HCOOH was given the name formic acid. The name formic acid was derived from Latin word formica meaning ant, because it was obtained. from red ants. Such names, which are based on the source of the compounds are called common names or trivial names.\nWith the increase in the number of organic compounds it became impossible to give names to such a large number of organic compounds. Moreover, trivial names do not give any information about the structure of the compounds. Need was, therefore, felt to name organic compounds in a systematic way. Thus, the &quot;International Union of pure and Applied chemistry&quot; (IUPAC) in 1957, set rules for giving systematic names to organic compounds on the basis of structure. This is known as the IUPAC system of nomenclature.\n16.2.1.1 Nomenclature of Alkanes\nAlkanes are named by both common and IUPAC systems.\n(a)\nCommon System\nThe first four members of the series are named as methane (CH,), ethane (CH), propane (CH) and butane (C,H,,) while the rest are named by using the Greek prefixes that indicate number of carbon atoms in the molecule. Thus the prefix pent (5) is used for the compound having five carbon atoms, hex (6) for six, hept (7) for seven, oct (8) for eight, non (9) for nine and dec (10) for ten carbon atoms. (see table 16.1). The suffix &quot;-ane&quot; should be added with above prefixes for complete name. Table 16.1 Name and Formulae of First 10 Alkanes\nNo. of\nNo. of\nMolecular\nMolecular\nCarbon\nName\nCarbon\nName\nFormula\nFormula\nAtoms\nAtoms\n1\nmethane\nCHA\n6\nhexane\nCH14\n2\nethane\nCH\n7\nheptane\nCH1\n3\npropane\nC,H,\n8\noctane\nC,H1",
        "content": "CH-CH2<br>CH-CH2<br>cyclobutane<br>CH<br>€€<br>CH2<br>CH<br>CH,<br>CH<br>CH2<br>cyclohexene<br>propene<br>cyclobutene<br>101<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>CH2<br>Alkynes and Cycloalkynes<br>CH2<br>C<br>HC=CH<br>H,C-C CH<br>propyne<br>CH2<br>ethyne<br>CH2<br>cyclohexyne<br>2.<br>Aromatic Hydrocarbons<br>The term aromatic was derived from the Greek word aroma (fragrant), because of their pleasant smell. Aromatic hydrocarbons, were typically obtained by treatment of pleasant-smelling plant extracts. Another name for aromatic hydrocarbons is arenes. Benzene (C,H,) is the parent member of this class and hence aromatic is the term used for benzene and all those compounds that resemble benzene in their chemical properties. Over time, chemists discovered that many derivatives of benzene are, in fact, odorless. Nevertheless, the term aromatic is still used to describe all derivatives of benzene, regardless of whether they are fragrant or odourless. Examples of some aromatic hydrocarbons are given below.<br>CH1<br>benzene<br>toluene<br>naphthalene<br>16.2 Alkanes and Cycloalkanes<br>vaz e in<br>Alkanes are open chain saturated hydrocarbons, which can be represented by the<br>Tidbit<br>An aromatic organic compound is cyclic, planar, fully conjugated compound containing (4n+2) pi electrons; where n should be 0, 1, 2, 3 ...<br>general formula CH2 (where &quot;n&quot; is the number of carbon atoms), while cycloalkanes are cyclic saturated hydrocarbons represented by the general formula CH (n&gt;2). It is to be noted from the general formula of cycloalkanes, that there are two hydrogen atoms less than the corresponding alkanes. In cycloalkanes, two carbon atoms of the molecule utilize one of their valency in forming bonds with other carbon atoms for formation of cyclic ring.<br>102<br>16.2.1<br>Nomenclature<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>The naming of organic compounds is called Organic Nomenclature. Nomenclature in organic chemistry is of two types: common (or trivial) and systematic (or IUPAC system). At the beginning of organic chemistry, each new compound was given an individual name, which was based on its source, some property or other trivial reason. For example, the compound HCOOH was given the name formic acid. The name formic acid was derived from Latin word formica meaning ant, because it was obtained. from red ants. Such names, which are based on the source of the compounds are called common names or trivial names.<br>With the increase in the number of organic compounds it became impossible to give names to such a large number of organic compounds. Moreover, trivial names do not give any information about the structure of the compounds. Need was, therefore, felt to name organic compounds in a systematic way. Thus, the &quot;International Union of pure and Applied chemistry&quot; (IUPAC) in 1957, set rules for giving systematic names to organic compounds on the basis of structure. This is known as the IUPAC system of nomenclature.<br>16.2.1.1 Nomenclature of Alkanes<br>Alkanes are named by both common and IUPAC systems.<br>(a)<br>Common System<br>The first four members of the series are named as methane (CH,), ethane (CH), propane (CH) and butane (C,H,,) while the rest are named by using the Greek prefixes that indicate number of carbon atoms in the molecule. Thus the prefix pent (5) is used for the compound having five carbon atoms, hex (6) for six, hept (7) for seven, oct (8) for eight, non (9) for nine and dec (10) for ten carbon atoms. (see table 16.1). The suffix &quot;-ane&quot; should be added with above prefixes for complete name. Table 16.1 Name and Formulae of First 10 Alkanes<br>No. of<br>No. of<br>Molecular<br>Molecular<br>Carbon<br>Name<br>Carbon<br>Name<br>Formula<br>Formula<br>Atoms<br>Atoms<br>1<br>methane<br>CHA<br>6<br>hexane<br>CH14<br>2<br>ethane<br>CH<br>7<br>heptane<br>CH1<br>3<br>propane<br>C,H,<br>8<br>octane<br>C,H1",
