        "theory": "1. Find the longest continuous chain of carbon atoms and use this chain as the base\nname or parent or stem name.\nCH1\nCH1\nHC-CH-CH-CH=CH | HC-CH-CH-CH -CH\n(correct)\n(correct)\nCH,\nH,C-CH-CH2-CH2—CH,\n(incorrect)\n2. Number the longest chain, beginning with the end nearest a branch.\nCH1\nH,C-CH-CH-CH-CH,\n(correct)\nCH1\nH1C-CH-CH2-CH2—CH,\n(incorrect)\n¥\n105\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\n3. Name the substituents on the longest chain (as alkyl groups), before the stem name. Give the location of each substituent by the number of the main chain carbon atom\nto which it is attached.\nCH1\nH,C-CH-CH-CH-CH,\n2-methylpentane\n4. When two or more different substituents are\npresent, list them in alphabetical order.\nCH, C2H,\nCH\nTidbit\nExcept carboxylic acids and its some derivatives, the names of all other organic compounds are written as single word without any space.\nH,C-CH-CH-CH-CH,\n3-ethyl-2-methylpentane\n5. When two or more of the same alkyl substituent are present, use the prefixes di-, tri-,\ntetra-, and so on (ignored in alphabetizing).\nCH, CH,\nH,C-CH-CH-CH2-CH,\n2,3-dimethylpentane\n6. When there are more than one longest chains, select the chain which carries more\nsubstituents.\nH,C-CH-CH,\nH,C-CH-CH-CH-CH,\n3-isopropylpentane (incorrect)\nOther examples\nCH,\nCH,\n3\nH,C-CH-CH-CH-CH,\n2,4-dimethylpentane\n16.2.1.2 Nomenclature of Cycloalkanes\nH,C-CH-CH,\nH,C—CH,—CH–CH2\ne in\nCH-CH-CH,\n3-ethyl-2-methylpentane (correct)\nCH, CH, CH,\nH,C-CH-CH-CH-CH,\n3\n2\n2,3,4-trimethylpentane\nCycloalkanes are alkanes that contain a ring of three or more carbons. Cycloalkanes are named by placing the prefix “cyclo-&quot; to the name of alkane having the same number\n106\nNOT FOR SALE of carbon atoms as in the ring. For convenience, cycloalkanes are represented by geometric figures e.g. cyclopropane is represented by a triangle, cyclobutane by a square, cyclopentane by a pentagon, cyclohexane by a hexagon and so on. Each corner of these geometric figures represent a -CH2-group.\nCH2\nCH-CH2 ог\ncyclopropane\nCH2-CH2\nCH2-CH2 от\ncyclobutane\nCH\n0.0 0.0\nCH\nCH, CH2 ог\ncyclopentane\nCH\ncyclohexane\nMonosubstituted cycloalkanes are named as alkylcycloalkanes. For polysubstituted cycloalkanes, the prefixes di-, tri- etc. should be used to represent the number of identical substituents. Their positions are specified by numbering the carbon atoms of the ring in the direction that gives the lowest number to the substituents at the first point of di of difference.\nCH,\nC2H,",
