        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 9",
        "theory": "trans-but-2-ene\nH\nCH,\n+ 602\n4CO, 4H2O + 2703 kJ/mol\nH\nCH,\nIsobutylene\n(2-methylpropene)\n16.5.5 Preparation of Alkenes\nAlkenes can be prepared by the following methods.\n16.5.5.1 Dehydration of Alcohols\nWhen an alcohol is heated at 170°C, in the presence of sulfuric acid, a water molecule is eliminated, resulting in the formation of an alkene.\nH\nOH\nH\nH\nH-C-C-H\nH2SO\n+ H2O\n170°C\nH H ethyl alcohol\nH\nH\nethene\n119\nNOT FOR SALE\nIndustrially dehydration of alcohol is carried out by passing vapours of alcohol over\nalumina (Al,O,) at 400°C.\n16.5.5.2 Dehydrohalogenation of Alkyl Halides\nWhen an alkyl halide is heated with alcoholic solution of KOH or NaOH, hydrogen and\nhalogen are eliminated which results in the formation of an alkene.\nH Br\nH-C-C-H + KOH\nH H\nethyl bromide\nX\nH\nH\nalcohol\nC.\n+ H2O + KBr\n60°C\nH\nH\nethene\n16.5.6 Reactivity of Alkenes\nAlkenes are more reactive than alkanes due the presence of pi (n) bond in the alkene molecules. The overlap of atomic orbitals forming the pi bond is not as effective as that in sigma (5) bonds. Thus, pi bond is weaker than a sigma bond and more easily broken. The electron density of pi bond lies above and below the bond axis. It is therefore, more exposed and easily accessible to an external electrophilic (electron seeking) reagent, commonly known as electrophile. It is, therefore, favorable for alkenes to add an electrophile across the double bond by breaking the weak pi bond to form strong sigma bonds and to produce a saturated compound. Such reactions in which unsaturated compounds are converted into saturated compounds, are called addition reactions. When addition reaction of alkenes is initiated by electrophile, this addition is specifically called electrophilic addition.\n16.5.7\nReactions of Alkenes\nvaz\n16.5.7.1 Hydrogenation (Addition of Hydrogen)\nAlkenes readily add hydrogen to form alkanes in the presence of finely divided catalyst such as nickel, platinum or palladium at a temperature of 200-250°C and under moderate pressure (1-5 atm). This is called catalytic hydrogenation and reaction occurs on the surface of catalyst. The reaction is exothermic and the heat released during hydrogenation of an alkene is called heat of hydrogenation.\nH.C=CH2+ H2\nethene\nCH,-CH=CH2+ H2\npropene\nNi/200-250°C\nor Pt or Pd/25°C\nNi/200-250°C\nPt or Pd/25°C\nCH, CH,\nethane\nCH,- CH2-CH1\npropane\n120\nNOT FOR SALE\nPreparation of vegetable ghee from vegetable oil is an example of catalytic hydrogenation at industrial scale. The catalyst used in this process is Rancy Nickel after the name of M. Raney, who developed this catalyst.\nVegetable oil + H,\n150-250°C Raney Ni\nVegetable ghee\n16.5.7.2 Hydrohalogenation\nAlkenes react with hydrogen halide (halogen acids: HCl, HBr or HI) to form an addition product, the alkyl halides (or haloalkanes). The order of reactivity of halogen acids increases down the group.\nCH2=CH, +H-Br\nethene\nCH, -CH, -Br\nethyl bromide\nWhen the alkene is symmetric (as in the case of ethene), only single product is formed. However if the alkene is asymmetric, two different isomeric products are possible. For example, propene can react with HBr in the following two ways.\nilab.com\nCH, CH-CH2",
        "content": "trans-but-2-ene<br>H<br>CH,<br>+ 602<br>4CO, 4H2O + 2703 kJ/mol<br>H<br>CH,<br>Isobutylene<br>(2-methylpropene)<br>16.5.5 Preparation of Alkenes<br>Alkenes can be prepared by the following methods.<br>16.5.5.1 Dehydration of Alcohols<br>When an alcohol is heated at 170°C, in the presence of sulfuric acid, a water molecule is eliminated, resulting in the formation of an alkene.<br>H<br>OH<br>H<br>H<br>H-C-C-H<br>H2SO<br>+ H2O<br>170°C<br>H H ethyl alcohol<br>H<br>H<br>ethene<br>119<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Industrially dehydration of alcohol is carried out by passing vapours of alcohol over<br>alumina (Al,O,) at 400°C.<br>16.5.5.2 Dehydrohalogenation of Alkyl Halides<br>When an alkyl halide is heated with alcoholic solution of KOH or NaOH, hydrogen and<br>halogen are eliminated which results in the formation of an alkene.<br>H Br<br>H-C-C-H + KOH<br>H H<br>ethyl bromide<br>X<br>H<br>H<br>alcohol<br>C.<br>+ H2O + KBr<br>60°C<br>H<br>H<br>ethene<br>16.5.6 Reactivity of Alkenes<br>Alkenes are more reactive than alkanes due the presence of pi (n) bond in the alkene molecules. The overlap of atomic orbitals forming the pi bond is not as effective as that in sigma (5) bonds. Thus, pi bond is weaker than a sigma bond and more easily broken. The electron density of pi bond lies above and below the bond axis. It is therefore, more exposed and easily accessible to an external electrophilic (electron seeking) reagent, commonly known as electrophile. It is, therefore, favorable for alkenes to add an electrophile across the double bond by breaking the weak pi bond to form strong sigma bonds and to produce a saturated compound. Such reactions in which unsaturated compounds are converted into saturated compounds, are called addition reactions. When addition reaction of alkenes is initiated by electrophile, this addition is specifically called electrophilic addition.<br>16.5.7<br>Reactions of Alkenes<br>vaz<br>16.5.7.1 Hydrogenation (Addition of Hydrogen)<br>Alkenes readily add hydrogen to form alkanes in the presence of finely divided catalyst such as nickel, platinum or palladium at a temperature of 200-250°C and under moderate pressure (1-5 atm). This is called catalytic hydrogenation and reaction occurs on the surface of catalyst. The reaction is exothermic and the heat released during hydrogenation of an alkene is called heat of hydrogenation.<br>H.C=CH2+ H2<br>ethene<br>CH,-CH=CH2+ H2<br>propene<br>Ni/200-250°C<br>or Pt or Pd/25°C<br>Ni/200-250°C<br>Pt or Pd/25°C<br>CH, CH,<br>ethane<br>CH,- CH2-CH1<br>propane<br>120<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Preparation of vegetable ghee from vegetable oil is an example of catalytic hydrogenation at industrial scale. The catalyst used in this process is Rancy Nickel after the name of M. Raney, who developed this catalyst.<br>Vegetable oil + H,<br>150-250°C Raney Ni<br>Vegetable ghee<br>16.5.7.2 Hydrohalogenation<br>Alkenes react with hydrogen halide (halogen acids: HCl, HBr or HI) to form an addition product, the alkyl halides (or haloalkanes). The order of reactivity of halogen acids increases down the group.<br>CH2=CH, +H-Br<br>ethene<br>CH, -CH, -Br<br>ethyl bromide<br>When the alkene is symmetric (as in the case of ethene), only single product is formed. However if the alkene is asymmetric, two different isomeric products are possible. For example, propene can react with HBr in the following two ways.<br>ilab.com<br>CH, CH-CH2",
