        "sciNote": "trans-but-2-ene\nH\nCH,\n+ 602\n4CO, 4H2O + 2703 kJ/mol\nH\nCH,\nIsobutylene\n(2-methylpropene)\n16.5.5 Preparation of Alkenes\nAlkenes can be prepared by the following methods.\n16.5.5.1 Dehydration of Alcohols\nWhen an alcohol is heated at 170°C, in the presence of sulfuric acid, a water molecule is eliminated, resulting in the formation of an alkene.\nH\nOH\nH\nH\nH-C-C-H\nH2SO\n+ H2O\n170°C\nH H ethyl alcohol\nH\nH\nethene\n119\nNOT FOR SALE\nIndustrially dehydration of alcohol is carried out by passing vapours of alcohol over\nalumina (Al,O,) at 400°C.\n16.5.5.2 Dehydrohalogenation of Alkyl Halides\nWhen an alkyl halide is heated with alcoholic solution of KOH or NaOH, hydrogen and\nhalogen are eliminated which results in the formation of an alkene.\nH Br\nH-C-C-H + KOH\nH H\nethyl bromide\nX\nH\nH\nalcohol\nC.\n+ H2O + KBr\n60°C\nH\nH\nethene\n16.5.6 Reactivity of Alkenes\nAlkenes are more reactive than alkanes due the presence of pi (n) bond in the alkene molecules. The overlap of atomic orbitals forming the pi bond is not as effective as that in sigma (5) bonds. Thus, pi bond is weaker than a sigma bond and more easily broken. The electron density of pi bond lies above and below the bond axis. It is therefore, more exposed and easily accessible to an external electrophilic (electron seeking) reagent, commonly known as electrophile. It is, therefore, favorable for alkenes to add an electrophile across the double bond by breaking the weak pi bond to form strong sigma bonds and to produce a saturated compound. Such r"
