        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 11",
        "theory": "acetic acid\nOn industrial scale, ethylene oxide is prepared, by air oxidation of ethene with silver oxide catalyst at 300°C.\nCH2=CH2 + 102&quot;\nAg2O\n300°C\nCH, CH,\nethene\nethylene oxide\n16.5.7.7 Ozonolysis\n(oxirane)\nWhen ozone (O3) gas is passed through a solution of alkene in an inert solvent, at low emperature (-80°C), an addition product molozonide is formed. This molozonide earranges rapidly to form ozonide.\nCH2=CH2+0,\nethene\nrearrangment\nH,C\nCH2\nH2C-CH2\n0-0\nethylene molozonide\nethylene ozonide\n123\nNOT FOR SALE\nOzonides are highly unstable and explosive and cannot be isolated. Ozonides are decomposed using reducing agents like zinc dust, to form carbonyl compounds. For example, cleavage of ethylene ozonide with zinc, yield two molecules of formaldehyde.\nH.C\nH2O\nCH, + Zn\n+2\n100°C\nH\nCH\n+ ZnO\nformaldehyde\n0 0\nethylene ozonide\nThis conversion process of an alkene using ozone to carbonyl compounds is called ozonolysis. This reaction can be used for locating the position of the double bond in an\nunknown alkene.\n16.5.7.8 Polymerization\nPolymerization is a process in which small molecules (monomers) chemically join together to form large molecules (polymers). Simple alkenes when subjected to high temperature and pressure in the presence of a catalyst, form long chain addition polymers. Polymerization of alkenes is essentially the conversion of pi bonds to sigma bonds, which is a thermodynamically favorable process. For example ethene polymerizes to form polyethene in different ways. One approach involves relatively high pressure (2000 atm) and high temperature (200 °C) and the use of a free radical initiator catalyst such as peroxides.\nnH.C=CH,\nRO, 200°C\n2000 atm\n+CH-CH,\n+\nethene\npolyethene (polyethylene)\nin\nPolyethylene is used as plastic material in the industries. It is used in the manufacture of shopping bags, sport nets, dustbins, water pipes, packing materials and cable\ninsulation.\n16.6\nIsomerism\nMany of the organic compounds have the same molecular formula but they are different from each other in their structure and properties. Such compounds are called isomers and this phenomenon is called isomerism.\nTidbit\nThe prefix iso- is derived from Greek word isos, &quot;the same&quot;. The suffix from the Greek word meros, meaning -mer in the word &quot;isomer&quot; is derived &quot;part&quot;. Thus isomers are different molecules that have the same parts (elemental composition).\nThere are two main types of isomerism i.e. Constitutional or Structural isomerism and Stereoisomerism. Each of these two main types of isomerism is further classified into\ndifferent types as shown in Figure 16.5.\n124\nIsomerism\nConstitutional isomerism\nNOT FOR SALE\nChain isomerism\nPosition isomerism\nFunctional group isomerism\nMetamerism\nTautomerism\nConformational\nisomerism\nStereoisomerism\nGeometrical\nConfigurational isomerism\nisomerism\nOptical\nisomerism\nFigure 16.5 Different types of isomerism.\n16.6.1 Constitutional isomerism\nIn this type of isomerism, the isomers have the same molecular formula but different. connectivity in structure. It is also called structural isomerism. Structural isomerism is\nof five types.\n16.6.1.1 Chain Isomerism or Skeletal Isomerism\nChain isomerism occurs when there is a difference in arrangement of the carbon atoms in main chain of a molecule. If two or more compounds having the same molecular formula with different carbon chains, then they are said to exhibit the property of Chain isomerism. This phenomenon is also called as skeletal isomerism. The number of possible chain isomers increases with number of carbon atoms. For example; butane has two isomers, pentane has three, hexane has five isomers and so on. The two chain isomers of butane (CH) are given below.\nCH-CH-CH-CH,\nn-butane",
        "content": "acetic acid<br>On industrial scale, ethylene oxide is prepared, by air oxidation of ethene with silver oxide catalyst at 300°C.<br>CH2=CH2 + 102&quot;<br>Ag2O<br>300°C<br>CH, CH,<br>ethene<br>ethylene oxide<br>16.5.7.7 Ozonolysis<br>(oxirane)<br>When ozone (O3) gas is passed through a solution of alkene in an inert solvent, at low emperature (-80°C), an addition product molozonide is formed. This molozonide earranges rapidly to form ozonide.<br>CH2=CH2+0,<br>ethene<br>rearrangment<br>H,C<br>CH2<br>H2C-CH2<br>0-0<br>ethylene molozonide<br>ethylene ozonide<br>123<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Ozonides are highly unstable and explosive and cannot be isolated. Ozonides are decomposed using reducing agents like zinc dust, to form carbonyl compounds. For example, cleavage of ethylene ozonide with zinc, yield two molecules of formaldehyde.<br>H.C<br>H2O<br>CH, + Zn<br>+2<br>100°C<br>H<br>CH<br>+ ZnO<br>formaldehyde<br>0 0<br>ethylene ozonide<br>This conversion process of an alkene using ozone to carbonyl compounds is called ozonolysis. This reaction can be used for locating the position of the double bond in an<br>unknown alkene.<br>16.5.7.8 Polymerization<br>Polymerization is a process in which small molecules (monomers) chemically join together to form large molecules (polymers). Simple alkenes when subjected to high temperature and pressure in the presence of a catalyst, form long chain addition polymers. Polymerization of alkenes is essentially the conversion of pi bonds to sigma bonds, which is a thermodynamically favorable process. For example ethene polymerizes to form polyethene in different ways. One approach involves relatively high pressure (2000 atm) and high temperature (200 °C) and the use of a free radical initiator catalyst such as peroxides.<br>nH.C=CH,<br>RO, 200°C<br>2000 atm<br>+CH-CH,<br>+<br>ethene<br>polyethene (polyethylene)<br>in<br>Polyethylene is used as plastic material in the industries. It is used in the manufacture of shopping bags, sport nets, dustbins, water pipes, packing materials and cable<br>insulation.<br>16.6<br>Isomerism<br>Many of the organic compounds have the same molecular formula but they are different from each other in their structure and properties. Such compounds are called isomers and this phenomenon is called isomerism.<br>Tidbit<br>The prefix iso- is derived from Greek word isos, &quot;the same&quot;. The suffix from the Greek word meros, meaning -mer in the word &quot;isomer&quot; is derived &quot;part&quot;. Thus isomers are different molecules that have the same parts (elemental composition).<br>There are two main types of isomerism i.e. Constitutional or Structural isomerism and Stereoisomerism. Each of these two main types of isomerism is further classified into<br>different types as shown in Figure 16.5.<br>124<br>Isomerism<br>Constitutional isomerism<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Chain isomerism<br>Position isomerism<br>Functional group isomerism<br>Metamerism<br>Tautomerism<br>Conformational<br>isomerism<br>Stereoisomerism<br>Geometrical<br>Configurational isomerism<br>isomerism<br>Optical<br>isomerism<br>Figure 16.5 Different types of isomerism.<br>16.6.1 Constitutional isomerism<br>In this type of isomerism, the isomers have the same molecular formula but different. connectivity in structure. It is also called structural isomerism. Structural isomerism is<br>of five types.<br>16.6.1.1 Chain Isomerism or Skeletal Isomerism<br>Chain isomerism occurs when there is a difference in arrangement of the carbon atoms in main chain of a molecule. If two or more compounds having the same molecular formula with different carbon chains, then they are said to exhibit the property of Chain isomerism. This phenomenon is also called as skeletal isomerism. The number of possible chain isomers increases with number of carbon atoms. For example; butane has two isomers, pentane has three, hexane has five isomers and so on. The two chain isomers of butane (CH) are given below.<br>CH-CH-CH-CH,<br>n-butane",
