        "sciNote": "16.6.1.2 Position Isomerism\nCH1-CH-CH,\nCH, isobutane\nPosition isomerism arises when there is a difference in the position of functional group. When the position of the functional groups with respect to main chain changes, the phenomenon is called as position isomerism. For example; position isomers of propyl alcohols and butylenes are given below.\n125\nCH3-CH2-CH2-OH\npropan-1-ol (n-propyl alcohol)\nCH3-CH2-CH=CH2\nbut-1-ene\n16.6.1.3 Functional group Isomerism\nNOT FOR SALE\nCH3-CH-CH3\nOH\npropan-2-ol\n(isopropyl alcohol)\nCH,-CH=CH -CH3\nbut-2-ene\nFunctional group isomerism involves in compounds having the same formula but different functional groups. For example, ethyl alcohol and dimethyl ether are functional group isomers.\nCH-CH2-OH\nethyl alcohol\nCH-O-CH,\ndimethyl ether\nSimilarly, Propionaldehyde and acetone are also functional group isomers.\nCH, –CH, CHO propionaldehyde\n16.6.1.4 Metamerism\nCH,-CO-CH,\nacetone\nThis type of isomerism is exhibited due to the unequal distribution of carbon atoms (or alkyl groups) on either side of the functional group. Isomers belong to the same homologous series. For example, diethyl ether and methyl n-propyl ether are metamers.\nCH,-CH,-O-CH=CH,\ndiethyl ether\n16.6.1.5 Tautomerism\nCH,-O-CH, -CH, -CH, methyl n-propyl ether\nחו\nTautomers are constitutional isomers that differ from each other by position of a proton (and may also a pi bond). It is a special type of functional group isomerism, in which process the isomers are in dynamic equilibrium with eac"
