        "id": "16.18",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 17",
        "theory": "Tidbit\nSyn addition is the addition of two substituents to the same\naddition gives a cis-alkene product (if cis/trans-isomerism is possible in product alkene). side of a double or triple bond.\n16.7.8.2 Reduction by Dissolving Metal\nTerminal alkynes and acetylene, react with metals such as sodium in liquid ammonia to form salts called alkynides or acetylides (Section 16.7.7). However, reduction of non-terminal alkynes to trans-alkene (anti addition), can be carried by reduction of alkynes with sodium metal in liquid ammonia.\nR\nH\nliq. NH,\nR-CEC-R + 2Na\n+2NaNH,\n-33°C\nH\nR\nalkyne\ntrans-alkene\nNon-terminal\n16.7.8.3 Hydrohalogenation\nTwo molecules of halogen acids are added to alkynes in two steps, to form geminal dihalides. In case of symmetric alkyne, the second step follows the Markovnikov&#x27;s rule. However, addition of halogen acids to asymmetric alkynes follows Markovnikov&#x27;s rule in both the steps.\nFor a symmetrical alkyne:\n848-\nH-CEC-H+H-Br\nBr\nCH1=CH\nethyne\nvinyl bromide (bromoethene)\nBr\nBr\n3+8-\n(Markovnikov&#x27;s rule)\nCH, CH + H-Br\nCH, CH\nvinyl bromide (bromoethene)\nFor an asymmetric alkyne;\n5+ 5- (Markovnikov&#x27;s rule)\nH-C=C-CH, + H-Br\npropyne\nBr\n1,1-dibromoethane\n(a gem-dibromide)\nBr\nCH, C-CH,\n2-bromopropene\nBr\nBr\n(Markovnikov&#x27;s rule)\nCH, C-CH, + H-Br\nCH,-C-CH,\nBr\n2-bromopropene\n2,2-dibromopropane\n137\n16.7.8.4 Hydration\nNOT FOR SALE\nAlkynes undergo acid-catalyzed addition of water across the triple bond in the presence of mercuric ion as a catalyst. A mixture of mercuric sulfate in aqueous sulfuric acid is commonly used as the reagent. The hydration of alkynes is similar to the hydration of alkenes, and it also goes with Markovnikov orientation. The enol formed on tautomerization give corresponding aldehyde or ketone. The product is an aldehydes in\ncase of acetylene, and ketone in case of higher alkynes.\nOH\n0\nH-C=C-H + H-OH\n- H.SO,, HgSO,\n100°C\nCH, CH\nethyne\nvinyl alcohol\nOH\nCH,—C—H acetaldehyde\nH2SO,, HgSO,\nH-CEC-CH,+H-OH\n+CH, C-CH,\n100°C\nCH, C-CH,\npropen-2-ol\npropyne\nacetone\n16.7.8.5 Halogenation (Bromination)\nBromine adds to alkynes in two steps, initially forming dibromoalkene (mainly trans)\nand then a tetra bromide (tetrabromoalkanes).\nH-CEC-H+Br-Br\nethyne\nBr\nHC CH + Br-Br",
