      "16.2.2 Physical Properties",
      "16.2.2.1 Physical Properties of Alkanes",
      "First four members (C-C) are colorless gases at room temperature. Straight chain alkanes upto seventeen carbons (C,-C) are colorless liquids at room temperature. Higher unbranched alkanes having eighteen or more carbon atoms",
      "are wax like colorless solids.",
      "The boiling points of normal alkanes increase with the increase in molecular mass. Straight chain alkanes have higher boiling points than isomeric branched alkanes. For example, the boiling points of n-pentane and isopentane are 36°C",
      "and 28°C respectively.",
      "The melting points of normal alkanes also increase with increasing molecular mass. However, there is no regularity in the change in melting point with the",
      "number of carbon atoms in a molecule.",
      "Alkanes, being non-polar are soluble in non-polar solvents like ether, benzene etc. but they are insoluble in polar solvents such as water.",
      "The density of alkanes also generally increases with increase in molecular weight, till it reaches a maximum value of 0.81 g/mL. Alkanes are therefore, lighter than water and this is the reason why petroleum floats on water surface.",
