      "Figure 16.1 Orbitals overlap description in formation of C-C and C-H sigma bonds of ethane.",
      "Self-assessmitet",
      "Draw orbital hybridization model of propane and show different types of overlaps in formation of carbon-carbon and carbon-hydrogen sigma bonds.",
      "16.2.3.2 Structure of Cyclopropane",
      "In cyclopropane (C,H,), each carbon atom, like alkanes, is bonded to four other atoms, so it uses sp3-hybrid orbitals to form sigma bonds. However, in this case, the sp3-sp overlap of C-C bond is not maximum as in the case of alkanes. This is due to the fact that in cyclopropane the C-C-C bond angle is 60° and not 109.5° (tetrahedral angle) due to which orbitals do not overlap exactly along their axes (Figure.16.2), but overlap outside of inter-nuclear axes, and form bent bonds. The C-C bond of cyclopropane is therefore weaker than C-C bond of alkane, due to less effective overlap of sp-hybrid orbitals of carbon in cycopropane. The C-H bonds are formed by overlap of sp3-hybrid orbital of carbon and is orbital of hydrogen.",
      "In cyclopropane, the three carbon atoms occupy the corners of an equilateral triangle, which results in the C-C-C bond angles to be of 60°. Since the hybridization of carbon is sp3, these C-C-C bond angles should be 109.5°.This deviation from the normal tetrahedral angle of 109.5° to 60° causes a strain in molecule, called Angle strain. The H-C-H bond angle is approximately 115° and the C-C and C-H bond lengths are 151 pm and 108 pm respectively.",
