      "117",
      "Let us consider buta-1,3-diene to illustrate the phenomenon of conjugation. All the four carbon",
      "Tidbit",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "atoms in buta-1,3-diene are sp2-hybridized. The When the double bonds in a diene",
      "C-C and C-H sigma bonds are the result of the overlap of sp2-hybrid orbitals with each other",
      "are separated from each other by two or more single bonds, they are called isolated double bonds. Adjacent",
      "and with Is orbitals of the hydrogen atoms double bonds in a diene are referred respectively. All the carbon and hydrogen atoms as comulated double bonds. lie in the same plane. Additionally, each of the four carbon atom also possess an unhybridized p orbital, which are perpendicular to the plane of sigma bonds. These four porbitals overlap to form delocalized pi molecular orbitals (Figure 16.4). These delocalized pi electrons are thus attracted by all four carbon atoms. The delocalization of pi electrons is responsible for greater stability of buta-1,3-diene. As a consequence of conjugation, the C-C single bond in buta-1,3-diene is 148 pm (1.48 A) which is shorter than the normal C-C single bonds (154 pm) of alkane while the C-C double bond are 137 pm (1.37 A) which are longer than normal isolated C-C double bonds (134 pm).",
