      "CH,-CH=CH -CH3",
      "but-2-ene",
      "Functional group isomerism involves in compounds having the same formula but different functional groups. For example, ethyl alcohol and dimethyl ether are functional group isomers.",
      "CH-CH2-OH",
      "ethyl alcohol",
      "CH-O-CH,",
      "dimethyl ether",
      "Similarly, Propionaldehyde and acetone are also functional group isomers.",
      "CH, –CH, CHO propionaldehyde",
      "16.6.1.4 Metamerism",
      "CH,-CO-CH,",
      "acetone",
      "This type of isomerism is exhibited due to the unequal distribution of carbon atoms (or alkyl groups) on either side of the functional group. Isomers belong to the same homologous series. For example, diethyl ether and methyl n-propyl ether are metamers.",
      "CH,-CH,-O-CH=CH,",
      "diethyl ether",
      "16.6.1.5 Tautomerism",
      "CH,-O-CH, -CH, -CH, methyl n-propyl ether",
      "חו",
      "Tautomers are constitutional isomers that differ from each other by position of a proton (and may also a pi bond). It is a special type of functional group isomerism, in which process the isomers are in dynamic equilibrium with each other. Tautomerization is the of inter-conversion of tautomers, and an equilibrium process, which means that the equilibrium will establish with specific concentrations of tautomers of a compound. One of the important example of tautomerism is keto-enol tautomerism. The keto and enol tautomers of acetone are given below.",
      "0",
