      "16.7.3",
      "Physical Properties",
      "pm.",
      "With the exception of acetylene which has garlic like odour, all other alkynes are odourless and colourless.",
      "The first three members (ethyne, propyne and butynes) are gases, next eight straight chain alkynes (Cs-C12) are liquids and higher alkynes are solids at room temperature and pressure.",
      "The boiling points of alkynes are slightly higher than the corresponding alkanes and alkenes and increase regularly with increase in the number of carbon atoms in the alkynes. However, their melting points do not follow a regular sequence. Instead, they change irregularly with increase in the number of carbon atoms (Table 16.4). Boiling points of terminal alkynes are lower than isomeric non-",
      "terminal alkynes.",
      "Alkynes like alkanes and alkenes are non-polar, therefore, they are insoluble in water but readily dissolve in organic solvents such as ether, ethanol, acetone,",
      "carbon tetrachloride, benzene etc.",
      "The density of alkynes increases with molecular mass. They are slightly denser than the corresponding alkanes and alkenes. All alkynes are lighter",
