        "sciNote": "isopropyl chloride\ntert-butyl chloride\nThe two alkyl halides, n-propyl chloride and isopropyl chloride, given above are example of position isomers.\nby IUPAC System\nThe systematic (IUPAC) nomenclature treats an alkyl halide as an alkane with a halo- substituent: Fluorine is fluoro-, chlorine is chloro-, bromine is bromo-, and iodine is iodo. The result is a systematic haloalkane name. The following steps should be followed for naming alkyl halides.\n1. Select the longest continuous chain of carbon atoms, containing the halogen atoms. 2. Number the chain from the end closer to halogen atom. If both alkyl and halogen are present in a molecule then number the chain from the end closer to alkyl group or halogen atom.\nIndicate the position of halogen and alkyl group by suitable locants.\nPrefix di, tri, tetra etc. is used to indicate the number of halogen atoms.\nIf two different halogen atoms are present in a molecule at same position from the two ends, the one to be assigned the lower locant is that one cited first in alphabetical order. When an alkyl and halogen occupy equivalent positions, again number the chain from the end to give lowest number to one that comes first in alphabetical order.\nExamples\nCH,-CI\nchloromethane\n1\nCH,1-CH,\nCH-CH-CH2-CI\n1-chloropropane\nCH-CH-CI\nchloroethane\nCH,\nCH,\nH1C-C-Cl\nH,C-C-Cl\nH 2-chloropropane\nen\nCH, 12 3\nBr CI\nT\nCH,\n2-chloro-2-methylpropane\nF\nBr\nH,C-C-CH-CH-CH-CH-CH,\nCH,\n4-bromo-5-chloro-2,2-dimethylheptane\nH,C-\n[2 3\nH,C-C-CH2-CH-CH,\nCH, 4-bromo-",
