        "sciNote": "H\ntransition state\nmethyl alcohol\nmethyl iodide\nIt is clear from the above one-step mechanism, that entering nucleophile can attack only from the back side because the front side is blocked by the halogen atom. In the transition state, carbon-bromine bond begins to break as the carbon-oxygen bond begins to form. In other words, the extent of bond formation on one side is equal to the extent of bond breaking on the other side.\nThis one-step mechanism is supported by kinetic information. The rate is found to double when the concentration of either reactant (nucleophile and substrate) is to be doubled. The reaction is, therefore, first order in each of the reactants and second order overall. The rate equation has the following form:\nrate = k [CH,Br][OH]\nBack-side attack of nucleophile literally turns the tetrahedron of the carbon atom inside out. In the product, the nucleophile assumes a stereochemical position in space, opposite to the position of the leaving group originally occupied. This is called inversion of configuration at the carbon atom, shown in above reaction. SN2 mechanism is generally favored by polar aprotic solvents.\n172\nThe type\nNOT FOR SALE\nof mechanism followed by the secondary alkyl halides is governed by the nature of the solvent. If polar protic solvent is present, they follow SNl mechanism, while in polar aprotic solvents, SN2 mechanism is followed.\nTable 17.2 Summary of Nucleophilic Substitution in Alkyl Halides\nFactors and characteristics\nSN1\nPromoting f",
