        "sciNote": "Examples are;\nCH, -Mg-Br\nCH, -CH2-Mg-Cl\nmethylmagnesium bromide\nethylmagnesium chloride\nCH, -Mg - Br phenylmagnesium bromide\n17.2.1 Preparation of Grignard Reagents\nGrignard reagents are prepared in the laboratory by the action of alkyl or aryl halides on freshly prepared magnesium metal in anhydrous or dry ether i.e. diethyl ether or\ntetrahydrofuran.\nether\nR-X + Mg\nalkyl halide\nR - MgX Grignard reagent\n176\nNOT FOR SALE\nIt is an example of heterogeneous reaction, in which solid magnesium react with alkyl halide in ether. Grignard reagent cannot be isolated, therefore, its ethereal solution is directly used in the synthetic reactions.\nThe ease of formation of Grignard reagent depends upon the nature of both alkyl and aryl group and the nature of halogen. Increasing size of the alkyl group makes the formation of Grignard reagent difficult. For the same alkyl group, the ease of formation increases down the group i.e. from fluorine to iodine. Alkyl magnesium fluorides and chloride are not obtained with good yield. Alkyl iodides are the most reactive but still bromides are used more commonly for preparation of Grignard reagent because bromides are less expensive and less toxic than iodides.\nDiethyl ether used in the preparation of Grignard reagent plays two important roles. At one hand, it provides a medium for the reaction, at the other hand, it dissolves the Grignard reagent by the process of solvation. Crystalline Grignard reagent obtained by cooling ethereal solution of Grigna",
