        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 4",
        "theory": "CH,\ntert-butyl\ncation (tertiary]\nFigure 17.3 Realtive stability order of different types of carbocations.\nMechanisms of Nucleophilic Substitution reactions\nAlkyl halides can undergo nucleophilic substitution by two different types of mechanisms. The type of mechanism depends upon the nature of alkyl halides.\n17.1.6.3 S1 Mechanism\nsubstitution\ne\nS\nN\nunimolecular\nnucleophilic\nSN1 stands for Substitution, Nucleophilic, unimolecular. It is a two-step mechanism. First step is slow (rate determining or rate limiting step) and involves the formation of intermediate carbocation by dissociation of alkyl halide or substrate. The term unimolecular means there is only one molecule involved transition state of the rate-determing step. The second step is fast and involves the attack of nucleophile on planar carbocation and formation of product. SN1 mechanism is generally followed by tertiary alkyl halides. As it involves the formation of carbocation, it is favored by highly ionizing polar solvents (having O-H, N-H bond). For example the hydrolysis of tert-butyl bromide follow Sal mechanism as shown below.\n170\nNOT FOR SALE\nStep-1: The substrate undergoes ionization and yield a carbocation. The bromine atom completely detaches from the substrate as bromide ion.\nH,C\nCH,\nBr\nslow step\nH,C\nH,C\nH,C\n+ Br CH,\ntert-butyl bromide\ncarbocation\n(trigonal planar)\nStep-2: In second step, the entering nucleophile attacks the planar carbocation on either face, to produce the end product.\nH,C\nH1C\nCH,\nfast CH, + HẠO\nC-OH OR H2O-C\nH,C\nH,C\nCH,\ncarbocation\nH,C\nCH,\nNucleophile\n(trigonal planar)\nilabor\nCH,\nHC-OH OR HO-CCH\nH,C\nfert-butyl alcohol\nThe kinetic studies have shown that reactions proceeding through SN1 mechanism follow the first order kinetics. The rate of reaction depends only upon the concentration of substrate. The rate equation for above reaction is;\nCH1\nTidbit\n+7\nH-\nTwo transition states are formed in Syl mechanism. One in first step during formation of intermediate, the other in second step during formation of product from intermediate.\nrate = k [(CH1),CBr]\nThe intermediate carbocation is planar, the nucleophile attack from either side. If a nucleophile attacks the carbocation from the side of the leaving group, the product molecule shows retention of configuration. Attack from the side opposite to leaving group, gives a product molecule showing inversion of configuration. If the reactant is chiral, mixture of two enantiomers (Section 16.6.5) are produced, called racemic mixture and this process is called racemization. Racemization is simply a combination of retention and inversion. However in SN1, the product is rarely completely racemic\n171\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nand there is often more inversion than retention of configuration. This is also called partial racemization.\n17.1.6.4 S2 Mechanism\nsubstitution\nS\nN\nbiomolecular\nnucleophilic\nThe abbreviation SN2 stands for Substitution, Nucleophilic, bimolecular. It is a single step concerted mechanism, with bonds breaking and forming at the same time. The entering nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the substrate before the leaving group departs. The middle structure is a transition state, rather than an intermediate. The term bimolecular means that the transition state of the rate-limiting step (the only step in this reaction) involves the collision of two molecules (or ions).\nThis mechanism is generally followed by primary alkyl halides and methyl halides. For example, the reaction of hydroxide ion with methyl bromide to form methyl alcohol follow SN2 mechanism, shown below.\nH\nH\nH\nHO +\nC-Br\nHOC-\nBr\nHO-C}}\n+ Br\nH\nH\nH\nH",
