        "theory": "H\ntransition state\nmethyl alcohol\nmethyl iodide\nIt is clear from the above one-step mechanism, that entering nucleophile can attack only from the back side because the front side is blocked by the halogen atom. In the transition state, carbon-bromine bond begins to break as the carbon-oxygen bond begins to form. In other words, the extent of bond formation on one side is equal to the extent of bond breaking on the other side.\nThis one-step mechanism is supported by kinetic information. The rate is found to double when the concentration of either reactant (nucleophile and substrate) is to be doubled. The reaction is, therefore, first order in each of the reactants and second order overall. The rate equation has the following form:\nrate = k [CH,Br][OH]\nBack-side attack of nucleophile literally turns the tetrahedron of the carbon atom inside out. In the product, the nucleophile assumes a stereochemical position in space, opposite to the position of the leaving group originally occupied. This is called inversion of configuration at the carbon atom, shown in above reaction. SN2 mechanism is generally favored by polar aprotic solvents.\n172\nThe type\nNOT FOR SALE\nof mechanism followed by the secondary alkyl halides is governed by the nature of the solvent. If polar protic solvent is present, they follow SNl mechanism, while in polar aprotic solvents, SN2 mechanism is followed.\nTable 17.2 Summary of Nucleophilic Substitution in Alkyl Halides\nFactors and characteristics\nSN1\nPromoting factors\nSN2\nNucleophile\nSubstrate\nSolvent\nKinetics\nStereochemistry\n17.1.7\nWeak Nucleophile\nStrong Nucleophile\n30 &gt; 20\nCH,X&gt;1°&gt;2°\nPolar protic\nPolar aprotic\nCharacteristics\nFirst Order\nSecond Order\nInversion and retention\nInversion\n1,2-Elimination Reactions\n17.1.7.1 Overview\nWe have already discussed that alkyl halides undergo substitution as well as elimination reactions in (Section 17.1.5). When treated with a strong base, an alkyl halide can undergo a type of elimination process, called 1,2-elimination, also known as B-elimination, in which a proton is removed from the ẞ position, the halide is ejected as a leaving group from the a position and a double bond is formed between. the a and ẞ positions:\nH\nH\nX\nx.B\nC\n: Base\nH\nC=C\nAlkene\nH\nH-Base\nAlkyl halides undergo elimination reactions by two different types of mechanisms.\n17.1.7.2 E1 Mechanism\nThe abbreviation E1 stands for Elimination, unimolecular. The mechanism is called unimolecular because the rate-limiting transition\nE 1\nelimination\nunimolecular\nstate involves a single molecule rather than a collision between two molecules. The slow step of an El reaction is the same as in the SNl reaction: unimolecular ionization\n173\nNOT FOR SALE\nto form a carbocation. In a fast second step, a base abstracts a proton from the carbon atom adjacent to the positive carbon (8-carbon). These reactions are usually followed by tertiary alkyl halides, like Snl reactions. For example, reaction of tert-butyl bromide with water, upon ß-elimination yield isobutylene.\nStep-1: The first step is similar to SN1 (Section 17.1.6.3)\nStep-2: The base abstracts B-hydrogen of carbocation and yield isobutylene.\nH,C\nH,C\nH\nfast\nC-H+ H2O\nC=CH, * HO\nH,C\nIB\nH,C\nH\ncarbocation\nbase\nisobutylene\n(trigonal planar)\nBecause the rate-limiting step involves unimolecular ionization of the alkyl halide, the rate equation is first-order. The rate depends only on the concentration of the alkyl halide and not on the concentration of the base.\nrate = k [R-X] or rate = k [(CH),C-Br]\n17.1.7.3 E2 Mechanism\nThis is a bimolecular elimination process, with\nE 2\nelimination",
