        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 8",
        "theory": "Ester of formic acid is also called formate ester e.g. methyl formate, ethyl formate etc. Grignard reagent react with ethyl formate (or any other formate ester), yielding an addition product. Addition product undergoes elimination and forms an aldehyde. This\n178\nNOT FOR SALE\naldehyde further reacts with another molecule of the Grignard reagent to yield secondary alcohol as final product.\nCoMex\nR-MgX+H-C-OC2H, H-C-OCH,\n0\n-C2H,OMgX\nR-C-H\nR\naldehyde\nethyl formate\naddition product\nOH\n1. RMgX\nR-C-H\nR\n2. H,0 (workup)\n2°-alcohol\nb. Reaction with esters of higher carboxylic acids\nEster of higher carboxylic acids yield tertiary alcohol as final product. For example, ethyl propanoate reacts in the same way as ethyl formate but the end product of this reaction is a tertiary alcohol, through intermediate ketone.\nCOMEX\n-MgX + C2H-C-OCH, C2H-C-OC2H1\nC2H,-C-R\n-C2H,OMgX\nketone\nethyl propanoate\nR addition product\nOH\n1. RMgX\nCH, C-R\nR\n2. H,0 (workup)\n3°-alcohol\n7.2.3.3 Reaction with carbon dioxide\nirignard reagents react with carbon dioxide forming an addition product. Addition\nroduct\nupon acid workup yields carboxylic acids.\nR-MgX+\nC\nH2O\nR-C-OMgX\nR-C-OH\n(workup)\naddition product\ncarboxylic acid\ncarbon dioxide\nr example methylmagnesium bromide upon addition to carbon dioxide followed by\nid workup results acetic acid.\n179\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\n0\nCH,-MgBr+\nmethylmagnesium bromide\nH2O*\nCH-C-OMgBr addition product\nCH,-C-OH\n(workup)\nacetic acid\nThis method is particularly used for conversion of alkyl halides to carboxylic acids. The resulting carboxylic acids contain a carbon more than parent alkyl halide.\n17.3\nAmines\nAmines are important nitrogen containing organic compounds. These are the derivatives of ammonia in which one or more hydrogen atoms have been replaced by one or more similar or different alkyl groups (or aryl groups).\nOn the basis of the number of alkyl or aryl groups directly bonded to nitrogen atom, amines have been classified into primary, secondary and tertiary amines shown below.\nAmmonia\nH\nH\nAmines\nH\nH\nNR R\nR\nR\nH\nH\nPrimary\nSecondary\n(1)\n(20)",
