        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 11",
        "theory": "17.3.5.1 Alkylation of Ammonia by Alkyl halides\nAmmonia is a very good nucleophile and will readily undergo alkylation when treated with an alkyl halide. When an alkyl halide is heated with alcoholic ammonia, it yields a mixture of primary, secondary, tertiary amines and quaternary ammonium salt. During this reaction the hydrogen atoms of ammonia are replaced by alkyl groups of alkyl halide, therefore, this reaction is also known as alkylation of ammonia. The reaction of ammonia with methyl iodide is given below.\nH\nH\nIN H\nCH.\nH\nH\nH.C-\nH-N:\nH-N-CH,\nH-N:\nان\nH\nH\nH\nmethyl amine\nammonia\nCH, –NH, + 1 – CH\nmethyl amine\nCH,-NH-CH,+ I-CH,\ndimethyl amine\n* CH,- NH–CH,+HI\ndimethyl amine\n* CH,-N=CH,+HI\nCH12\ntrimethyl amine\n5-12-3\nCH,-N-CH, I\ne in\nCH,-N-CH,+I-CH,\nCH,\ntrimethyl amine\nquaternary methyl ammomium iodide\nIf the quaternary ammonium salt is the desired product, then an excess of the alkyl\nhalide is used.\n17.3.5.2 Reduction of Nitrogen Containing Functional Groups\n17.3.5.2.1 Reduction of Nitriles\nWhen nitriles are reduced they yield the corresponding primary amines. This reduction can be brought about by hydrogen in the presence of catalyst such as Ni, Pt or Pd\n184\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\n(catalytic hydrogenation) or by lithium aluminum hydride in ether, followed by\nworkup.\nR-CEN\n2H,/Ni or LIAIH, /ether\nR-CH2-NH2\nnitrile\n1°-amine\nCH, -CN\n1.LIAIH, /ether\n2. H2O\n* CH,–CH,–NH,\nethanamine\nNi\nCH,– CH,–CH, NH,\npropanamine\nethanenitrile\nCH, CH2-C=N+2H,\npropanenitrile\n17.3.5.2.2 Reduction of Nitroalkanes\nPrimary amines are prepared by the reduction of nitroalkanes with hydrogen in the presence of Ni or Pt or Pd catalyst. They can also be reduced by Fe or Sn in hydrochloric\nacid.\nCH, NO, + 3H2\nnitromethane\n-\n-\nCH, CH, NO,\nnitroethane\n17.3.5.2.3 Reduction of Amides\nCH, NH, + 2H2O methanamine\nFe/HCL CH,-CH2-NH2\nethanamine\nAmines are obtained when amides are reduced by lithium aluminum hydride in ether. Primary, secondary and tertiary amides yield corresponding primary, secondary and tertiary amines respectively.\nR-C-NH primary amide\nR-C-NHR\nsecondary amide\nR-C-NR2\ntertiary amide\n1. LIAIH,/ether",
