        "sciNote": "2. H2O\nR-CH2-NH2\nprimary amine\n1. LiAlH,/ether\n2. H2O\nR-CH,-NHR\nsecondary amine\n1. LIAIH,/ether\nR-CH2-NR2\n2. H2O\ntertiary amine\n185\n17.3.6 |Reactivity of Amines\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe chemistry of amines is mainly due to the lone pair of electron on nitrogen atom. This lone pair of electrons is available to the electron deficient reagents such as acids and electrophiles. Therefore, they can act as base or nucleophile in reactions. Therefore, common chemical reactions are acid-base reactions, nucleophilic substitution and nucleophilic addition reactions.\nFunctioning as a nucleophile\nFunctioning as a base\nR\nR\nR\nCI\nR\nCI\nH-CI, R-N-H\nHC-CI\nR-N-CH1\nR\nR\nR\nR\nR\nR\n17.3.7 Reactions of Amines\n17.3.7.1 Overview\nAmines are important and reactive organic compounds. These react with a number of different substances to produce valuable organic compounds. Some of their reactions are: 17.3.7.2 Alkylation of Amines by Alkyl Halides\nWhen a primary amine is treated with an alkyl halide, they produce a mixture of secondary, tertiary amines and quaternary ammonium salt. In this process the hydrogen atoms in the amine are successively replaced by the alkyl groups of alkyl halide. This reaction proceeds via SN2 mechanism.\nH\nCH,\nH.C\n[\nH\nH-N:\nH\nprimary amine\nH-N-CH 10 CH,\nN-CH,\nawa\nH-N:\nCH1\nin\nCH,\nsecondary amine.\nCH1\nH-N:\nCH,\nH.C\nH\n시\nN-CH\nH-N-CH,\nH.C\n10\nCH,\ntertiary amine\nCH\nH,C-N-CH\n10\nCH,\nquaternary ammonium salt\n17.3.7.3 Reaction of Amines with Aldehydes and Ketones\nPrimary amines react with aldehydes and"
