      "nucleophilic",
      "SN1 stands for Substitution, Nucleophilic, unimolecular. It is a two-step mechanism. First step is slow (rate determining or rate limiting step) and involves the formation of intermediate carbocation by dissociation of alkyl halide or substrate. The term unimolecular means there is only one molecule involved transition state of the rate-determing step. The second step is fast and involves the attack of nucleophile on planar carbocation and formation of product. SN1 mechanism is generally followed by tertiary alkyl halides. As it involves the formation of carbocation, it is favored by highly ionizing polar solvents (having O-H, N-H bond). For example the hydrolysis of tert-butyl bromide follow Sal mechanism as shown below.",
