      "H-Base",
      "Alkyl halides undergo elimination reactions by two different types of mechanisms.",
      "17.1.7.2 E1 Mechanism",
      "The abbreviation E1 stands for Elimination, unimolecular. The mechanism is called unimolecular because the rate-limiting transition",
      "E 1",
      "elimination",
      "unimolecular",
      "state involves a single molecule rather than a collision between two molecules. The slow step of an El reaction is the same as in the SNl reaction: unimolecular ionization",
      "173",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "to form a carbocation. In a fast second step, a base abstracts a proton from the carbon atom adjacent to the positive carbon (8-carbon). These reactions are usually followed by tertiary alkyl halides, like Snl reactions. For example, reaction of tert-butyl bromide with water, upon ß-elimination yield isobutylene.",
      "Step-1: The first step is similar to SN1 (Section 17.1.6.3)",
      "Step-2: The base abstracts B-hydrogen of carbocation and yield isobutylene.",
