      "The greater the number of alkyl groups, the greater will be the electronic density on nitrogen and therefore, greater will be the power of nitrogen to accept proton. We might expect tertiary aliphatic amines would be the strongest and primary aliphatic amines would be weakest. However in aqueous solutions, secondary is more basic than primary and tertiary aliphatic amines. Because ammonium ions (conjugate acid) are charged, they are strongly solvated by water and the energy of solvation contributes to their stability. The additional alkyl groups around the ammonium ions of tertiary amines. decrease the number of water molecules that can approach closely and solvate the ions. However, ammonium ions of primary and secondary amines are more stabilized by water through solvation. But the opposing trends of inductive stabilization and steric hindrance of solvation are balanced for secondary alkyl amines, which make them stronger bases than primary or tertiary alkyl amines in aqueous solutions. The pKь values of some amines in water are given in Table 17.4.",
