        "title": "18.1.5.4 Reaction of Grignard reagent with Esters",
        "theory": "tertiary alcohols.\n18.1.5.4 Reaction of Grignard reagent with Esters\nGrignard reagents react with esters to yield carbonyl compounds (aldehydes and ketones). These carbonyl compounds react with more of the Grignard reagent in the same reaction mixture and yield alcohols as the final products. Formate ester give secondary while higher esters yield tertiary alcohols (Section 17.2.3.2).\nOH\nR-MgX+R/H-C- OR\n-MgX(OR)\nR/H-C-R\n1. RMgX 2. H,0* aldehyde/ketone (workup)\nR/H-C-R\nester\nR\nalcohol\n18.1.5.5 Reduction of Aldehydes and Ketones Carbonyl compounds (aldehydes and ketones) can easily be reduced to alcohols, either by hydrogenation in the presence of nickel, platinum or palladium catalysts at elevated temperature (200°C) and pressure (10 atmosphere); or by using reducing agents such as lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH4) or sodium borohydride (NaBH4) in ether. Following reduction with LiAlH or NaBH4, a separate workup step with acid or event with pure water is required to liberate the alcohol product.\nO\n1. LIAIH, or NaBH,, ether\nC\n2. H2O\nOH\nC- T-\naldehyde/ketone\nH\nalcohol\nOH\nNi or Pt or Pd\nC- + H2 aldehyde/ketone\n·C·\n200°C, 10 atm\nH\nalcohol\nFormaldehyde produces methyl alcohol, higher aldehydes give primary alcohols while\nketones yield secondary alcohols.\nОН\nH-C-H\nformaldehyde\n1. LiAlH,, ether\n2. H2O\nH-C-H\nH methyl alcohol\n201\nOH\nNOT FOR SALE\nR-C-H\nR-C-H higher aldehyde\n1. LIAIH,, ether\n2. H.0\nH 1o-alcohol\nон\n1. LIAIH,, ether\nR-C-R+ H2\nR-C R\n2. HO\nketone\nH\n2°-alcohol\n18.1.5.6 Reduction of Carboxylic Acids and Esters\nCarboxylic acids are exceedingly difficult to reduce. A very powerful reducing agent such as lithium aluminium hydride is required to convert a carboxylic acid to a primary alcohol. Sodium borohydride is not a potent hydride donor as lithium aluminium hydride and does not reduce carboxylic acids.\n0\nR-C-OH\ncarboxylic acid\nOH\nно\n1. LIAIH,, ether 2. H2O\nR-C-H\nCH-C-OH\n||\n1. LiAlH,, ether\n2. H2O\nacetic acid\nH 1- alcohol\nOH\nCH, C-H\nH\nethyl alcohol\ne in\nEsters can also be reduced by lithium aluminium hydride. Two alcohols are formed from each ester molecule. The acyl group of the ester is cleaved, giving a primary alcohol.\n0\nR-C-OR\n1. LiAlH,, ether 2. HO\ncarboxylic ester\nOH",
