        "title": "18.1.7.3 Acid catalysed Dehydration",
        "theory": "+ H2O\n170°C\nH H\nH\nH\nethyl alcohol\nethene\nThe order of rate of dehydration of different alcohols is;\ntertiary alcohols &gt; secondary alcohols &gt; primary alcohols.\n204\nNOT FOR SALE\n18.1.7.4 Preparation of Esters Alcohols react with carboxylic acids in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid catalyst to form esters. This method is commonly used for preparation of esters and also called Fischer Esterification. It is a type of condensation reaction.\nR—0—II + R-C-OH\nR-C-OR HO\nester\nalcohol\ncarboxylic acid\n0\nCH,—0—H + CH1—C—OH\nH\nmethyl alcohol\nacetic acid\nCH,-C-OCH, + H2O\nmethyl acetate\nFischer esterification is reversible, and the position of equilibrium lies slightly to the side of products when the reactants are simple alcohols and carboxylic acids. When the Fischer esterification is used for preparative purposes, the position of equilibrium can be made more favorable by using either the alcohol or the carboxylic acid in excess, or removing water from the reaction mixture. For steric reasons, the order of alcohol reactivity in the Fischer esterification is; methyl alcohol &gt; primary alcohol &gt; secondary alcohol &gt; tertiary alcohol. 18.1.7.5 Oxidation\nOxidation of an alcohol yields a carbonyl compound. Whether the resulting carbonyl compound is an aldehyde, a ketone, or a carboxylic acid depends on the alcohol and on\nthe oxidizing agent.\nPrimary alcohols may be oxidized either to an aldehyde or to a carboxylic acid. Vigorous oxidation with solutions of strong oxidizing agents like, acidified chromate (Cro), dichromate (Cr,0,2) or permanganate (MnO,) solution leads to the formation of a carboxylic acid, through aldehyde intermediate.\nOH\n0\nR-C-H\nlol\n[o]\nR-C-H\nR-C-OH\nH\n-H2O\n1\n1o -alcohol\nOH\nCH1-C-H\nH\nethyl alcohol\naldehyde\ncarboxylic acid\n0\nJol\n[o]\nCH,-C-H\n→CH,-C-OH\n-H2O\nacetic acid\nacetaldehyde\n205\nNOT FOR SALE\n18.1.7.4 Preparation of Esters Alcohols react with carboxylic acids in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid catalyst to form esters. This method is commonly used for preparation of esters and also called Fischer Esterification. It is a type of condensation reaction.\nR—0—II + R-C-OH\nR-C-OR HO\nester\nalcohol\ncarboxylic acid\n0\nCH,—0—H + CH1—C—OH\nH\nmethyl alcohol\nacetic acid\nCH,-C-OCH, + H2O\nmethyl acetate\nFischer esterification is reversible, and the position of equilibrium lies slightly to the side of products when the reactants are simple alcohols and carboxylic acids. When the Fischer esterification is used for preparative purposes, the position of equilibrium can be made more favorable by using either the alcohol or the carboxylic acid in excess, or removing water from the reaction mixture. For steric reasons, the order of alcohol reactivity in the Fischer esterification is; methyl alcohol &gt; primary alcohol &gt; secondary alcohol &gt; tertiary alcohol. 18.1.7.5 Oxidation\nOxidation of an alcohol yields a carbonyl compound. Whether the resulting carbonyl compound is an aldehyde, a ketone, or a carboxylic acid depends on the alcohol and on\nthe oxidizing agent.\nPrimary alcohols may be oxidized either to an aldehyde or to a carboxylic acid. Vigorous oxidation with solutions of strong oxidizing agents like, acidified chromate (Cro), dichromate (Cr,0,2) or permanganate (MnO,) solution leads to the formation of a carboxylic acid, through aldehyde intermediate.\nOH\n0\nR-C-H\nlol\n[o]\nR-C-H\nR-C-OH\nH",
