        "title": "18.3.2 Physical Properties",
        "theory": "Ethers are highly inflammable.\nThey have much lower boiling points than those of alcohols of comparable molecular weights due to absence of hydrogen bonding (Figure 18.3).\nLow molecular weight ethers show some solubility in water as they can form hydrogen bonding with water (Figure 18.3). Ethers are readily soluble in organic solvents.\nEthers are generally less dense than water.\nThey are readily soluble in organic solvents.\nAlcohol\nEther\nwater + ether\nH\nR\nR\n0-H\nR\n0-R\n0-H\n0\nR\nR\nR\nH\nR\nhydrogen bond\ndonor\nno hydrogen bond\nhydrogen bond\nacceptor\nno donor\nacceptor\ndonor\nacceptor\nFigure 18.3 Hydrogen bonding in alcohol molecules, and between water and ether.\n3C\n217\nNOT FOR SALE\n18.3.3 Preparation\nEthers can be prepared by the following methods.\n1. Intermolecular dehydration of alcohols\nAlcohols on intramolecular dehydration with concentrated sulphuric acid, give alkenes (Sections 16.5.4.1 and 18.1.7.3). However, intermolecular dehydration of alcohol with concentrated sulphuric acid at 140°C, yields ethers.\nR-OH + HO-R\nalcohol\nH2SO\nR-O-R + H2O\n140°C\nalcohol\nether\nH2SO,\n140°C\n→ CH,−CH,−0−CH,—CH,+H,0\nCH,—CH,=OH+HO–CH,CH,\nethyl alcohol\nethyl alcohol\ndiethyl ether\nThe reaction conditions must be well controlled otherwise at temperature higher than 140°C; the formation of alkenes is favoured. This method is useful for preparation of symmetric ethers because in an attempt to prepare an unsymmetrical ether, by mixing different alcohols, we usually obtain a mixture of ethers, which is too difficult to be separated.\n2.\nWilliamson&#x27;s Synthesis\nIn this method, an alkoxide is treated with an alkyl halide. The alkoxide is usually derived from alcohols.\nROH + Na\nalcohol\nRONa+ RX\nsodium\nalkoxide\nalkyl halide\nR - ONa+ H2\nsodium alkoxide\nRO R NaX\nether\nWe in\nFor example ethyl methyl ether can be prepared as follows.\nC,H,-OH + Na\nethyl alcohol\nC2H, ONa + CH, CI\nsodium ethoxide methyl chloride\n18.3.4\nChemical Reactivity\nONa+ H2\nC,H,- ONa+\nsodium ethoxide\nCH,-O-CH, + NaCl\nethyl methyl ether\nEthers are quite stable compounds. They do not react with bases, active metals, oxidising and reducing agents. They are also stable to weak and dilute acids. However,",
        "content": "Ethers are highly inflammable.<br>They have much lower boiling points than those of alcohols of comparable molecular weights due to absence of hydrogen bonding (Figure 18.3).<br>Low molecular weight ethers show some solubility in water as they can form hydrogen bonding with water (Figure 18.3). Ethers are readily soluble in organic solvents.<br>Ethers are generally less dense than water.<br>They are readily soluble in organic solvents.<br>Alcohol<br>Ether<br>water + ether<br>H<br>R<br>R<br>0-H<br>R<br>0-R<br>0-H<br>0<br>R<br>R<br>R<br>H<br>R<br>hydrogen bond<br>donor<br>no hydrogen bond<br>hydrogen bond<br>acceptor<br>no donor<br>acceptor<br>donor<br>acceptor<br>Figure 18.3 Hydrogen bonding in alcohol molecules, and between water and ether.<br>3C<br>217<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>18.3.3 Preparation<br>Ethers can be prepared by the following methods.<br>1. Intermolecular dehydration of alcohols<br>Alcohols on intramolecular dehydration with concentrated sulphuric acid, give alkenes (Sections 16.5.4.1 and 18.1.7.3). However, intermolecular dehydration of alcohol with concentrated sulphuric acid at 140°C, yields ethers.<br>R-OH + HO-R<br>alcohol<br>H2SO<br>R-O-R + H2O<br>140°C<br>alcohol<br>ether<br>H2SO,<br>140°C<br>→ CH,−CH,−0−CH,—CH,+H,0<br>CH,—CH,=OH+HO–CH,CH,<br>ethyl alcohol<br>ethyl alcohol<br>diethyl ether<br>The reaction conditions must be well controlled otherwise at temperature higher than 140°C; the formation of alkenes is favoured. This method is useful for preparation of symmetric ethers because in an attempt to prepare an unsymmetrical ether, by mixing different alcohols, we usually obtain a mixture of ethers, which is too difficult to be separated.<br>2.<br>Williamson&#x27;s Synthesis<br>In this method, an alkoxide is treated with an alkyl halide. The alkoxide is usually derived from alcohols.<br>ROH + Na<br>alcohol<br>RONa+ RX<br>sodium<br>alkoxide<br>alkyl halide<br>R - ONa+ H2<br>sodium alkoxide<br>RO R NaX<br>ether<br>We in<br>For example ethyl methyl ether can be prepared as follows.<br>C,H,-OH + Na<br>ethyl alcohol<br>C2H, ONa + CH, CI<br>sodium ethoxide methyl chloride<br>18.3.4<br>Chemical Reactivity<br>ONa+ H2<br>C,H,- ONa+<br>sodium ethoxide<br>CH,-O-CH, + NaCl<br>ethyl methyl ether<br>Ethers are quite stable compounds. They do not react with bases, active metals, oxidising and reducing agents. They are also stable to weak and dilute acids. However,",
