        "title": "Learning outcomes & introduction",
        "theory": "Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers\nTeaching\nAssessment\nWeightage\n1\nCC CCC\nAfter studying this unit, students will be able to:\nExplain nomenclature, structure and acidity of alcohols as exemplified by ethanol. (Understanding)\nDescribe the preparation of alcohols by reduction of aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids and esters. (Applying)\nExplain reactivity of alcohols. (Understanding)\nDescribe the chemistry of alcohols by preparation of ethers and esters, oxidative cleavage of 1,2-diols. (Applying)\nDiscuss thiols (RSH). (Understanding)\nExplain the nomenclature, structure and acidity of phenols. (Applying)\ne Z\nDescribe the preparation of phenol from benzene sulphonic acid, chlorobenzene, acidic oxidation of cumene and hydrolysis of diazonium salts. (Applying)\n◆ Discuss the reactivity of phenol and their chemistry by electrophilic aromatic substitution,\nreaction with sodium metal and oxidation. (Applying)\nDifferentiate between alcohol and phenol. (Understanding) Describe isomerism in alcohols and phenols. (Understanding) Identify ethers from their formula. (Understanding)\n194\nA\nIntroduction\nJ\nNOT FOR SALE\nAlcohols, phenols and ethers are derivatives of water. In alcohol and phenol one hydrogen of water has been replaced by an alkyl group and aryl group respectively. Whereas in ether both hydrogen atom have been replaced by two alkyl (or aryl)\ngroups.\n18.1\nR-OH\nAlcohols\nAlcohols\nAr-OH\nPhenols\nAr/R-O-R/Ar\nEthers\nAlcohols are organic compounds, in which a hydroxyl group (-OH) is attached to an aliphatic carbon atom. Hydroxyl group is the functional group of alcohols. The general formula of alcohols (containing one -OH group) is R-OH or CaH2n+1OH e.g. methyl alcohol (CH3OH), ethyl alcohol (C2H2OH) etc.\nAlcohols\nb.co\nC÷OH\nhydroxyl group (functional group)\naliphatic carbon\nmay be monohydric or polyhydric, depending upon the number of -OH roups in a molecule. Monohydric alcohol contain one -OH group, while polyhydric cohols contain more than one -OH group. Polyhydric alcohols may further be lassified into dihydric alcohols (or diols), trihydric alcohols (or triol), etc. containing wo and three hydroxyl groups respectively. Dihydric alcohols (diols) are also called ycols because of their sweet taste. (Greek glycys means &quot;sweet&quot;) (Examples are given nomenclature).\nonohydric alcohols may be primary, secondary or tertiary. When the hydroxyl group attached to the primary carbon atom, it is classified as primary alcohol but when the droxyl\nis attached to the secondary or tertiary carbon atom, the alcohol is cordingly classified as secondary or tertiary alcohol.\nH\ngroup\nH\nR\nR\n++++\nH-C-OH\nH\nethyl alcohols\n8.1.1\nR-C-OH\nH\nR-C-OH\nH\nprimary (10) alcohols secondary (2°) alcohols\nNomenclature\nohols are named by common and IUPAC systems.\nR-C-OH\nR\ntertiary (3) alcohols\n195\na. Common System\nNOT FOR SALE\nIn this system, monohydric alcohols are named as &quot;alkyl alcohols&quot;. That is alkyl group of the alcohol is named first, which is followed by the word alcohol. For example,\nCH, -OH methyl alcohol\nCH, - CH2-OH\nethyl alcohol\nCH,\nCH, CH, CH, - OH\nn-propyl alcohol\nCH-CH-OH\nisopropyl alcohol\nThe n-propyl alcohol and isopropyl alcohol are isomers. The four isomeric butyl\nalcohols are names as follows.\nCH, - CH2 - CH2 – CH2 – OH\nn-butyl alcohol\nCH,\nOH&lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 460 210&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers&quot; style=&quot;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #dbeafe;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0&quot;&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;105&quot; y=&quot;112&quot; font-size=&quot;21&quot;&gt;R—OH&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;225&quot; y=&quot;112&quot; font-size=&quot;21&quot;&gt;Ar—OH&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;335&quot; y=&quot;112&quot; font-size=&quot;21&quot;&gt;R—O—R′&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;132&quot; y=&quot;155&quot; font-size=&quot;14&quot;&gt;alcohol&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;238&quot; y=&quot;155&quot; font-size=&quot;14&quot;&gt;phenol&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;345&quot; y=&quot;155&quot; font-size=&quot;14&quot;&gt;ether&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;",
