        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 3",
        "theory": "R-O-H\nThis allows the release of hydrogen atom as proton. Due to this acidic behaviour, alcohols react with sodium or potassium to form alkoxide salt with the liberation of\nhydrogen gas.\n2ROH + 2Na\nalcohol\n2RONa+ H, ↑\n2CH,CH,OH + 2Na\nethyl alcohol\nsodium alkoxide\n2CH,CH,ONa+ H2 ↑\nsodium ethoxide\nHowever, alcohols are very weakly acidic, and even less acidic than water, as clear\nfrom pKa values.\nH2O H2O\nwater\nR-OH + H2O\nalcohol\nhydroxide ion\nHO + H2O pKa = 15.7\nR-O +HO pKa = 16-18\n(conjugate base)\nalkoxide ion\n(conjugate base)\n199\nNOT FOR SALE\nare\nThe alkyl group in alcohols releases electrons (electron donating group) towards the oxygen atom and therefore decreases the stability of conjugate base of alcohol, alkoxide ion. That is why the alcohols are less acidic than water. Tertiary alcohols less acidic than secondary alcohols and secondary alcohols are less acidic than primary alcohols, because of the same electron donating effect of alkyl groups discussed above.\nH\nH-C-OH\nH\nH\nR-C-OH\nH\nmethyl alcohol\nprimary alcohol\nR\nR\nR-C-OH\nR-C-OH\nH secondary alcohol\nR\ntertiary alcohol\nIncreasing acid strength\nIt is to be noted that alcohols are not acidic enough to react with aqueous NaOH or\nKOH.\nR-OH + NaOH\n18.1.5 Preparations of Alcohols\n18.1.5.1 Hydration of Alkenes\nno reaction\nReaction of alkenes in the presence of sulphuric acid yield alcohol upon direct hydration (Section 16.5.7.3).\nH2SO,\nH.C=CH2+ H2O 100°C\nethene\n18.1.5.2 Hydrolysis of Alkyl halides\nCH-CH-OH ethyl alcohol\nNee in\nme\nAlcohols can be prepared by the hydrolysis of alkyl halides by means of water or an aqueous alkali (Sections 17.1.6.3 and 17.1.6.4).\nCH, I + KOH\nCH, OH + KI\nH2O\nmethyl iodide\nmethyl alcohol\n18.1.5.3 Reaction of Grignard reagent with Aldehydes and Ketones Grignard reagent adds to the carbonyl group of aldehyde and ketone to form an addition compound which on acid workup gives an alcohol (Section 17.2.3.1).\n+\nOMgX\nOH\nR-MgX+C-\nH,0*\n-C-\n(workup)\nR\nR\nGrignard aldehyde/ketone\nreagent\naddition product\nalcohol\n200\nNOT FOR SALE\nFormaldehyde reacts with Grignard reagents to produce primary alcohols. Other aldehydes give secondary alcohols. Ketones on reaction with Grignard reagent produce",
