        "theory": "methoxyethane\nbp 8°C\nCH,CH2-C-H propanal\nbp 49°C\nCH,CH,CH,-OH\npropane-1-ol\nbp 97°C\nCH,-C-CH,\nacetone bp 56°C\nLower aldehydes and ketones, upto four carbon atoms are water soluble. Water solubility decreases as the size of the non-polar alkyl group increases. Higher members of aldehydes and ketones are usually insoluble in water, while formaldehyde is highly soluble in water. Carbonyl compounds cannot form hydrogen bonds with themselves as there is no hydrogen on the carbonyl oxygen. However, their solubility in water is due to hydrogen bond formation of carbonyl oxygen with water molecules (Figure 19.3)\nH\nO...\nH\nH\nR\nKetone\nR\nR\nH\nAldehyde\nFigure 19.3 Hydrogen bond of aldehyde and ketone with water.\n19.4 Preparation of aldehydes and Ketones Aldehydes and ketones may be prepared by the following methods.\nA\n231\n19.4.1 Ozonolysis of Alkenes\nNOT FOR SALE\ncalled ozonide. The ozonide is reduced directly to aldehydes and ketones by zinc and Ozone reacts vigorously with alkenes to form an unstable intermediate compound water, to produce aldehyde and/or ketones. The reaction is called ozonolysis.\nmixture\nSymmetrical alkene produces single product while asymmetric alkene yield a of aldehydes/ketones (Section 16.5.7.7).For example, ozonolysis of symmetric but-2-ene (both cis and trans) gives only acetaldehyde, while asymmetric 2-methylbut-2-ene yields a mixture of acetaldehyde and acetone.\nCH.\nH\n1. 0,\nc=c\n2. Zn/H2O, 100°C\nCH1\nH\nCH1\nH\nH\nCH,\ntrans-but-2-ene\nacetaldehyde\n0\nacetaldehdye\n0\nCH,\nCH,\n+ ZnO\nH\n1. 0,\nZnO\n2. Zn/H2O, 100°C\nCH\nCH,\nCH\nH\nCH,\nacetone\nacetaldehyde\n2-methylbut-2-ene\n19.4.2 Hydration of Alkynes\nAlkynes undergo acid-catalyzed addition of water across the triple bond in the presence of mercuric sulphate and sulfuric acid as a catalyst to produce carbonyl compounds. Acetaldehyde is produced in case of acetylene, while ketones are produced from higher alkynes (Section 16.7.8.4).\n+ - H2SO,, HgSO,\nH-C=C-H + H-OH\nethyne\n100°C\nZ\nCH,-C-H\nacetaldehyde\n0\nOH\nCH, CH\nVinyl alcohol\n-\nCH, CEC-CH, +H-OH-\n8+ 5- H2SO1\nH2SO,, HgSO,\nOH\nCH2-C-CH,\nCH-C-CH",
