        "title": "19.6.4 Oxidation Reactions of Carbonyl Compounds",
        "theory": "OH\nH\n12 OH\nHO\nH\n12\nH\nOH\nOpen form\nClosed form\nFructose can also cyclize to form a five-member ring structure, called furanose ring, which is also a hemiacetal. The five-member rings formed when the -OH group on C-5 reacts with the carbonyl group on C-2.\nсон\nLo\nносна\nносна\nHO-H\nHOH\nOH\nOH\n5\nH\n[H]\n.0.\nOH\n5\n2 CH2OH\nHO 2\nH\nHOH\nH\n3 CH2OH\nOH\nH\nOH\nH 1\nCH2OH\nD-Fructose\nA furanosa ring\nD-Fructose can also cyclize to give pyranose forms that result from the reaction involving\nthe hydroxyl group at C-6.\nCH2OH\nно-н\nHOH\nHOH\nCH2OH\nD-Fructose\nOH\nOH\nH\nH\n2\nH\nOH\nOH\nHO\n2CH2OH\nCH2OH\nHO\n13\n3\n1\nOH H\nOH\nH\n243\nА ругалова ring\nNOT FORSALE\nKey Points\nAldehydes and ketones are two different classes of organic compounds\ncontaining carbonyl functional group.\nOxidation of primary alcohols gives aldehydes and that of secondary alcohol\ngives ketones.\nThe carbon-oxygen bond in aldehydes and ketones is highly polar and hence\nthey are very reactive compounds.\nBoth aldehydes and ketones undergo nucleophilic addition reactions.\nGenerally, ketones are somewhat less reactive than aldehydes towards\nnucleophiles.\nLower aldehydes and ketones are water soluble.\nAldehydes are oxidized to alcohols in the presence of oxidizing agents like acidified dichromate and permanganate, as well a mild oxidising agent. ◆ Ketones are quit resistant to oxidation and can only be oxidised by strong\noxidizing agents in drastic conditions.",
