        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 11",
        "theory": "i. Both aldehydes and ketones contain carbonyl group, then what is the basic\ndifference between an aldehyde and a ketone?\nHow acid catalyst increases the electrophilic character of carbonyl carbon? Addition reaction is the characteristic of a double bond. Aldehydes and ketones undergo nucleophilic addition to carbon-oxygen double bond, while alken undergo electrophilic addition to carbon-carbon double bond. Why?\niv. Aldehydes are oxidized easily than ketones. Why?\nV.\nHydration of aldehyde and ketone can be catalyzed by both acid and base, bu addition of hydrogen cyanide can only be catalysed by base. Why?\nvi. Why addition of Grignard reagent to a carbonyl generally do not require am\ncatalyst?\nvii. How ketones are somewhat less reactive than aldehydes towards nucleophile? viii. Why lower aldehydes and ketones are highly soluble in water, although there\nno acidic hydrogen available in these compounds?\n246\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nix. The carboxylic acid(s) formed upon oxidation of ketones, must have carbon\natoms less than parent ketone. Why?\nX.\nBriefly explain a test that can be used for detection of aldehydes from ketones\nin laboratory.\nQ3. Write detail answers of the following questions.\n1. (a) Write the structural formulae and give IUPAC names for all possible\naldehydes and ketones isomers of the molecular formula C,H,O.\n(b) Explain the structure and reactivity of carbonyl compounds.\n2. (a) Give a reaction for the formation of the following from aldehydes and\nketones.\ni. Cyanohydrin\niv. Hydrazone\nii. Oxime\nv. Acetal\niii. Imine\n(b) Using appropriate Grignard reagent and aldehyde or ketone, how each of the following can be prepared?\ni. butan-1-ol\niv. 2-methylbutan-2-ol\nii. ethyl alcohol iii. butan-2-ol\n3. (a) Write any three methods for preparation of aldehydes.\n(b) How does acetaldehyde react with the following reagents?\nEthyl magnesium iodide\nila\niii. Lithium aluminium hydride\nii. Zinc-mercury amalgam in HCI iv. Acidified dichromate\n(a) How aromatic ketones are prepared by Friedel-Craft acylation? (b) Starting from acetone, how will you prepare each of the following?\ni. propane\niv. acetone hydrazone\nii. propan-2-ol\niii. acetone cyanohydrin\nv. acetic acid\n(a) Explain the acid and base-catalysed mechanism of nucleophilic addition to\ncarbonyl group.\n(b) Discuss the physical properties of aldehydes and ketones.\n(c) Write the systematic names for the following organic compounds;\ni.\nH,C-C-CH=CH-CH,\niii.\n.CH,\nCH, CH,\nH-C-CH-CH-CH,\nii.\niv.\n247\nCH, CH,\niff\nH1C-CH2-C-CH-CH-CH,\nO CH, Br O\nH-C-CH-CH-C-H\nV.\n0\nCH,\nNOT FOR SALE\nH-C-C=CH-C-H\n(d) Draw the structural formulae for the following organic compounds;\nii. 2,2-dimethylbutanal i. 2,4-dimethylpent-2-enal\niii. 3,3-dimethylhex-4-en-2-one iv. 4-methylhex-5-en-3-one v. pent-4-yn-2-one\nProject\nIdentify aldehydes and ketones in the laboratory tests.\nAdd 50 mL of 0.1 M AgNO, to the beaker and add NH4OH to this. A brown precipitate will form. Continue adding NH4OH until the solution becomes clear. To this, add 25 ml of 0.8 M KOH. Again, add NH4OH until solution becomes clear.\nTo perform demonstration, add 5 mL of glucose solution to a flask (or test tube) and to this add 25 mL of Tollen&#x27;s reagent. Stopper the flask and swirl the solution so that the whole of the inner surface of the flask is wetted. The solution will turn yellow and brown then become cloudy and dark before silver begins to form on the inside of the flask. This should take a couple of minutes. When a satisfactory mirror has formed, rinse the flask and analyse the silver mirror.\nawaz e\n248\nNOT FORSALE\nUnit\n20",
        "content": "i. Both aldehydes and ketones contain carbonyl group, then what is the basic<br>difference between an aldehyde and a ketone?<br>How acid catalyst increases the electrophilic character of carbonyl carbon? Addition reaction is the characteristic of a double bond. Aldehydes and ketones undergo nucleophilic addition to carbon-oxygen double bond, while alken undergo electrophilic addition to carbon-carbon double bond. Why?<br>iv. Aldehydes are oxidized easily than ketones. Why?<br>V.<br>Hydration of aldehyde and ketone can be catalyzed by both acid and base, bu addition of hydrogen cyanide can only be catalysed by base. Why?<br>vi. Why addition of Grignard reagent to a carbonyl generally do not require am<br>catalyst?<br>vii. How ketones are somewhat less reactive than aldehydes towards nucleophile? viii. Why lower aldehydes and ketones are highly soluble in water, although there<br>no acidic hydrogen available in these compounds?<br>246<br>*<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>ix. The carboxylic acid(s) formed upon oxidation of ketones, must have carbon<br>atoms less than parent ketone. Why?<br>X.<br>Briefly explain a test that can be used for detection of aldehydes from ketones<br>in laboratory.<br>Q3. Write detail answers of the following questions.<br>1. (a) Write the structural formulae and give IUPAC names for all possible<br>aldehydes and ketones isomers of the molecular formula C,H,O.<br>(b) Explain the structure and reactivity of carbonyl compounds.<br>2. (a) Give a reaction for the formation of the following from aldehydes and<br>ketones.<br>i. Cyanohydrin<br>iv. Hydrazone<br>ii. Oxime<br>v. Acetal<br>iii. Imine<br>(b) Using appropriate Grignard reagent and aldehyde or ketone, how each of the following can be prepared?<br>i. butan-1-ol<br>iv. 2-methylbutan-2-ol<br>ii. ethyl alcohol iii. butan-2-ol<br>3. (a) Write any three methods for preparation of aldehydes.<br>(b) How does acetaldehyde react with the following reagents?<br>Ethyl magnesium iodide<br>ila<br>iii. Lithium aluminium hydride<br>ii. Zinc-mercury amalgam in HCI iv. Acidified dichromate<br>(a) How aromatic ketones are prepared by Friedel-Craft acylation? (b) Starting from acetone, how will you prepare each of the following?<br>i. propane<br>iv. acetone hydrazone<br>ii. propan-2-ol<br>iii. acetone cyanohydrin<br>v. acetic acid<br>(a) Explain the acid and base-catalysed mechanism of nucleophilic addition to<br>carbonyl group.<br>(b) Discuss the physical properties of aldehydes and ketones.<br>(c) Write the systematic names for the following organic compounds;<br>i.<br>H,C-C-CH=CH-CH,<br>iii.<br>.CH,<br>CH, CH,<br>H-C-CH-CH-CH,<br>ii.<br>iv.<br>247<br>CH, CH,<br>iff<br>H1C-CH2-C-CH-CH-CH,<br>O CH, Br O<br>H-C-CH-CH-C-H<br>V.<br>0<br>CH,<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>H-C-C=CH-C-H<br>(d) Draw the structural formulae for the following organic compounds;<br>ii. 2,2-dimethylbutanal i. 2,4-dimethylpent-2-enal<br>iii. 3,3-dimethylhex-4-en-2-one iv. 4-methylhex-5-en-3-one v. pent-4-yn-2-one<br>Project<br>Identify aldehydes and ketones in the laboratory tests.<br>Add 50 mL of 0.1 M AgNO, to the beaker and add NH4OH to this. A brown precipitate will form. Continue adding NH4OH until the solution becomes clear. To this, add 25 ml of 0.8 M KOH. Again, add NH4OH until solution becomes clear.<br>To perform demonstration, add 5 mL of glucose solution to a flask (or test tube) and to this add 25 mL of Tollen&#x27;s reagent. Stopper the flask and swirl the solution so that the whole of the inner surface of the flask is wetted. The solution will turn yellow and brown then become cloudy and dark before silver begins to form on the inside of the flask. This should take a couple of minutes. When a satisfactory mirror has formed, rinse the flask and analyse the silver mirror.<br>awaz e<br>248<br>NOT FORSALE<br>Unit<br>20",
