        "id": "19.4",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 3",
        "theory": "100°C\npropyne\npropen-2-ol\n19.4.3\nOxidation of Alcohols\nacetone\nOxidation of a primary alcohol with strong oxidizing agents like, acidified chromate (CrO42), dichromate (Cr2O72) or permanganate (MnO4) solution leads to the formation of a carboxylic acid as final product, through aldehydes intermediate. However, good yield of aldehydes can be obtained using oxidizing agents such as\n232\nNOT FOR SALE\npyridinium chlorochromate (PCC), a complex of chromium trioxide with pyridine and HCl, without over (excessive) oxidation (Section 18.1.7.5).\nOH\nR-C-H + [0]\nT\nH\n1°-alcohol\nOH\nCH,-C-H + [0]\nH\nethyl alcohol\nPCC,CH,CI,\n-H2O\nR-C-H aldehyde\nPCC, CH, CI,\nCH,-C-H\n-H2O\nacetaldehyde\nSecondary alcohols are oxidized to ketones by the same reagents that oxidize primary\nalcohols.\nilab\nOH\nR-C-H + [0]\nR\n2°-alcohol\nOH\nCH,-C-H + [0]\nI\nCH,\nisopropyl alcohol.\nR-C-R\n-H2O\nketone\n-H2O\nCH1-C-CH1\nacetone\n19.4.4 Friedel-Crafts Acylation of Aromatics\nBenzene reacts with acid halides (or anhydrides) in the presence of a Lewis acid catalyst. to give aromatic ketones. For example, benzene react with acetyl chloride in the presence of aluminum chloride catalyst to form acetophenone (Section 16.8.5.2.6).\nbenzene\n+\nar\nCH, C-Cl\nacetyl chloride\nAICI,\nacetophenone\nCH,\n+ HCl\n233\n19.5 Reactivity of Carbonyl Group\nNOT FOR SALE\nderivatives. Their most common reactions are nucleophilic addition; addition of a Ketones and aldehydes undergo many reactions to give a wide variety of useful nucleophile and a proton across the C=O double bond. The reactivity of the carbonyl group arises from the high electronegativity of the oxygen atom and the resulting polarization of the carbon-oxygen double bond. The electrophilic carbonyl carbon carbon) and is more susceptible for attack by a nucleophile, from either side of the atom is sp2-hybridized and planar, and relatively unhindered (compare to tetrahedral double bond. When a nucleophile attacks the carbonyl group, the carbon atom changes hybridization from sp2 to sp, with corresponding change in geometry from trigonal\nplanar to tetrahedral as shown below.\nNu:\nR\nR\nNu\n0:\nR\nR\nsp3 (Tetrahedral)\n(Trigonal planar)\n19.6 Reactions of Aldehydes and Ketones.\nein\nAldehydes and ketones are very reactive substances mostly undergoing nucleophile addition reaction across C=O bond. These can also be oxidized as well as reduced.\nSome of their reactions are;\n19.6.1\nNucleophilic Addition reactions: (Acid and Base catalyzed) As already discussed in Section 19.5, that aldehydes and ketones mainly undergo nucleophilic additions reactions. In nucleophilic addition reaction, the nucleophile attacks on the carbonyl carbon, and the hybridization of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3. The geometry also changes from trigonal planar to tetrahedral. The electrons of the pi bond are forced out to the oxygen atom to form an alkoxide ion, which is protonated to give the product of nucleophilic addition. A generalized example of nucleophilic addition of strong nucleophile (Nu), followed by protonation of alkoxide ion is shown below.\n234\nNuF\nRom\nR\nnucleophilic attack",
