        "id": "19.5",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 4",
        "theory": "NOT FOR SALE\nNu\nNu\nR\n-OF\nH-Nu\nR\nRC-O\nR\nalkoxide\nThese reactions may be either acid catalyzed or base catalyzed. a. Acid-Catalyzed Addition Reactions\nproduct\nH\n+ Nu\nBecause of the unshared electron pair on oxygen atom, carbonyl oxygen acts as Lewis base, and can be protonated. Acid can catalyze the addition of weak nucleophiles to carbonyl compounds by protonating the carbonyl oxygen atom, to form an oxonium ion. This increases the electrophilic nature of the carbonyl carbon by inducing more positive charge on it and thus enhances its ability to be attacked by weak nucleophiles. For example, the mechanism of acid-catalyzed nucleophilic addition of generalized\nreaction is given below.\nila\nH\nH\nH*\nOH\nNu-H\n-H*\nNu\nproduct\naldehyde/ketone\nresonance structures\nof oxonium ion\nAddition of water, alcohol, ammonia, amines etc. generally follow this mechanism. b. Base-Catalyzed Addition Reactions\nA base-catalyzed nucleophilic addition will take place in the presence of a strong base, which is usually the conjugate base of a reagent used for nucleophilic addition. The base which is also a nucleophile, attacks at the carbon atom of the carbon-oxygen double bond, and pi electrons of C-O bond move to the oxygen atom, which can easily accommodate the negative charge, because of its high electronegativity. The reaction is completed by the abstraction of a proton, from the reagent by negative oxygen, to regenerate the base. Thus, base increases the nucleophilic character of the reagent to\nbe added.\naldehyde/ketone\nभू\nNu\nOH\nH-Nu\n+ Nu\nNu\nNu\nalkoxide ion\nproduct\n235\n*\nNOT FOR SALE\nAddition of hydrogen cyanide in the presence of cyanide base to form cynohydrins,\nfollow this type of mechanism (Section 19.6.3.3).\n19.6.2\nRelative Reactivity\nGenerally aldehydes are more reactive than ketones towards nucleophilic additions, They usually react more quickly than ketones and the position of the equilibrium usually lies more toward the products than with ketones. The enhanced reactivity of\naldehydes is due to an electronic effect and a steric effect.\n(a) Electronic Effect\nA ketone has two electron donating alkyl groups that can stabilize (neutralize) the partial positive on the carbon atom of the carbonyl group. In contrast, aldehydes have only one electron-donating group (formaldehyde has none); thus the partial positive charge of an aldehyde is less stabilized than a ketone. As a result, aldehydes are more electrophilic than ketones and therefore more reactive.\nR\nO\nA ketone\nR\nhas two electron-donating alkyl groups that stabilize the partial positive charge\n(b)\nSteric Hindrance\nR + H An aldehyde\nhas only one electron-donating alkyl group that stabilizes the partial positive charge\nAn aldehyde has only one bulky alkyl group (except formaldehyde) while ketone has two alkyl groups with carbonyl carbon. Thus the aldehydic carbonyl carbon is more exposed toward nucleophilic attack, than ketones. Similarly the transition state of nucleophilic addition of aldehyde is less crowded and of lower energy\nketones.\nav\nthan\nNu\nNu\nHO\nNu\nH-Nu\nR\nR\nR\n-Nu\nR\nR\nR\nketone less electrophilic\n0\nalkoxide",
