      "of oxonium ion",
      "Addition of water, alcohol, ammonia, amines etc. generally follow this mechanism. b. Base-Catalyzed Addition Reactions",
      "A base-catalyzed nucleophilic addition will take place in the presence of a strong base, which is usually the conjugate base of a reagent used for nucleophilic addition. The base which is also a nucleophile, attacks at the carbon atom of the carbon-oxygen double bond, and pi electrons of C-O bond move to the oxygen atom, which can easily accommodate the negative charge, because of its high electronegativity. The reaction is completed by the abstraction of a proton, from the reagent by negative oxygen, to regenerate the base. Thus, base increases the nucleophilic character of the reagent to",
