      "19.6.4 Oxidation Reactions of Carbonyl Compounds",
      "Both aldehydes and ketones undergo oxidation in the presence of oxidizing agents, however, aldehydes are more easily oxidized han ketones as the hydrogen atom attached to the carbonyl group in aldehydes is oxidized to -OH group.",
      "Tidbit",
      "OR acetal",
      "Acetals formed from ketones are often been dropped from the IUPAC called ketals, although this term has nomenclature and the term acetal is now applied to the adducts of both aldehydes and ketones.",
      "a. Oxidation of Aldehydes Aldehydes are easily oxidized to carboxylic acids, in the presence of strong oxidizing agents like acidified permanganate or acidified dichromate, but also by mild oxidizing agents like Fehling's solution, Benedict's solution and Tollen's reagent. Oxidation with strong oxidizing agents is already discussed in (Section 18.1.75). Oxidation with mild oxidizing agents such as Fehling's solution, Benedict's solution and Tollen's reagent are used to distinguish aldehydes from ketones, as ketones are not oxidized by these reagents.",
      "Tollen's reagent test",
      "follen's reagent is ammonical silver nitrate and prepared by adding dilute solution of mmonia to solution of silver nitrate until the initially formed precipitate of silver(1) xide dissolves. Ammonia forms a complex of diamminesilver(1) hydroxide [Ag(NH3)2JOH) which is water soluble. When aldehyde is heated with this Tollen's agent, it oxidizes the aldehyde to corresponding carboxylate ion and the silver ion resent in Tollen's reagent, is reduced to metallic silver which deposits on the walls of",
      "241",
      "LosCALE",
      "test tube and forms a silver mirror. It is also called silver mirror test. Formation of silver mirror indicate the presence of aldehyde because ketone cannot be oxidized by Tollen's",
      "reagent.",
