        "theory": "R-CEN + 2H2O + HCl\nnitrile\nBase-catalyzed hydrolysis:\nR-CEN+H2O + NaOH\nnitrile\nR-C-OH + NH Cl\ncarboxylic acid\nR-C-ONa+ NH,\nsodium carboxylate\nThe reaction proceeds through the formation of an amide intermediate which may be isolated if desired. Hydrolysis of ethanenitrile yield acetic acid.\n20.5.3\nCH, CEN + 2H2O\nethanenitrile\nH or OH\nCH, C-OH + NH,\nacetic acid\nOxidation of Primary Alcohols\nPrimary alcohols can be oxidized to carboxylic acids by oxidizing agents like acidified permangnate or dichromate, through intermediate aldehyde. (Section 18.1.7.5)\nон\n0\n0\nR-C-H\nJol\n[o]\nR-C-H\nR-C-OH\naldehyde\ncarboxylic acid\nH 1°-alcohol\nOH\nLawa in\nCH,-C-H\nH2O\nCH,-C-H\nacetaldehyde\nethyl alcohol\n20.5.4\n[0]\n0\nCH1-C-OH\nacetic acid\nOxidation of Aldehydes.\nOxidation of af aldehyde in the presence of oxidizing agents like acidified permanganate dichromate or silver oxide gives a carboxylic acid with the same number of carbon atoms (Sections 18.1.7.5 and 19.6.4).\n258\nNOT FOR SALE\n[o]\nR-C-H\naldehyde\n20.5.5 Oxidation of Alkyl Benzenes\nR-C-OH\ncarboxylic acid\nBenzoic acid can be prepared by the oxidation of alkyl side chain present on the benzene ring, with oxidizing agent like alkaline permanganate. Any side chain is converted to carboxyl group, and benzoic acid is formed in product. The purple colour of the potassium permanganate is eventually replaced by a dark brown precipitate of manganese dioxide. The mixture is finally acidified with dilute sulphuric acid.\nCH,\nCOOH\n1. KMnO1, OH\n+ 3[0]\n2. H,0*\ntoluene\nCH2-CH,\n1. KMnO1, OH\n+ 6[0]\n2. H2O*\nethylbenzene\n+ H2O\nbenzoic acid\nCOOH\n+ CO2+ 2H2O\nbenzoic acid\nThe reaction illustrates the striking stability of aromatic rings towards oxidizing agents.\n20.6 Reactivity\nThe carboxylic group is so named because it contains a carboxyl group and, a hydroxyl group. The two groups influence each other to such an extent that the reactivity of carboxylic acids shows little resemblance to those of carbonyl compounds (aldehydes and ketones) on one hand and alcohols on the other hand.\nbut\nAlthough the carboxylic acid (and its derivatives) also contain a carbonyl group, still they do not exhibit the characteristic reactions of aldehydes and ketones. For example, they do not undergo nucleophilic addition reactions and more commonly react by nucleophilic acyl substitution, where one nucleophile replaces another on the acyl (C-0) carbon atom, shown below.\n0\nR-C-Z + Nu\ncarboxylic acid\nor derivative\n0\nR-C Nu +\nZ",
