        "theory": "Carbon\natoms\nHCOOH\nformic acid\nCH3COOH\n2\nacetic acid\n3\nCH3CH2COOH propionic acid\n4\nLA\n5\nCH3CH2CH2COOH butyric acid\nCH3CH2CH2CH2COOH\nvaleric acid\nSources\nFrom Latin word formica means &quot;ant&quot;\nFrom Latin word acetum means &#x27;vinegar&#x27;\nFrom Greek word protos means &#x27;first&quot; and pion means &quot;fat&quot;\nFrom Latin word butyrum means &quot;butter&#x27;\nIsolated from roots of valerian plant\nIn case of branched acids, the common name of the acid is also used. Substituents are located with Greek letters a, ß,7,8 etc. as given below.\nB\na\nCH-CH-COOH\nCH,\na-methylpropionic acid\n(isobutyric acid)\nY\nB\nCH-CH-CH-COOH\nBr\nB-bromobutyric acid\n251\nH\n(b)\nIUPAC Nomenclature\nNOT FOR SALE\nAliphatic monocarboxylic acids are named as alkanoic acids. These are obtained by replacing the ending &#x27;-e&#x27; of the corresponding alkane with the suffix -oic acid&#x27;e.g.\n0\nH-C-OH\nmethanoic acid\nO\nCH,-C-OH\nethanoic acid\nO\nCH, CH2—C—OH\npropanoic acid\nIn case of branched acids, the chain is numbered starting from the carboxyl carbon atom\nand substituents are located in the usual way e.g.\nCH,\n0\nCH-CH-CH-C-OH\n3-methylbutanoic acid\nار\nCH, Br\nCH-CH-CH-C-OH\n2-bromo-3-methylbutanoic acid\nCarboxylic acids containing two carboxyl groups are named as alkanedioic acid. Their trivial names are also given in parenthesis.\n0 0\n။\n0\n요\nHO-C-CH-C-OH HO-C-CH2-CH2-C-OH\npropanedioic acid\nbutanedioic acid (succinic acid)\nHO-C-C-OH\nethanedioic acid\n(oxalic acid)\n(malonic acid)\nSimilarly, the unsaturated carboxylic acids are named as alkenoic acid, alkynoic acid e.g.\n3\nCH=CH-COOH\npropenoic acid\nCH,-C=C-COOH\n2-butynoic acid (but-2-ynoic acid)\nIf carboxyl group is attached to a cyclic ring, it is named by adding suffix -carboxylic acid to name of cycloalkane.\nCOOH\ncyclohexanecarboxylic acid\nNomenclature of acid halides",
