        "theory": "am\nCOOH\ncyclopentanecarboxylic acid\nThe names of acid halides are derived from the names of corresponding carboxylic acids. The ending -ic of the name of acid is replaced by -yl, and the word acid is\nreplaced by halide, in both common and IUPAC systems.\nH-C-Cl\nformyl chloride\n0\nCH,-C-Cl\nCommon name: IUPAC name:\nacetyl chloride\nmethanoyl chloride\n252\nethanoyl chloride\nCH3-CH2-C-Cl\npropionyl chloride propanoyl chloride\nNOT FOR SALE\nNomenclature of amides Names of amides are also derived from carboxylic acids. The ending -ic acid of common names and -oic acid from IUPAC name is replaced by -amide.\n0\nHC—NH,\n0\nCH,-C-NH, CH,-CH,-CNH,\nCommon name:\nformamide\nIUPAC name:\nmethanamide\nNomenclature of esters\nacetamide\nethanamide\npropionamide propanamide\nThe names of esters consist of two words. The first word is derived from the alkyl group of the alcohol (alkyl group attached to oxygen), and the second word from the carboxylate group of the carboxylic acid, with the ending-ic acid is replaced by-ate in both common\nand IUPAC systems.\n0\nH-C-OCH,\nCommon name: methyl formate\nIUPAC name: methyl methanoate\nCH-C-OCH, CH-C-OCH, CH-CH-C-OCH,\nmethyl acetate\nethyl acetate\nmethyl propionate\nmethyl ethanoate\nethyl ethanoate\nmethyl propanoate\nNomenclature of acid anhydrides\nAnhydrides are named by replacing the word &#x27;acid&#x27; of the parent acid by anhydride. For mixed anhydrides, the parent name of each acid is written in alphabetic order, followed by word anhydride.\nCH,-C-0-C-CH,\nCommon name:\nacetic anhydride\nIUPAC name:\nethanoic anhydride\nNomenclature of nitriles\n-E-O-E-CH,\nCH-CH-C-0-C-CH,\nacetic propionic anhydride ethanoic propanoic anhydride\nof\nNitriles contain the cyano (-C=N) group. Although nitriles lack the carbonyl group carboxylic acids, they are classified as acid derivatives because they hydrolyze to give carboxylic acids and can be synthesized by dehydration of amides.\nCommon names of nitriles are derived from the corresponding carboxylic acids. Begin with the common name of the acid, and replace the suffix -ic acid with the suffix -onitrile. They can also be named as alkyl cyanides in common system, written in parenthesis. The IUPAC name is constructed from the alkane name, with the suffix-nitrile added.\nCH-C=N CH-CH,-C=N CH,-CH,-CH,C=N\nCommon name: acetonitrile\nIUPAC name:\n(methyl cyanide)\nethanenitrile\npropiononitrile (ethyl cyanide) propanenitrile\n253\nbutyronitrile (n-propyl cyanide) butanenitrile\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe carboxylate ion exists as a resonance hybrid of two canonical forms.\nCö:\nR-C\n0:\nR-C\nÖ:\nThe resonance hybrid can be represented as\nR-\nof-\nTo understand the greater acidity of carboxylic acids compared with alcohols, consider\nthe ionization and Ka (and pKa) values of ethyl alcohol and acetic acid.\nIonization of ethyl alcohol:\nC2 H1 —OH + H2O -\nethyl alcohol",
