        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 5",
        "theory": "substitution product\nwww\n259/\nNOT FOR SALE\nketones, because the carbon atom of the carboxyl group is less electrophilic, due to The carboxyl group is also less susceptible to nucleophilic attack than aldehydes and delocalization of one of lone-pair of hydroxyl group with carbonyl (Section 20.2).\nH-C\nOH\nརྔ\n0:\nH-C\nOH\nSimilarly the carbonyl group withdraws electrons from hydroxyl, making the O-H bond more polar and it becomes easier for the hydrogen atom to ionize than O-H bond of\nalcohols. Hence, carboxylic acids are more acidic than alcohols.\n20.7 Reactions of Carboxylic Acids\nFollowing are some of the reactions of carboxylic acids that involve the conversion of the carboxylic acids to their derivatives as well as synthesis of some other compounds.\n20.7.1 Conversion to Carboxylic Acid Derivatives\n20.7.1.1 Conversion to Acyl Halides\nAcyl halides or acid halides are the most reactive of carboxylic acid derivatives and they can be used for preparation of other functional derivatives of carboxylic acid. Acyl chlorides are more common and less expensive than bromides and iodides. They are usually prepared by replacing the -OH of carboxyl with -Cl, using thionyl chloride or phosphorous pentachloride.\nR-C-OH + SOCI2\nR—C—Cl + SO2 + HCl\ncarboxylic acid\n0\nthionyl chloride\nacid chloride\nR-C-OH + PCI,\ncarboxylic acid phosphorous\npentachloride\nR-C-CI + POCI, + HCl\n20.7.1.2 Conversion to Acid Anhydrides\nacid chloride\nAcid anhydrides are derived from acids by removing water from two carboxylic acid molecules. As the name indicates, these are anhydrous carboxylic acids. They are actually diacyl oxides. The dehydration of water from carboxylic acid to form anhydride, can be accomplished by using dehydration agents like phosphorous\npentaoxide.\n260\n36\nR-C OF + HO-C-R\ncarboxylic acids\n0\nCH-C-O1 +0-C-CH,\nacetic acids\n7.1.3 Conversion to Esters.\nNOT FOR SALE\nP2O,\nR-C-0-C-R\nP2O,\nanhydride\nii\nCH,-C-0-C-CH,\nacetic anhydride\nters are a class of organic compounds that are derived from acids by replacing the droxyl group (-OH) by an alkoxy group (-OR). They are obtained by elimination of\nater molecule from an acid and an alcohol.\nhen a carboxylic acid and an alcohol are heated in the presence of an acid catalyst, equilibrium is established with the formation of ester and water. This reaction is versible and called esterification (Section 18.1.7.4). The position of equilibrium can made more favorable in the right, by using either the alcohol or the carboxylic acid excess, or removing water from the reaction mixture.\n0\nR-C-OP\ncarboxylic acid\nCO\nH*\n-O-R\nalcohol\nR-C-OR+\nester\nhyl acetate is an important ester which can be prepared by this method by the reaction.\nacetic acid with ethanol.\nCH,—C—O3 + |—0—C2H ̧\nacetic acid\nethyl alcohol\nH+\nCH-C-OCH, +\nethyl acetate\nsters can also be prepared by the reaction of an alcohol with acid halide or acid nhydride (alcoholysis of acid halide or anhydride). In case of conversion of acid halide\nester, pyridine base is used to consume the HCl formed in reaction.\nCH -C—C + CH-O-H\nacetyl chloride\n0\nethyl alcohol\n-C-0-C-CI, + C2H2-0-\nacetic anhydride\nethyl alcohol\npyridine\n0\nCH-C-OC2H, + HC",
