        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 7",
        "theory": "NaOH\nΔ\nC2H, + Na2CO,\nsodium propionate\nethane\n20.7.4 Reactions of Carboxylic Acid Derivatives\n20.7.4.1 Reactions of Acyl Halides\nAcid halides are the most reactive of all derivatives of carboxylic acid.\n(i)\nFriedel-Crafts Acylation Reaction\nAcid halides react with benzene in the presence of Lewis acid to form aromatic ketones. (Section 16.8.5.2.6).\n20.7.4.2 Reactions of Acid Anhydrides\nAcid Anhydrides are more reactive than esters but less reactive than acid halides,\ntoward nucleophiles.\n(i) Hydrolysis\nOn hydrolysis, anhydrides form corresponding carboxylic acids. The hydrolysis of anhydrides can occurs in the presence of acid or base catalyst, though uncatalyzed\nhydrolysis can also occurs readily.\n-0-C-R&#x27;+ H-OH-\nanhydride\nR-C-OH + R-C-OH\ncarboxylic acids\n263\n20.7.4.3 Reactions of Esters\nFollowing are some of the important reactions of esters.\n(i) Hydrolysis\nNOT FOR SALE\nEsters are commonly hydrolyzed to carboxylic acid and alcohol, on heating with water very slowly. However the hydrolysis of ester can be catalyzed by an acid or\nbase.\nThe acid-catalyzed hydrolysis is reversible, and is the reverse of acid-catalyzed\nesterification (Section 18.1.7.4 and 20.7.1.3).\n0\nH+\nR-C-OR + H-OH\ncarboxylic acid\nR-C-OH + R-OH\nester\nalcohol\nThe alkaline hydrolysis will yield a salt of carboxylic acid and alcohol. This reaction is also called saponification reaction, because such type of reaction is used for preparation of soap from fats.\n0\nH2O\nR-C-OR + NaOH\nester\nR-C-ONa + R-OH sodium carboxylate\nalcohol\n(ii)\nReduction of Esters\nEster can be reduced to alcohols in the presence of strong reducing agent such as lithium aluminum hydride in ether (Section 18.1.5.6).\nReaction of Ester with Grignard Reagent\nEsters react with two equivalents of Grignard reagent to give an alcohol (Section 17.2.3.2).\n20.7.4.4 Reactions of Amides\nwa\nAmides are the least reactive of the common carboxylic acid derivatives. Like other acid derivatives, amides can also reacts with nucleophiles like water.\n() Hydrolysis\nAmides on hydrolysis form the corresponding carboxylic acids and ammonia. The reaction is very slow and requires the use of strong acid or base as catalyst. Both are irreversible and in case of base catalyst, salt of carboxylic acid is obtained.\nR-C-NH, H-OH\namide\nH&quot; or &quot;OH heat\n264\nR-C-OH + NH,\ncarboxylic acid\n(ii) Reduction\nNOT FOR SALE\nAmides can be reduced to primary amines in presence of lithium aluminium hydride\n(Section 17.3.5.2.3).\n။\n1. LIAIH,/ether\nR-C-NH2\n2. H2O\nprimary amide\nR-CH2-NH2\n20.7.4.5 Reactions of Nitriles\nprimary amine\nAlkyl nitriles or simply nitriles are also considered as derivatives of carboxylic acids. The following are some of the important reactions of nitriles.\n(i)\nHydrolysis\nHydrolysis of nitriles with aqueous acid or base yields a carboxylic acid. In the presence of base catalyst, the final product is actually salt of carboxylic acid (Section 20.5.2). (ii) Reduction\nNitriles yield primary amines when reduced with catalytic method, or using lithium aluminium hydride in ether (Section 17.3.5.2.1).\n(iii) Reaction with Grignard Reagent\nNitriles on reaction with Grignard reagent produce ketones. The strongly nucleophilic Grignard reagent add to carbon-nitrogen triple bond in a similar fashion to that seen in aldehydes and ketones. The reaction proceeds via an imine salt intermediate, which is further hydrolysed to give ketones.\nR-MgXR-CEN-",
        "content": "NaOH<br>Δ<br>C2H, + Na2CO,<br>sodium propionate<br>ethane<br>20.7.4 Reactions of Carboxylic Acid Derivatives<br>20.7.4.1 Reactions of Acyl Halides<br>Acid halides are the most reactive of all derivatives of carboxylic acid.<br>(i)<br>Friedel-Crafts Acylation Reaction<br>Acid halides react with benzene in the presence of Lewis acid to form aromatic ketones. (Section 16.8.5.2.6).<br>20.7.4.2 Reactions of Acid Anhydrides<br>Acid Anhydrides are more reactive than esters but less reactive than acid halides,<br>toward nucleophiles.<br>(i) Hydrolysis<br>On hydrolysis, anhydrides form corresponding carboxylic acids. The hydrolysis of anhydrides can occurs in the presence of acid or base catalyst, though uncatalyzed<br>hydrolysis can also occurs readily.<br>-0-C-R&#x27;+ H-OH-<br>anhydride<br>R-C-OH + R-C-OH<br>carboxylic acids<br>263<br>20.7.4.3 Reactions of Esters<br>Following are some of the important reactions of esters.<br>(i) Hydrolysis<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Esters are commonly hydrolyzed to carboxylic acid and alcohol, on heating with water very slowly. However the hydrolysis of ester can be catalyzed by an acid or<br>base.<br>The acid-catalyzed hydrolysis is reversible, and is the reverse of acid-catalyzed<br>esterification (Section 18.1.7.4 and 20.7.1.3).<br>0<br>H+<br>R-C-OR + H-OH<br>carboxylic acid<br>R-C-OH + R-OH<br>ester<br>alcohol<br>The alkaline hydrolysis will yield a salt of carboxylic acid and alcohol. This reaction is also called saponification reaction, because such type of reaction is used for preparation of soap from fats.<br>0<br>H2O<br>R-C-OR + NaOH<br>ester<br>R-C-ONa + R-OH sodium carboxylate<br>alcohol<br>(ii)<br>Reduction of Esters<br>Ester can be reduced to alcohols in the presence of strong reducing agent such as lithium aluminum hydride in ether (Section 18.1.5.6).<br>Reaction of Ester with Grignard Reagent<br>Esters react with two equivalents of Grignard reagent to give an alcohol (Section 17.2.3.2).<br>20.7.4.4 Reactions of Amides<br>wa<br>Amides are the least reactive of the common carboxylic acid derivatives. Like other acid derivatives, amides can also reacts with nucleophiles like water.<br>() Hydrolysis<br>Amides on hydrolysis form the corresponding carboxylic acids and ammonia. The reaction is very slow and requires the use of strong acid or base as catalyst. Both are irreversible and in case of base catalyst, salt of carboxylic acid is obtained.<br>R-C-NH, H-OH<br>amide<br>H&quot; or &quot;OH heat<br>264<br>R-C-OH + NH,<br>carboxylic acid<br>(ii) Reduction<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>Amides can be reduced to primary amines in presence of lithium aluminium hydride<br>(Section 17.3.5.2.3).<br>။<br>1. LIAIH,/ether<br>R-C-NH2<br>2. H2O<br>primary amide<br>R-CH2-NH2<br>20.7.4.5 Reactions of Nitriles<br>primary amine<br>Alkyl nitriles or simply nitriles are also considered as derivatives of carboxylic acids. The following are some of the important reactions of nitriles.<br>(i)<br>Hydrolysis<br>Hydrolysis of nitriles with aqueous acid or base yields a carboxylic acid. In the presence of base catalyst, the final product is actually salt of carboxylic acid (Section 20.5.2). (ii) Reduction<br>Nitriles yield primary amines when reduced with catalytic method, or using lithium aluminium hydride in ether (Section 17.3.5.2.1).<br>(iii) Reaction with Grignard Reagent<br>Nitriles on reaction with Grignard reagent produce ketones. The strongly nucleophilic Grignard reagent add to carbon-nitrogen triple bond in a similar fashion to that seen in aldehydes and ketones. The reaction proceeds via an imine salt intermediate, which is further hydrolysed to give ketones.<br>R-MgXR-CEN-",
