        "sciNote": "279\n21.2.1\nNOT FOR SALE\nSome proteins also contain smaller quantities of sulphur and phosphorous. There are about 20 different amino acids, which combine in different sequence a different numbers and produce an infinite number of different types of proteins. The bacterium, Escherichia coli (E. coli) has been estimated to contain about 300 different types of proteins. Similarly, human body contains about 100,000 differe kinds of proteins. However, none of the proteins of E. coli is identical with any\nthe human proteins.\nClassification\nProteins have been classified into the following three major classes.\n1. Simple Proteins\n3. Derived Proteins\n1. Simple Proteins\n2. Compounds or Conjugated Proteins\nSimple proteins are made up of amino acid subunits joined together by peptide bond When hydrolysed by enzymes, simple proteins yield only the amino acids and the derivatives from which they are formed, examples of simple proteins include albumin globulins, collagens, histones, glutelins, albuminoids etc. 2. Compounds or Conjugated Proteins. Conjugated proteins are complex proteins composed of simple proteins and a non-protein substance referred to as a prosthetic group or\ncofactor.\nTidbit Albumin\nA class of simple, sulphur-containing water-soluble proteins that coagulate when heated; occurring in egg white milk, blood, and other animal and\nvegetable tissues and secretions.\nMost prosthetic groups or cofactors are small organic molecules formed from vitamins. When hydrolysed by enzymes, ",
