        "theory": "Both cellulose and starch are naturally occurring polysaccharides that are very prevalent in the world of plants.\nThe paper is mainly cellulose, as is the cotton in clothing fabrics and the wood used home construction.\nStarch is a component of many types of foods, including bread, potatoes, rice, com, beans and peas.\nAcid\nn(C,H,O) + nH,0\nn C, H120%\nsucrose\nglucose\nWaze i\n21.1.2\nFunctions\nBasic function of carbohydrates is the production of energy for the performance of vital activities in living organisms. They are also known as &#x27;fuel of life&#x27; because they produce energy by the process of oxidation.\nCarbohydrate + O2 (Oxidation)\nCO2+ H2O + Energy\nThese are chief sources of energy, therefore, they spare proteins and lipids for other important functions of the body. Some important functions are given below.\nGlucose is used as an immediate source of energy for the sick and sportsmen. It is\nalso used in the manufacture of jams and sweets.\n276\nNOT FOR SALE\nFructose is used as a sweetening agent in confectionary, in medicinal syrup. It is also used to prevent sandiness in ice-creams. It is also used as a substitute of table sugar (sucrose) for the obese and the diabetic. Sucrose is used as a food and as ingredient of jams, jellies, confectionaries, syrup. It is also used in the preparation of sucrose octaacetate, which is used for the denaturation of alcohols and for making anhydrous adhesives.\nStarch is principally used as a food. It is industrially used for manufacturing of ethanui by the process of fermentation. It is also used as a stiffening agent in textile industry and in laundry. It is also used as an adhesive to fasten paper.\nCellulose has no food value but is used as a roughage in our diet for promoting the peristaltic motion of digestive tract. It is used for the manufacturing of\npaper.\nOligosaccharides are involved in the formation of secreted proteins like antibodies\nand blood clotting factors.\nThe receptors on the cell membrane are the complexes of carbohydrates with certain proteins. The receptors are involved in molecular targeting or molecular recognition.\nTidbit\nAdenosine Triphosphate\n21.1.3 Nutritional Importance Carbohydrates are the most important energy containing nutrients for living organisms. Plant Adenosine triphosphate, also known as contains stored carbohydrates called starch ATP, is a molecule that carries energy while animals contain stored carbohydrates within cells. It is the main energy currency called glycogen. Both, these polymers are of the cell and it is an end product of broken down into the simplest monosaccharide the processes of photophosphorylation units known as glucose, before oxidation. Then (adding a phosphate group to a molecule glucose is oxidized in the body cells by respiration and fermentation. All living using energy from light), cellular different types of pathways (series of things use ATP. It is also used in signal enzymatic chemical reactions) to release convert pathways for cell communication energy. This energy is stored in the form of and is incorporated into deoxyribonucleic molecules known as ATP (adenosine acid (DNA) during DNA synthesis. triphosphate) in the different parts of the body.\nCH120% + 60,\n12\n6CO2+ 6H2O + Energy\n(ATP)\nOne mole of glucose on complete oxidation by citric acid cycle produces 36 ATPs.\n277\nRole of Various Carbohydrates in Health and Disease\nThere are different types of carbohydrates, which are involved in the health and diseas\nof living beings. Some of these are given below.\n1.\nSucrose\nTable sugar or cane sugar is the generally available form of sucrose. Sucrose is disaccharide. Sucrose is a combination of fructose (fruit sugar) and glucose (simp plant sugar).Sucrose is a popular sweeting agent. It is used in healthy sweets, so drinks and energy drinks. Sucrose is a caloric carbohydrate and thus is efficient t provide energy. 1 gram of sucrose produces 4 kilo calories energy when metabolise\nExcess of sucrose may cause some problems such as,\nTooth decay\nObesity\nPlaque formation upon our teeth\nGum diseases\n2. Lactose\nLactose is the disaccharide naturally found in milk. It is composed of glucose and galactose. Human milk contains 7.2% lactose while cow milk contains about 4.8% lactose Milk is a universal food of new born mammals and one of the most complete human diets. Although glucose could be found in several types of foods, lactose is the only source of galactose.\nSome of the important benefits of lactose (galactose) are,\nGalactose has various biological functions and serves in neural and immunological\nGalactose is a component of several macromolecules, which are important constituents of nerve cells membrane.\npresse\nGalactose is also a component of the molecules present on blood cells\ndetermine the ABO blood types.\nAccording to recent studies, lactose may play a role in the absorption of calcium and other minerals such as copper and zinc, especially during infancy.\nThe main disadvantage associated with lactose (glactose) is that many human adults are unable to digest milk due to deficiency of lactase, (Lactase is an enzyme, splits lactose into glucose and galactose) which is called lactose-intolerance. Lactose intolerance is the inability to digest lactose that results in intestinal discomfort such as, cramps, flatulence, colic pains, abnormal intestinal flow, nausea and watery diarrhoea. These symptoms appear within 30-90 minutes after the ingestion of milk. It is important to remind that lactose\n278\nNOT FOR SALE\nintolerance is not a disease but a condition. To avoid this, fermented milk products such as\nyogurt and cheese should be used.\nJ.\ncan\nGlucose\nbe\nGlucose is a simple carbohydrate. It is the most common and popular monosaccharide that found in sweet fruits such as grapes, which contains 20-30% glucose. The carbohydrates you eat are converted to glucose in the body. The concentration of glucose in the blood, referred to as the blood glucose level, is vital to the proper functioning of the Some of the benefits and uses of glucose are,\nhuman body.\nIt is mainly used by the living bodies for the production of instant energy.\nIt is an important constituent of human blood, human blood contains normally\nabout 70-110mg of glucose per 100cm3.\nIt also regulates the temperature of the body.\nThe surplus glucose is converted to a polymer called glycogen in human beings. Glycogen is then stored in the liver and muscles. When the body is deficient in glucose, this liver and muscle glycogen is hydrolysed to glucose according to the body requirement.\nThe disadvantages related to the extra intake of glucose is,\n21.2\nHuman pancreas secretes a hormone known as insulin. Insulin helps in the metabolism of glucose to produce energy. In some cases, defects in the metabolism of glucose occurs due to which blood glucose level rise from the normal level. This condition results in a disease known as diabetes mellitus. The consequences of unchecked diabetes include hardening of blood vessels, dysfunction of kidney, diabetic coma that causes premature death.\nProteins\nThe term protein is derived from the Greek word proteios, which means &quot;first, prime or chief&quot;. Proteins are naturally occurring polymers composed of amino acid units joined to one another by amide (or peptide) bonds. Spider webs, animal hair and muscle, egg whites and haemoglobin (the molecule that transports oxygen in the body to where it is needed) are all proteins. They are present in all animals, plants, bacteria and viruses.\n◆ Chemically, proteins are complex nitrogenous organic compounds, which are\nassociated with the concept of life itself.\nBy chemical composition, proteins are composed of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen. Percentage of nitrogen is fairly constant and is about 16% of the molecular\nweight of protein.",
