        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 3",
        "theory": "279\n21.2.1\nNOT FOR SALE\nSome proteins also contain smaller quantities of sulphur and phosphorous. There are about 20 different amino acids, which combine in different sequence a different numbers and produce an infinite number of different types of proteins. The bacterium, Escherichia coli (E. coli) has been estimated to contain about 300 different types of proteins. Similarly, human body contains about 100,000 differe kinds of proteins. However, none of the proteins of E. coli is identical with any\nthe human proteins.\nClassification\nProteins have been classified into the following three major classes.\n1. Simple Proteins\n3. Derived Proteins\n1. Simple Proteins\n2. Compounds or Conjugated Proteins\nSimple proteins are made up of amino acid subunits joined together by peptide bond When hydrolysed by enzymes, simple proteins yield only the amino acids and the derivatives from which they are formed, examples of simple proteins include albumin globulins, collagens, histones, glutelins, albuminoids etc. 2. Compounds or Conjugated Proteins. Conjugated proteins are complex proteins composed of simple proteins and a non-protein substance referred to as a prosthetic group or\ncofactor.\nTidbit Albumin\nA class of simple, sulphur-containing water-soluble proteins that coagulate when heated; occurring in egg white milk, blood, and other animal and\nvegetable tissues and secretions.\nMost prosthetic groups or cofactors are small organic molecules formed from vitamins. When hydrolysed by enzymes, conjugated proteins yield the amino acids from which they are comprised of and some other chemical componer Examples of conjugated proteins include nucleoproteins, glycoproteins, phosphoprotein and haemoglobins.\nTable 21.1 Different Types of Conjugated Proteins and Prosthetic Group\nS. No.\nConjugated Protein\n1.\nNucleoproteins\n2.\nPhosphoproteins\n3.\n4.\n5.\nLipoproteins Glycoproteins\nChromoprotein\n280\nProsthetic Group\nDNA or RNA\nPhosphoric acid\nLipids\nCarbohydrates Coloured compounds\n¥\n3. Derived Proteins\nNOT FOR SALE\nThese are proteins derived by partial to complete hydrolysis of the simple or conjugated proteins by the action of acids, alkalies or enzymes. They may be proteins, meta-proteins, conjugated proteins etc. They include two types of derivatives, primary derived proteins\nand secondary derived proteins.\nPrimary derived proteins: They are also called as denatured proteins, in which some or all cross linkages which normally keep the molecular structure are broken. Denaturing agents may be physical or chemical such as vigorous shaking, heat,\nlight, low pH etc.\nSecondary derived proteins: These are formed by hydrolysis of simple or conjugated proteins by acid or enzymes e.g. proteoses, peptones, etc.\n21.2.2\nStructure\nProteins are macromolecules having very complicated structures. Biochemists divided protein structures into four levels, to make it easier and to handle the complexity of the overall structures. In short, all the proteins appear in any of the following four different\nstructures.\n1. Primary Structure\nThis structure shows simply the sequence and number of different amino acids in a polypeptide chain. For example, the hormone insulin has two polypeptide chains, A and B. It also shows whether the polypeptide chain is open, branched or cyclic.\n2. Secondary Structure\nX-rays diffraction experiments have shown that long running polypeptide chains tend to twist or coil upon themselves in a special pattern. Secondary structure show this folding of the polypeptide chains to form a specific coiled structure held together by strong hydrogen bonding. Secondary structure may assume any of the following two different forms.\na. Alpha-helix form\n3. Tertiary Structure\nb. B-pleated sheets\nThe long polypeptide chain of proteins molecules undergoes folding and re-folding on itself and give rise to a definite three dimensional structure. This is called tertiary structure. This structure makes proteins rounded and somewhat rigid molecules.\n4. Quaternary Structure\nThis structure shows the association of many individual protein sub-units, each with its own tertiary structure into a complex functional unit. Examples are myoglobin (oxygen storage) and haemoglobin (oxygen carrier).\n281\n3. Derived Proteins\nNOT FOR SALE\nThese are proteins derived by partial to complete hydrolysis of the simple or conjugated proteins by the action of acids, alkalies or enzymes. They may be proteins, meta-proteins, conjugated proteins etc. They include two types of derivatives, primary derived proteins\nand secondary derived proteins.\nPrimary derived proteins: They are also called as denatured proteins, in which some or all cross linkages which normally keep the molecular structure are broken. Denaturing agents may be physical or chemical such as vigorous shaking, heat,\nlight, low pH etc.\nSecondary derived proteins: These are formed by hydrolysis of simple or conjugated proteins by acid or enzymes e.g. proteoses, peptones, etc.\n21.2.2\nStructure\nProteins are macromolecules having very complicated structures. Biochemists divided protein structures into four levels, to make it easier and to handle the complexity of the overall structures. In short, all the proteins appear in any of the following four different\nstructures.\n1. Primary Structure\nThis structure shows simply the sequence and number of different amino acids in a polypeptide chain. For example, the hormone insulin has two polypeptide chains, A and B. It also shows whether the polypeptide chain is open, branched or cyclic.\n2. Secondary Structure\nX-rays diffraction experiments have shown that long running polypeptide chains tend to twist or coil upon themselves in a special pattern. Secondary structure show this folding of the polypeptide chains to form a specific coiled structure held together by strong hydrogen bonding. Secondary structure may assume any of the following two different forms.\na. Alpha-helix form\n3. Tertiary Structure\nb. B-pleated sheets\nThe long polypeptide chain of proteins molecules undergoes folding and re-folding on itself and give rise to a definite three dimensional structure. This is called tertiary structure. This structure makes proteins rounded and somewhat rigid molecules.\n4. Quaternary Structure\nThis structure shows the association of many individual protein sub-units, each with its own tertiary structure into a complex functional unit. Examples are myoglobin (oxygen storage) and haemoglobin (oxygen carrier).\n281",
