        "sciNote": "Difference between DNA and RNA\nDNA and RNA differs in the following points from each other.\nComparison\nDNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)\nDNA is found in the nucleus,\nLocation\nStructure\nLength\nRNA(Ribonucleic Acid)\nRNA is formed in the nucleolus and then moves to specialized regions of\nwhile a small amount of DNA also the cytoplasm depending on the type\npresent in mitochondria.\nDNA consists of two strands, arranged in a double helix. These strands are made up of subunits called nucleotides.\nDNA is a much longer polymer than RNA. A chromosome, for example, is a single, long DNA\nof RNA formed.\nRNA has only one strand, but like DNA, is made up of nucleotides.\nRNA molecules are variable in\nmolecule, which would be several length, but much shorter than long\ncentimetres in length when\nunravelled.\nDNA polymers.\nSugar\nThe sugar in DNA is deoxyribose.\nRNA contains ribose sugar.\n299\n*\nBases\nBase Pairs\nFunction\nThe bases in DNA are Adenine (A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C) and Thymine (T).\nAdenine and Thymine pair (A-T) Cytosine and Guanine pair (C-G)\nDNA replicates and stores genetic\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe bases in RNA are Adenine (A), Guanine (G) and Cytosine (C) like DNA, but contains Uracil (U) rather than Thymine.\nAdenine and Uracil pair (A-U) Cytosine and Guanine pair (C-G)\nRNA converts the genetic\ninformation. It is a blueprint for all information contained within DNA\ngenetic information contained. within an organism\nto a format used to synthesize proteins and then moves it to ribosomal protein "
