        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 13",
        "theory": "Sources of Calcium\nHigh amount of calcium is found in dairy products such as milk and yogurt. On average 250mL of cows&#x27; milk or 150g yogurt contains 300mg of calcium.\nThe main sources of calcium are milk and milk products, egg yolk, legumes, green leafy vegetables, almonds, hazelnuts, broccoli, okra, spinach, apricot, figs, pulses, sesame seeds.\nDeficiency of Calcium\nila\nDecrease of calcium in the diet is contributing to a factor known as osteoporosis, a condition that causes brittle bones in adults.\nDecrease of calcium level in plasma leads to a condition known as hypocalcemia. Excess of Calcium\nIncrease in the concentration of calcium in plasma leads to a condition known as hyperparathyroidism.\niii. Phosphorus\nPhosphorous is also the major constituent of bones and teeth. It is present in all the body cells in association with proteins, lipids and carbohydrates in the form of phosphoproteins, phospholipids and similar compounds.\nThe major function of phosphorus is in the formation of the bone component hydroxyapatite with calcium. 80% of the phosphorous in the body is present as calcium salts in the skeleton and, therefore, is essential for healthy bone and tooth structure. It has à unique role in the storage and transformation of energy in the body.\nDaily Requirement\nAn adult requires about 800mg per day of phosphorous.\nSources of Phosphorous\nMost foods contain phosphorus. It is present in association with calcium in its sources.\n303\nNOT FOR SALE\nFoods that are rich in protein are also excellent sources of phosphorus. These\ninclude meat and poultry, fish, eggs, milk and other dairy products.\nSome non-protein food sources also contain phosphorus. For example, whole\ngrains, potatoes, garlic, dry fruits and carbonated drinks\nDeficiency of Phosphorous\nDeficiency of phosphorous in the body leads to various problems such as, joint or bone pain, loss of appetite, irritability or anxiety, fatigue and poor bone\ndevelopment in children.\nDeficiency of phosphorous in children leads to renal rickets. Excess of Phosphorous\nToo much phosphate can be toxic.\niv.\nAn excess can cause diarrhoea, as well as a hardening of organs and soft tissues. An increase in the plasma phosphate level due to decrease in its excretion leads to kidney dysfunction.\nZinc\nZinc is antioxidant and important for the maintenance of a healthy immune system. Zinc is an important element for the normal growth, reproduction and longevity of animals. Daily requirement\nThe recommended daily amount of zinc for an adult is 10 mg.\nIt is a constituent of several types of enzymes such as alkaline phosphatase, carbonic anhydrase etc.\nIt also forms a complex with insulin and helps in its storage and release according to the needs of the body. It is also required for maintaining the plasma concentration of vitamin A.\nSources of Zine\nThe different sources of zinc include meat, liver, eggs, fish, milk and cereals.\nDeficiency of Zine\ne\nrment Deficiency of zinc results in slow healing of wounds, impairment of acuity of taste and skin problems.\nExcess of Zinc\nExcess intake of zinc can cause gastrointestinal discomfort and when chronic, may disrupt the balance of other chemicals in the body, including copper and iron.\n304\nKey Points\nBiochemistry studies the chemistry of life.\nNOT FOR SALE\nCarbohydrates, proteins, lipids, nucleic acid are called biomolecules. Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes (aldoses) and polyhydroxy ketones (ketoses) or compounds formed by linking aldoses and ketoses.\nGlucose is known as &quot;aldose&quot; and has an aldehyde structure at carbon atom number\none.\nFructose is known as &quot;ketose&quot; and has a keto structure at carbon atom number two. The main classes of carbohydrates are monosaccharide, oligosaccharide and\npolysaccharide.\nMonosaccharides and oligosaccharides are collectively called sugars. Polysaccharides are non-sugars.\nWhen two monosaccharide units combine together by an oxygen atom with elimination of one water molecule, this type of linkage is called glycosidic linkage or glycosidic bond.\nAmino acids are monomers of proteins and they are connected by peptide bond in proteins.\nThe structure of protein is considered at four levels, primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary.\nLipids are heterogeneous class of organic compounds. They may be saponifiable or non-saponifiable.\nThe oils, which contain more unsaturation, are hydrogenated to produce solid ghee. DNA and RNA are two examples of nucleic acid and their function is replication and protein synthesis.\nThe building blocks of nucleic acids are nucleotides.\nThe three components of a deoxyribonucleotide are a five-carbon sugar called deoxyribose, a phosphate group and a nitrogenous base.\nEnzymes are protein in nature, which can speed up a chemical reaction. Enzyme inhibition may be irreversible or reversible.\nMost animal enzyme&#x27;s optimum temperature is body temperature (37°C). A pH, at which an enzyme shows the maximum activity, is called optimum pH.\n305\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe substances, which decrease the activity of an enzyme, known as enzyme\ninhibitor and this process is known as enzyme inhibition.\nLipids have three major subclasses: simple lipids, compound lipids and derived\nlipids.\nThe monomer that makes up a nucleic acid is called a nucleotide.\nNucleotide has three parts: an inorganic phosphate group, a five-carbon monosaccharide sugar, and a nitrogen containing structure known as a nitrogenous\nbase.\nDNA contains genetic information in the form of codes.\nMinerals essential for life are the inorganic nutrients.\nIron is a part of haemoglobin, a carrier of oxygen in the body. Calcium is the main constituent of bones and teeth. Living organism cannot synthesized the essential minerals.\nExercise\nQI. Choose the correct one from the given options.\n1. The optimum pH of pepsin is around\na. 3.0\nc. 2.0\nd. 2.5\n2. The most abundant carbohydrate found in nature is\na. cellulose\nb. 3.5",
