        "def": "chemical interactions of these substances with one another. Living organisms consist Biochemistry is the study of the chemical substances found in living organisms and the primarily of the elements carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen. Many other elements such as sulphur and phosphorus are essential to life but they do not comprise a large portion\nElements combine to form the molecules that make up living cells. The substances that are associated with life are called biochemical substances. Biochemical substances are divided into two groups: Figure 21.1 gives an approximate mass composition for the human body in terms of types of biochemical substances present.\nthe concepts of biochemistry, biomolecules their Bioorganic molecules make up only about\none-fourth of body mass. The bioinorganic substance (water) constitutes over two- third of the mass of the human body, and another 4%-5% of body mass comes from inorganic salts."
