        "title": "22.4 Pesticides",
        "theory": "are also some of the major by-products.\nBreaking of larger, stable molecules requires catalysis. Refinery products such as gas oil and fuel oil is passed through catalytic cracker to obtain gasoline and kerosene oil as major products and by-product, propene.\n317\nII. Reforming Process\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe process of reforming of hydrocarbon is carried out by using heat, pressure and/or catalyst in order to obtain desired products. In this process, heavy naphtha is converted\ninto aromatics such as benzene, toluene and xylene as well as hydrogen as\nby-product\nReforming process breaks down the molecules of low octane rating naphtha into high octane gasoline blending components (reformates). It is one of the most important processes\nin oil refineries.\nFractional Distillation of Petroleum\nPetroleum in the unrefined form is called crude oil. It is a naturally occurring thick viscous brown or greenish black liquid, which is obtained from earth crust. The crude oil consists of different substances. These substances in crude oil are miscible. Therefore they do not separate out into layers. Thus, fractional distillation, which is carried out in the fractionating towers, is used in refining petroleum. Fractional distillation is the process of heating up a mixture containing different substances with different boiling points and after condensation drawing the different fractions off. Each fraction is not pure compound but is a mixture of different hydrocarbons with similar boiling points may b Crude oil is first treated to remove the sulphur or sulphur compounds that present. The cleaned hydrocarbon material is then distilled and fractions with variou boiling ranges are collected. (Figure 22.1).\nCOOL\n(25°C)\nRefinery Gas &lt; 20°C\nCrude Oil\nHHHHHHH\nPetroleum Ether 20°C-60°C\nNaphtha 60°C-100°C\nGasoline (Petrol) 80°C-180°C\nKerosine 160°C-300°C\nDiesel Oil\n250°C-350°C\nLubricating Oil 300°C-370°C Fuel Oil\n400°C-600°C\nResidue\n&gt; 600°C\nFurnace\nFigure 22.1 Fractional distillation column.\n318\ne in\nNOT FOR SALE\nThe main goal of refinery operations is the production of gasoline. The principal fractions obtained during fractional distillation are given in Table 22.3. Table 22.3 Fractions of Petroleum and their Important Uses\nNumber of carbon\nS.No.\nFraction\nBoiling\natoms per molecule\npoint\n(°C)\nImportant uses\n1\nRefinery gas\nC to C1\nbelow\nuse as a fuel\n20\nmaking other organic\ncompounds\nPetroleum\n2\nether\nCs-C2\n20-60\nuse as a solvent\nuse as a solvent\nLight\n3\nC-C,\n60-100\nnaphtha\nuse as a raw material for\nmaking other petrochemicals\nGasoline\nC1-C10\n10\n80-180\nuse as a motor fuel\nuse as a jet fuel\nKerosene\n5\nC11-C15\n(Paraffin oil)\nheating\nHeavy oil\n(Diesel oil,\nC15-C18\n250-350\ngas oil)\n7",
