        "theory": "Lubricating\noil\nC18-C50 300-370\n8\nFuel oil\nC - C\n400-600\nuse as fuel for ships and factories use as industrial fuel\n160-300 use for oil fired domestic\nuse as a fuel for diesel engines\n℗ making greases, vaseline,\ncandles etc\nuse for lubricating heavy machinery\n9\nResidue\nMore than\nabove\nmetalling roads\n(Solid mass)\nСто\n600\n319\n22.6\nNOT FOR SAL\nSynthetic Polymers (PVC and Nylon)\nA polymer is a macromolecule (sometimes with a very high molecular weight) formed as a result of\nTidbit\na process known as polymerization, whereby Difference between Polymer\nsmall organic molecules combine together to form large molecules (polymers).\nThe small molecules, which undergo polymerization, are called &#x27;the monomers&#x27;. Thus, a polymer is a large molecule built up from many hundreds or thousands of monomer units joined together.\nA synthetic compound is man-made and produced\nas a result of chemical reactions. A synthetic polymer is a man-made macromolecule that is made of thousands of repeating units.\nand Macromolecules Polymers are also calle macromolecules due to their larg size but converse is not alway true. A macromolecule may may not contain monomer unit e.g., chlorophyll (C,H2O,N‚Mg is a macromolecule but not\npolymer since there are m\nmonomer units present so we ca conclude that all polymers an macromolecules while macromolecules may not B polymers in nature.\nThe well-known synthetic polymer is plastic polyethylene or polythene which is composed of large molecules formed by the repeated combination of ethene molecules. Polyn are classified as either &#x27;addition polymers&#x27; or &#x27;condensation polymers&#x27; depending\ntheir method of formation.\n1. Addition Polymers\nThe polymers formed by the polymerization of monomers containing double or tri bonds (unsaturated compounds) are called addition polymers. Thus, addition polyn results from the self- combination of many monomer units into a substance wit molecular weight which is a multiple of the monomer.\nH\nH\na\nΠ\nH\nH\nethene\nH\nH\nC-C\nH H\nΠ\npolyethene\nAddition polymers have the same empirical formula as their monomers. For exam\nwhen ethene is heated under pressure, a transparent solid polymer, polyethen\nobtained.\n320\nPreparation of Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC)\nmedical devices, etc.\nNOT FOR SALE\nconstruction industry, pipes (drinking and wastewater), wire and cable insulation, Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is thermoplastic polymer widely used in building and Vinyl chloride monomer (VCM) is the initiator of PVC. It is commercially produced by treating acetylene with HCI, as a result it forms an addition product called vinyl\nchloride in the presence of HgCl2 or CuCl.\nH-C=C-H+ CHCI\nacetylene\nHgCl,\nCuCl\nHC=CH-CI\nvinyl chloride\nAt 70-80°C vinyl chloride monomers polymerize and polyvinyl chloride of high\nmolecular weight is produced.\nC\nПCH2=CHCl\nCH2-CH\nCI\npolyvinyl chloride\nvinyl chloride\n1. Condensation Polymers\nThe polymers which are formed by the combination of two or more monomers with the elimination of small molecules such as water, hydrogen chloride (HCI), ammonia (NH,) or methanol (CH, OH) etc., are known as condensation polymers. If the carbon-chain back bone has two functional group attached to condensation reaction can occur involving polymerization and the polymer produced in such a case is called condensation polymer. e.g., nylon 6, 6 (six carbon alom in each monomer) is formed by the condensation of hexamethylene diamine with adipic acid (hexanedioic acid).\nnHO-C-(CH,),-C-OH+ nH,N–(CH,),NH,\nadipic acid\nhexamethylene diamine",
