      "Auxochromes are commonly called as 'colour helpers' or 'colour intensifiers\". The auxochrome, the part of the dye which causes it to adhere to the material which it colours (usually textiles) are -NH,, -OH, NR,,-COOH and SO,H of these groups. -NH, and - NR, cause solubility in acids while - OH, -COOH and -SO,H cause solubility in basic solutions.",
      "Classification of Dye: There are several ways for classification of dyes. One of the prominent ways of classification is the classification based on the source of materials. It is further divided into two main types:",
      "i. Natural Dyes ii. Synthetic Dyes",
      "i. Natural Dye",
      "aw on the",
      "Natural dyes are dyes or colorants derived from plants, invertebrates, or minerals. The majority of natural dyes are vegetable dyes from plant sources e.g. roots, berries, bark, leaves and wood. Other organic sources include fungi and lichens.",
      "ii. Synthetic Dyes",
      "Almost all the colours in use today are synthetic dyes. Synthetic dyes are used everywhere in everything from clothes to paper, from food to wood. This is because they are cheaper to produce, brighter, more colour-fast and easy to apply to fabric.",
      "For example, acid dyes, azo dyes, basic dyes, mordant dyes, etc.",
      "314",
      "Tidbit",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Differences between dyes and pigments Dyes and pigments are substances that impart colour to a material. The major difference between dyes and pigments is the particle size. Dyes are much finer than pigments. Dyes, also known as colourants in which the colouring matter is dissolved in liquid, are absorbed into the material to which they are applied.",
      "Pigments, on the other hand, consist of extremely fine particles of ground colouring matter suspended in liquid which forms a paint like film that actually bonds to the surface.",
      "22.4 Pesticides",
      "Pesticides are chemicals that are used to destroy, repel or otherwise lower pest infestations to protect crops from damage or simply we can say a substance that kills a pest (insect, weed, bacteria, nematode etc.), for example lead arsenate, Pb,(ASO),",
      "nicotine sulphate etc.",
      "Characteristics of an Ideal Pesticide",
      "An ideal pesticide should possess the following characteristics:",
      "Mab",
      "It should be effective only against the specific target organism. It should be non-toxic to other living organisms.",
      "It should be non-persistent.",
      "It should be biodegradable.",
      "It should break down in nature into harmless products and cause no damage to environment.",
      "It should be inexpensive and easily available.",
      "Pesticides are the chemical substances that are used to control the pests such as DDT, heptachlor etc. DDT has the formula,",
      "CI",
      "CI",
      "H",
      "CI",
      "dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT)",
      "DDT acts directly on insects. It is a very stable chemical, it's half-life is estimated to be 10-15 years. DDT is insoluble in water, therefore, it accumulates in animals and in particular in their fatty tissues. Therefore, the environmentalists banned the use of DDT",
      "Nowadays, parathion is used instead of DDT.",
      "in the world.",
      "315",
      "*",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "OCH,",
      "OCH,",
      "NO, Parathion",
      "This compound breaks down fairly readily to products that appear to cause no biological damage. So, it has no long term damages but carefully handling is necessary. In short, the ideal pesticide is clearly not yet available.",
      "22.5",
      "Petrochemicals",
      "used to",
      "Petroleum is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons formed by the decomposition of fossil remains. It exists as a gas (natural gas), liquid (crude oil) or as solids (oil shales) and is found deep underground or below seabed. About 10% of the petroleum prepare a range of chemicals called petrochemicals. Petrochemicals as a group of chemicals produced directly or indirectly from the hydrocarbons of petroleum or natural gas. Examples of petrochemicals are plastics, rubbers, fibres, paints, solvents, and detergents.",
      "Since every area of human activity makes use of petrochemicals or the materials made from them, our life without petrochemicals would be very different and less comfortable. The different petrochemicals are compiled in Table 22.2.",
