        "sciNote": "360\nNOT FOR SALE\nIf the ratio is not in whole numbers then number of moles of each element is multiplied by a suitable small number to get the whole number ratio. > Finally the symbol of each element is written and their number of moles are written as subscripts. This gives the empirical formula of a compound.\nCalculation of Molecular Formula\nMolecular formula shows the actual number of atoms of each kind of element in a compound. Molecular formula is either same to empirical formula or integral multiple\nof the empirical formula.\nwhere\nmolecular formula=x empirical formula\nn=\nmolecular mass\nempirical formula mass\nThe molecular mass can be obtained from mass spectrometric experiment (Section 24.2.2.4) or with the help of colligative properties of solution.\nExample 24.1: Trioxane (1,3,5-trioxane) is a cyclic trimer of formaldehyde, a precursor for the production of polyoxymethylene plastics. 17.471 g of trioxane upon complete combustion produces 10.477 g of water and 25.612 g of carbon dioxide. The molecular mass of trioxane determined by mass spectrometry, is 90.079 a.m.u. Calculate the empirical and molecular formulae of trioxane.\nSolution:\n%C =\n%H=\n%C-25.612g\nX\n17.471g 44.010\nmass of H2O produced\nmass of organic compound 18.0153\nmass of CO, produced mass of organic compound 44.010 25.612g 12.011\n12.011\n-x100\n-x100= 40.008%\n2.0158\nX-\nx100\n%H=\n10.477g 2.0158\nX\n-x100= 6.71%\n17.471g 18.0153\n%O=100-(40.008+6.71)=53.282%\nNow find the moles of each element;\n¥\n40.008\nmoles of C =\n=3",
