        "title": "24.2.2.5 Mass Spectrometry",
        "theory": "20\n30\n40\nm/z\nFigure 24.16 Mass Spectrum of methyl alcohol.\n377\nNOT FOR SALE\nSociety, Technology And Science\nForensic science applies science principles, techniques, and methods to the investigation of crime. Forensic analytical chemistry is defined as analytical chemistry as it pertains to law. Forensic chemists perform specialized analysis to identify materials and learn the nature of such evidences.\nA wide array of laboratory techniques and instrumentation is used in forensic studies. This includes ultraviolet, infrared, and visible spectrophotometry, gas chromatography and mass spectrometry; high pressure liquid chromatography and atomic absorption spectrophotometry. The techniques and instrumentation chosen depend on the type of sample or substance to be examined.\nGas chromatography is most widely used in identification of explosive, accelerator, propellants and drugs or chemicals involved in chemical weapons production. The gas chromatograph (GC) separates volatile substances into separate components by passing the volatile materials through a long absorbent column. In some cases, GC alone is not adequate method for identification such as containments in food and drugs. It is then combined with another method to separate the compound even further and results in greater sensitivity. The GC is often connected to a mass spectrometer. Mass spectrometry (MS) breaks samples apart and separates the ionized fragments by mass and charge. Vast libraries of comparison fragments make computer-aided identification of materials possible even when the sample is very small. Most forensic laboratories have access to a combined gas chromatograph/mass spectrometer (GC/MS). High pressure liquid chromatography (HPLC) separates many types of drugs and may also be combined with MS.\nawaz e\n378\nC\nCCC C\nNOT FOR SALE\nKey Points\nA set of experiments used to know about the quality of a substance is called qualitative analysis.\nMethods used to find the quantities of substances are quantitative analysis. Burning of substances in the presence of oxygen is called combustion. Most organic compounds on burning produces CO, and H,0.\nEmpirical formula shows the simplest ratio of atoms present in a molecule of compound.\nMolecular formula gives the exact number of atoms present in a molecule of a compound.\nTwo or more compounds may have the same molecular formula and are called isomers.\nStructural formula gives the arrangement of atoms in molecule of a compound. Spectroscopy helps us to identify atoms/functional groups in a substance by the interaction of electromagnetic radiation with the substance.\nIn IR spectroscopy organic compounds are exposed to weak radiations in the range of 4000-625 cm-1.\nA 100% transmittance means no absorption.\nIn UV/Visible spectroscopy, the radiations having wavelengths in the range of 200-800 nm are used.\nThese radiations are more energetic and can change the distribution of loosely bound electrons.\nLike electrons, atomic nuclei protons also spin about an axis. Spinning charged bodies produce magnetic field.\nAll nuclei having spin quantum number greater than zero are NMR active.\na\nC C C C C C C\n*\n379\nNOT FOR SALE\nExercise\nQ1. Choose the correct one from the given options.\n1. Which one of the following is quantitative chemical analysis?\na. Salt analysis\nc. Flame test\n2. C,H,O is the molecular formula of\nb. Titration\nd. Bromine-water test\n(i) ethanol\n(ii) methanol\na. (i) and (ii)\nc. (i) and (iii)\n(iii) dimethyl ether\n(iv) diethyl ether\nb.\n(ii) and (iii)\nd.\n(ii) and (v)\n3. Which one of the following techniques is mainly used for determination of\nfunctional group in a compound?\na. IR Spectroscopy\nc. UV/Visible spectroscopy\nb. NMR\nd. Mass spectroscopy\n4. Two different compounds will always give different absorption peak in\nregion of IR.\na. 1500-625 cm-\nc. 3000-2500 cm\n1\nb. 4000-1500 cm\nd. 3500-1500 cm-1\n5. Which one of the following methods, relatively gives more information about the\nstructure of an organic compound?\na. Mass Spectroscopy\nc. IR Spectroscopy\n6. A mass spectrum is shown below.\nRel. Abundance\n100\n80-\n60-\n40-\nb. NMR Spectroscopy\nin\nd. UV-Visible Spectroscopy\naw\n20-",
