        "sciNote": "Amines:-Amines are the organic compounds of nitrogen that may be considered as derived from ammonia (NH,) by replacing one or more of the hydrogen atoms by organic radicals (-CH,, -C,H,) or organic functional groups.\nBeer Lambert's Law: Bear Lambert's Law is the linear relationship between absorbance (A) and concentration (C) of an absorbing species in the solution Le. absorbance is directly proportional to the concentration of the solution and path length (I) of the solution through which the light passes. A = Ecl\nBond Enthalpy: The bond enthalpy of a specific bond is the enthalpy change involved in breaking that covalent bond into individual gaseous atoms at 298 K. It is a measure of bond strength or bond dissociation energy.\nCarbocation:-A group of atoms having positively charged carbon atom which has six electrons in its valence shell is called carbocation. Among the simplest examples are methanium CH3* and ethanium C2Hions.\nCarbonyl group: A carbonyl group is a functional group composed of a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom >C=0.\nCatenation: Catenation is the binding of an element to itself through covalent bonds to form chain or ring molecules.\nCoal: A naturally occurring solid material, of black or brownish colour nom illy occur in rock strata called coal beds consisting mostly of high molecular weight hydrocarbons. Produced by decomposition of prehistoric plant material under high temperature and pressure. Used principally as a fossil fuel.\nComplex Ion: A c",
