        "sciNote": "391\nNOT FOR SALE\nSubstituent group: A substituent is an atom or group of atoms which replaces one or more hydrogen or other atom or group of atoms on the parent molecule.\nSubstrate: It is a molecule or a compound that undergoes a chemical reaction, whether under the action of a catalyst, an enzyme or an inhibitor. It can also refer to a surface on which other chemical reactions are performed.\nSulphydryl or Marcapto: A mercapto group is a functional group containing a sulfur atom bonded to a hydrogen atom. General formula: -SH.\nSynthetic Organic Compound: The compounds which can be synthesized laboratory by chemical manipulations (chemical reactions) from living or non-living\norganisms.\nTautomerism: A type of isomerism wherein the isomers interchange into one another very early to exist together in equilbrim.\nElectronegativity: Electronegativity is a measure of the tendency of an atom to attract a shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond.\nUltra violet visible spectroscopy: An instrumental scanning method that analyses the absorption of light within UV-Visible region.\nUnsaturated Hydrocarbon: Unsaturated hydrocarbons are hydrocarbons that have double or triple covalent bonds between adjacent carbon atoms.\nVital force theory: A theory that an organic molecule cannot be produced from inorganic molecules, but can only be produced from a living organism or some part of a living organism.\nWilliamson Synthesis: A method of synthesizing ethers by reaction of a sodium alkoxide with ",
