        "sciNote": "Where I, is the intensity of the sample beam, and I, is the intensity of the reference beam. In many parts of the spectrum, the transmittance is nearly 100%, meaning that the sample is nearly transparent (no absorbance) to radiation of that\nwavenumber.\nIn using infrared spectroscopy for structure determination, peaks in the range 4000- 1500 cm' are usually emphasized because this is the region in which the vibrations characteristic of particular functional groups are found and therefore called functional. groups region. The region 1500-625 cm' is known as the fingerprint region. It is highly unlikely that the IR spectra of two different compounds (except enantiomers) will show the same frequencies for all of their various complex vibrations. Hence, this region of the infrared spectrum provides a \"fingerprint\" of a molecule. The IR spectra of ethanol\nand toluene are shown in Figure 24.6 and 24.7.\n368\nCH3CH2OH\nMICRONS\nNOT FOR SALE\nO-H\nbeed\nCH, bend\nO-Hoop bend\nCH2 band\nC-O stretch\nO-H stretch H-bonded\nsp C-H stretch\nWAVENUMBERS (cm3)\nFigure 24.6 IR spectrum of ethanol.\nMICRONS\nTRANSMITTANCE\nsp C-H stretch\n2500\nsp C-H stretch\nmond subst\nCHa\nBromatic OHO stretch\nWAVENUMBERS (cm)\nFigure 24.7 IR spectrum of toluene.\nApplications of Infrared Spectroscopy\ni.\nmona subst\nInfrared spectroscopy gives information about presence of different organic functional groups in a molecule.\nThe identity of a compound can also be established by comparing the finger print region of infrared spectrum "
