      "Introduction",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "Analytical chemistry is a branch of chemistry, which deals with the study of chemical characterizes the identity of a substance, while quantitative analysis examines its mass or concentration. Techniques used in qualitative analysis include chemical tests, spectroscopy, flame tests etc. Quantitative analysis employs gravimetric analysis, volumetric analysis and separation techniques, such as filtration, distillation, precipitation, extraction, centrifugation etc. There is overlap of techniques used characterize them. Spectroscopic techniques are also used for quantitative analysis. 24.1 Classical Methods of Analysis: Combustion analysis and determination of Molecular Formula",
      "Before attempting to deduce the structure of an unknown organic compound by spectroscopic techniques (Section 24.2), we can simplify the problem somewhat by examining the molecular formula of the substance.",
      "The classical procedure for determining the molecular formula of a substance involves; qualitative elemental analysis, quantitative elemental analysis and then molecular mass determination. A qualitative elemental analysis is used to find out what types of atoms are present: C, H, O etc. (Section 15.7). A quantitative elemental analysis such as combustion analysis, determines the relative percentages of each distinct type of atom in the molecule. Both of these techniques are used to establish the empirical formula for the compound. While knowing the molecular mass, a molecular formula can also be calculated.",
      "24.1.1 Combustion Analysis",
      "Tidbit",
      "The elemental composition of organic compounds may be determined by a gravimetric method known as combustion analysis. Combustion analysis is carried out for finding the percentage composition and empirical formula of an organic compounds containing carbon and hydrogen (C.Hy) or carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen (C.H,O.). In combustion analysis, a known weight of an organic compound is mixed with cupric oxide (CuO) and taken in a platinum boat, which is placed in a furnace etc. are present in the compound, heated to about 850°C, in a stream of pure oxygen then conventional combustion dioxide and water vapours which are swept out of the gas. Organic compound on burning produces carbon analysis cannot be used, for",
