      "5(ppm)",
      "Figure 24.10 'H NMR spectrum of methyl alcohol.",
      "24.2.2.4 Atomic Emission and Absorption Spectroscopy The study of the electromagnetic spectrum of elements is called Atomic Spectroscopy. Electrons exist in energy levels within an atom. These levels have well defined energies and electrons moving between them must absorb or emit radiations having energy equal to the difference between them. Every element has unique emission or absorption spectrum. Thus, the emission or absorption atomic spectra are used as finger print to identify the elements. Atomic spectroscopy is thought to be the oldest instrumental method for the identification of elements. Atomic emission and absorption spectrophotometry is used for qualitative and quantitative analysis of different metals.",
      "Flame Photometry",
      "One of the widely used emission spectroscopic technique is flame photometry (more accurately called Flame Atomic Emission Spectrophotometry). An aqueous analyte",
      "373",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "is introduced into flame. Water evaporates and solid salt is left behind. The salt breaks into constituent atoms. The atoms go to vapour state. The gaseous atoms get excited in the flame. The excited atoms loose energy of characteristic wavelength, which is dispersed by a grating or prism and detected in the spectrophotometer. The intensity of light emitted by the atoms is directly proportional to the concentration",
      "of analyte.",
      "Flame",
      "Lens Slit Filter",
      "Amplifier",
      "Photo detector",
      "Burner",
      "Mixing chamber",
      "Atomizer",
      "LCD display",
      "Sample",
      "Compressed Air supply",
      "Compressed Fuel supply",
      "Figure 24.11 Schematic diagram of Flame Photometer.",
      "in",
      "Flame photometer has both qualitative and quantitative applications. The radiations. emitted by the flame photometer are characteristic to particular metal. Flame photometer with monochromators emits radiations of characteristic wavelengths which help to detect the presence of a particular metal in the sample. The concentration of unknown solution can be calculated from working curve of standard solutions. This technique is usually used to find the concentration of various alkali and alkaline earth metals in soil samples which are critical for soil cultivation. Flame photometry is also used to determine the concentrations of Na and K+ ions in biological samples and also estimation of various metals in soft drinks and juices.",
      "Atomic Absorption Spectrophotometry",
      "Atomic absorption spectrophotometry is used for the analysis of elements. The sampl solution is evaporated in a flame, and the sample is converted to atomic vapour. Som of the atoms in the flame get excited but majority of them are in ground state. When light of particular wave length from the hollow cathode lamp is passed through the",
      "374",
      "vapours",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "atoms, they absorb the light for excitation from ground state to high energy state. The absorbance of light by atoms is directly proportional to the concentration of atomic in the flame. For the analysis of different elements different types of lamps are used which produce light of different wave lengths. Each element shows absorption of particular wave length. Before introducing the sample of unknown concentration, solution of known concentrations of that element are aspirated into the flame one by one. Their absorbances are recorded and a working curve is constructed, between concentration and absorbance. Then sample of unknown concentration is aspirated into the flame and its concentration is determined from their absorbance using working curve.",
      "Atomic absorption spectroscopy is used for the estimation of over sixty metals in polluted water, body fluids, soft drinks etc.",
