CHEM_12_DATA[0].sections.push({id:"13.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 13 Exercise — fully worked answers",theory:`MCQs (printed p.44): 1) a, Al; Be and Al have similar electronegativity. 2) a, Si and Al are the most abundant listed crustal elements. 3) c, Li⁺ has high charge density and strongly polarizes carbonate, reducing its thermal stability. 4) a, BaSO₄ is insoluble. 5) a, Li forms the normal oxide Li₂O; heavier alkali metals favor peroxide/superoxide products. 6) b, F₂ is the strongest oxidizing halogen. 7) b, I has the lowest electron affinity among the choices. 8) c, NaF has the highest melting point among the listed sodium halides (small F⁻ gives stronger lattice attraction). 9) b is the intended choice; HF’s unusually high boiling point is explained directly by intermolecular hydrogen bonding, enabled by fluorine’s high electronegativity. 10) b, CO₂ is acidic; Al₂O₃ and SnO₂ are amphoteric and CaO is basic. 11) b, Al rapidly forms a protective oxide layer. 12) c, K is the textbook answer among the listed choices for readily reacting with oxygen; exact oxide products and thermodynamic comparisons depend on conditions. 13) c, Mg(OH)₂ is the strongest base among these choices. 14) a, inert-pair effect is strongest for Pb in this set. 15) a, C and Si have little tendency to form stable dichlorides compared with heavier Group 14 elements.

Short answers (printed p.45): 1) Down Group IIA, larger cations have lower charge density and polarize the oxyanion less; thermal stability of nitrates/carbonates therefore generally increases down the group. 2) Group IIA elements are alkaline-earth metals: two valence electrons, commonly +2 ions, reactive but less so than Group IA, form basic oxides/hydroxides, and show increasing size down the group (with Be showing anomalous covalent/amphoteric behavior). 3) Carbonates of Group IA are generally more thermally stable than those of Group IIA because +1 cations polarize CO₃²⁻ less; within a group, stability generally rises as cation size increases. 4) BeCl₂ is unusually covalent/polymeric because small Be²⁺ strongly polarizes Cl⁻; the larger Group IIA chlorides are more ionic. 5) Group IVA chlorides become less covalent and more ionic down the group as cation polarizing power falls; carbon and silicon chlorides are covalent molecular compounds, while heavier chlorides show greater ionic character and hydrolysis behavior varies. 6) Oxidizing power decreases F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂ because the ability to gain electrons decreases down the group. 7) Test halogens with halide solutions: chlorine displaces Br⁻ and I⁻, bromine displaces I⁻, iodine displaces neither; observe characteristic colour changes, using safe small-scale lab protocols. 8) Be(OH)₂ reacts with both acids and strong bases, so it is amphoteric; e.g. Be(OH)₂ + 2H⁺ → Be²⁺ + 2H₂O, and Be(OH)₂ + 2OH⁻ → [Be(OH)₄]²⁻. 9) Argon’s quoted radius is a van der Waals radius, measured between non-bonded atoms; it is larger than covalent radii used for bonded atoms, so it does not follow the simple period trend. 10) First ionization energy generally rises across Period 3, but Al is below Mg because its removed electron is in higher-energy 3p; S is slightly below P because electron pairing in 3p increases repulsion.

Long answers: 1a) Halide ions act as reducing agents by donating electrons to an oxidant; reducing strength rises down the group: I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻ > F⁻. 1b) H–X bond enthalpy measures energy to break the bond; it decreases down the group as bond length increases, so thermal stability of hydrogen halides generally decreases HF > HCl > HBr > HI. 2a) Group IIA oxygen products depend on cation size and oxygen conditions: smaller Be/Mg/Ca favor normal oxides, while larger Sr/Ba can form peroxides more readily. 2b) Inert-pair effect is the reluctance of the outer ns² pair in heavier p-block elements to participate in bonding; it stabilizes lower oxidation states down groups (e.g. Pb(II) relative to Pb(IV)). 3a) On heating Group IIA nitrates, carbonates and hydrogen carbonates decompose; decomposition becomes harder down the group as cation polarizing power decreases. Typical equations: MCO₃ → MO + CO₂; M(NO₃)₂ → MO + 2NO₂ + ½O₂; 2MHCO₃ → MCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O. 3b) Group IVA oxides change from acidic CO₂/SiO₂ through amphoteric GeO₂/SnO₂ to more basic PbO; oxidation state and structure affect details. 4a) Period 3 oxides change from strongly basic Na₂O and MgO, to amphoteric Al₂O₃, to acidic covalent oxides of Si, P, S and Cl; acidity generally increases across the period. 4b) Hydroxides similarly change from strongly basic NaOH/Mg(OH)₂ to amphoteric Al(OH)₃ and weakly acidic/sparingly soluble silicon hydroxide forms, then acidic oxyacids for P, S and Cl; solubility/structure should be considered rather than treating every oxide as a simple hydroxide. 5a) F–F bond enthalpy is unexpectedly low due to lone-pair repulsion; Cl–Cl is stronger, while Br–Br and I–I weaken with increasing bond length. H–X bond enthalpy falls HF > HCl > HBr > HI. 5b) I⁻ is the strongest reducing halide because it is most readily oxidized; Br⁻ is next, then Cl⁻, with F⁻ weakest. Example: Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂.

Project: Flame-test reference table: Li⁺ crimson red; Na⁺ intense yellow; K⁺ lilac; Ca²⁺ orange-red/brick red; Sr²⁺ crimson; Ba²⁺ apple green; Cu²⁺ blue-green. Record salt and observed colour, then infer the cation; clean the wire between samples and use teacher supervision. Flame colours can overlap, so a flame test is a screening clue, not definitive identification.`,source:"Checked against Unit 13 printed pp.44–45 (JPG 024_page_1.jpg and 024_page_2.jpg); explanations expanded for learning."});
CHEM_12_DATA[1].sections.push({id:"14.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 14 Exercise — fully worked answers",theory:`MCQs (printed pp.78–79): 1) d, Cu⁺ is d¹⁰ and diamagnetic; 2) d, potassium ferrocyanide is a complex salt; 3) a, [CuCl₄]²⁻ is tetrahedral; 4) d, chromium is +6 in CrO₂Cl₂; 5) b, [BeF₄]²⁻ is tetrahedral; 6) d, Co is a typical transition element (Zn/Cd have filled d subshells; Ra is s-block); 7) c, paramagnetism depends on unpaired electrons; 8) a, ligands donate electron pairs; 9) c, Sb is not ferromagnetic; 10) d, [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ has coordination number 6; 11) d, oxalate C₂O₄²⁻ is bidentate; 12) b, Cu²⁺ colour arises mainly from d–d transitions; 13) a, dichromate is an oxidizing agent; 14) c, German silver contains Cu, Ni and Zn; 15) c, neutral-ligand names are generally retained, with conventional exceptions such as aqua, ammine and carbonyl.

Short answers (p.79): 1) Cu⁺ has configuration [Ar]3d¹⁰, so all d electrons are paired; Cu²⁺ is [Ar]3d⁹ with one unpaired electron and is paramagnetic. 2) First-row configurations: Sc [Ar]3d¹4s²; Ti 3d²4s²; V 3d³4s²; Cr 3d⁵4s¹; Mn 3d⁵4s²; Fe 3d⁶4s²; Co 3d⁷4s²; Ni 3d⁸4s²; Cu 3d¹⁰4s¹; Zn 3d¹⁰4s². (Argon core understood.) 3) Partially filled d orbitals and variable oxidation states enable adsorption and intermediate formation, lowering activation energy; some metals also offer suitable surfaces. 4) Transition elements form complexes because their small, often highly charged ions accept lone pairs from ligands into available orbitals; variable oxidation states and d-orbital participation support diverse coordination. 5) ns and (n−1)d orbital energies are close, so different numbers of both types of electrons can participate in bonding. 6) Sc³⁺ is d⁰; it has no d–d transition and no unpaired d electron, so its compounds are usually colourless and diamagnetic. 7) Transition elements have partly filled d subshells in atoms or common ions; compared with s/p elements they commonly show variable oxidation states, coloured/paramagnetic ions, complex formation, catalytic activity and alloy formation. 8) In [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺, water is neutral and the complex charge is +3, so Cr oxidation state is +3. 9) Fe is a catalyst in Haber synthesis; the Contact process uses V₂O₅ (or Pt) for SO₂ oxidation. Fe is unsuitable for that step under the process conditions and sulfur-containing impurities can poison catalysts. 10) Zn²⁺ is d¹⁰ with no available d–d transition, so its salts are generally white/colourless; Cu²⁺ is d⁹ and absorbs visible light through d–d transitions, giving coloured salts.

Long answers: 1a) Sc through Zn configurations are listed above; 1b) Cr and Cu differ from simple Aufbau predictions because half-filled d⁵ and filled d¹⁰ subshells are relatively stable, giving Cr [Ar]3d⁵4s¹ and Cu [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹. 2a) In a ligand field, d orbitals split into energy levels. Absorption of visible light promotes an electron between levels; the complementary light gives the observed colour. d⁰/d¹⁰ ions generally lack d–d colour. 2b) General transition properties: variable oxidation states; complex formation; coloured ions; magnetic behaviour from unpaired d electrons; catalytic activity; high density, hardness and melting points (with exceptions); alloy formation. 2c) V and Fe catalysis: V₂O₅ catalyzes SO₂ + ½O₂ ⇌ SO₃ in the Contact process; finely divided Fe catalyzes N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ in the Haber process. Both work through surface adsorption and reaction intermediates. 3) Coordination nomenclature: name cation before anion; within a complex name ligands alphabetically (ignoring multiplicative prefixes), then metal; use di-, tri-, tetra- or bis-/tris- for complex ligand names; anionic ligands end in -o (chlorido, cyanido, hydroxido), neutral ligand names include aqua, ammine, carbonyl; write oxidation state of the metal in Roman numerals. Anionic complexes use metal names ending -ate (ferrate, cuprate). Examples: [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ hexaamminecobalt(III) chloride; K₄[Fe(CN)₆] potassium hexacyanidoferrate(II). 4a) Acidified KMnO₄ oxidizes alkenes/alkynes and other organic reductants; products depend on substrate and conditions. For example, dilute cold permanganate oxidizes an alkene to a vicinal diol; hot acidic permanganate may cleave C=C bonds. Purple permanganate is reduced (often to nearly colourless Mn²⁺ in acid). 4b) Alloys are mixtures of metals or a metal with another element; differing atom sizes disrupt the regular lattice, often increasing hardness/strength and corrosion resistance while changing melting point, conductivity and malleability. Examples include brass (Cu–Zn), bronze (Cu–Sn) and German silver (Cu–Ni–Zn). 5a) Copper commonly shows +1 and +2; Cu⁺ is d¹⁰, Cu²⁺ is d⁹. 5b) With hydroxide, [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ forms pale-blue Cu(OH)₂ precipitate; excess NH₃ produces a deep-blue ammine complex [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺; carbonate gives a blue-green basic copper carbonate precipitate. Equations (simplified): [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂(s) + 6H₂O; [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O; Cu²⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → CuCO₃(s) (actual precipitate may be basic carbonate depending conditions).

Project: Heating blue CuSO₄·5H₂O drives off water and leaves white anhydrous CuSO₄: CuSO₄·5H₂O(s) → CuSO₄(s) + 5H₂O(g). After cooling, adding water rehydrates the salt and restores the blue colour; the hydration is exothermic. Record colour before/after heating and after water addition. Use goggles, a heat-safe container, tongs and teacher supervision; do not seal the vessel.`,source:"Checked against Unit 14 printed pp.78–80 (JPG 041_page_1.jpg through 042_page_1.jpg)."});
CHEM_12_DATA[2].sections.push({id:"15.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 15 Exercise — fully worked answers",theory:`MCQs (printed pp.97–98): 1) b, urea was the first organic compound synthesized from inorganic starting materials in the laboratory (Wöhler, 1828). 2) a, graphite is elemental carbon, not an organic compound; urea, methane and acetic acid are organic compounds. 3) a, anthracite has the highest carbon content among the listed coal types. 4) c is the closest textbook choice: petroleum is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons, with abundant alkanes and cycloalkanes; it is not only one class. 5) d, methane is the major component of natural gas. 6) b, refinery gas is not a product of coal destructive distillation; coal tar, coal gas and coke are. 7) c, Fischer–Tropsch synthesis converts CO and H₂ into hydrocarbons. 8) a, pyridine is heterocyclic. 9) d, amide group is –CONH₂. 10) d, oxygen is not detected by the standard direct Lassaigne tests for N/S/halogens. 11) d, Lassaigne’s extract is used to test nitrogen, sulphur and halogens. 12) c, yellow AgI insoluble in NH₄OH indicates iodine. 13) a, CO and H₂ are the Fischer–Tropsch feedstocks. 14) d, buckminsterfullerene has pentagonal and hexagonal rings. 15) d, pyridine contains nitrogen and is heterocyclic.

Short answers: 1) Organic compounds are carbon compounds typically containing C–H bonds; organic chemistry studies their structures, properties, reactions and synthesis. Some carbon compounds such as CO, CO₂, carbonates, carbides and elemental carbon are conventionally treated as inorganic. 2) Lassaigne test principle: fuse a small sample with sodium so covalently bound N/S/halogens become water-soluble ionic salts; extract into water and test separate portions. Nitrogen gives Prussian blue after FeSO₄/oxidation; sulphur gives black PbS with lead acetate (or violet complex with nitroprusside); halides give AgX after acidifying with HNO₃ and adding AgNO₃. Perform only as a supervised microscale lab because sodium and reagents are hazardous. 3) A homologous series is a family with the same functional group and general formula; successive members differ by –CH₂– (14 mass units), have similar chemical reactions and show gradual physical-property trends. 4) Buckminsterfullerene is elemental carbon but belongs in carbon/organic chemistry because it forms discrete molecular carbon structures and is studied alongside carbon compounds; “organic” conventions are broader than the literal presence of C–H. 5) Carbon-containing substances generally treated as inorganic include CO, CO₂, carbonates, bicarbonates, carbides, cyanides and elemental carbon (graphite/diamond/fullerenes, by convention). 6) Partial synthesis starts from naturally derived material and chemically modifies it; total synthesis builds a compound from simpler starting materials. 7) Coal can be converted to liquid hydrocarbons by gasification to CO/H₂ (syngas), followed by Fischer–Tropsch catalytic synthesis; historical direct hydrogenation processes are another route. 8) Carbon’s tetravalency, catenation, multiple bonding, stable C–C/C–H bonds and ability to form chains/rings with many heteroatoms create a vast number of compounds. 9) Acyclic compounds have open chains (straight or branched); cyclic compounds contain one or more rings. Rings may be carbocyclic or heterocyclic and aromatic or non-aromatic.

Long answers: 1) Fossil fuels are energy-rich remains of ancient biomass transformed over geological time. Coal forms mainly from buried plant matter; ranks include peat, lignite, bituminous coal and anthracite, with increasing carbon content. Petroleum and natural gas form from buried marine organic matter under heat and pressure and occur in porous reservoirs. They are non-renewable on human timescales and their combustion releases CO₂ and air pollutants. 2) Element detection: carbon is oxidized with CuO to CO₂, which turns limewater milky (CaCO₃); hydrogen forms water that condenses or turns anhydrous CuSO₄ blue. Nitrogen, sulphur and halogens are tested after sodium fusion (Lassaigne extract) using the confirmatory reactions above. Oxygen is not normally detected by a single direct Lassaigne test; infer it from composition or functional-group tests. 3) Destructive distillation of coal yields coke (fuel, metallurgical reducing agent), coal gas (fuel), coal tar (source of aromatic chemicals/dyes/medicines) and ammoniacal liquor (ammonia salts/fertilizer feedstock). Fractions depend on process temperature and setup. 4) Organic compounds commonly show covalent bonding, catenation, isomerism, comparatively low melting/boiling points for small molecules, solubility in organic solvents, and slower reactions than many ionic reactions; these are trends, not universal rules. 5) A functional group is the atom/group that largely determines characteristic reactions. Classification examples: alkane C–C single bonds; alkene C=C; alkyne C≡C; haloalkane C–X; alcohol –OH; ether –O–; aldehyde –CHO; ketone >C=O; carboxylic acid –COOH; ester –COOR; amine –NH₂; amide –CONH₂; nitrile –C≡N. 6) By carbon skeleton, organic compounds are acyclic (straight/branched chains) or cyclic. Cyclic structures include homocyclic/carbocyclic rings (all ring atoms carbon) and heterocyclic rings (one or more ring heteroatoms); both may be alicyclic or aromatic. They can also be grouped as aliphatic, aromatic and heterocyclic depending on structure.

Project: For urea, detect C and H by heating a small sample with dry CuO: CO₂ clouds limewater and water vapour turns anhydrous CuSO₄ from white to blue. Detect N by sodium-fusion extract and the supervised Prussian-blue test. Use microscale quantities, eye protection and a fume hood/teacher supervision; do not perform sodium fusion at home. Record sample, reagent, observation and inference separately.`,source:"Checked against Unit 15 printed pp.97–98 (JPG 050_page_2.jpg and 051_page_1.jpg); the preceding carbon/hydrogen and Lassaigne procedures were scanned on pp.94–95."});
CHEM_12_DATA[3].sections.push({id:"16.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 16 Exercise — fully worked answers",theory:`MCQs (printed pp.158–159): 1) b, C₅H₁₂ has three constitutional isomers: pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane. 2) b, zinc dehalogenation of 2,2,3,3-tetrabromobutane gives but-2-yne. 3) b, Lindlar catalyst partially hydrogenates but-2-yne to cis-but-2-ene. 4) d, excess HCl adds twice to acetylene to form 1,1-dichloroethane. 5) c, homolysis gives an alkyl free radical. 6) d, chlorine is deactivating but ortho/para-directing. 7) d, none of the listed alkenes has two different substituents on each alkene carbon, so none shows E/Z isomerism. 8) a, C₂H₆O has two common constitutional isomers (ethanol and dimethyl ether). 9) d, Markovnikov addition gives tert-butyl bromide. 10) b, 2-methylbut-2-ene cleavage gives acetone and acetaldehyde. 11) c, alcoholic KOH promotes dehydrohalogenation. 12) b, sec-butyl bromide has a stereogenic carbon. 13) c, UV chlorination of toluene proceeds by free-radical substitution at the side chain. 14) a, ethyne has an acidic terminal hydrogen and forms an acetylide salt with NaNH₂. 15) b, nitro is meta-directing, so m-chloronitrobenzene is major.

Short answers (p.159): 1) Aliphatic hydrocarbons are open-chain or non-aromatic cyclic compounds; aromatic hydrocarbons contain conjugated, delocalized ring systems such as benzene. 2) Plane-polarized light has electric-field oscillations in one plane; an optically active chiral sample rotates that plane. 3) Shake an alkene with dilute bromine water: the orange/brown colour disappears as Br₂ adds across C=C. 4) Benzene is resonance-stabilized by six delocalized π electrons and usually undergoes substitution; ethene has a localized π bond and readily undergoes addition. 5) Halogens deactivate benzene by their inductive electron withdrawal, but lone-pair resonance donation directs substitution to ortho/para positions. 6) Chirality is non-superimposability on a mirror image; a chiral carbon has four different substituents and is one common cause, but chirality can arise without one such carbon. 7) But-1-ene has no E/Z because one alkene carbon has two H atoms; each carbon of 2-butene has two different substituents, allowing cis/trans forms. 8) Cyclopropane has severe angle strain and bent bonds, making it more reactive than unstrained propane. 9) A terminal alkyne has ≡C–H; with ammoniacal AgNO₃ or Cu(I) reagent it can form an insoluble acetylide, unlike an internal alkyne. 10) Tautomerism is interconversion of constitutional isomers by proton and double-bond shift (often keto–enol); resonance structures differ only in electron placement, not atom positions, and are not isolable molecules.

Long answers: 1a) Chlorination of methane is a radical chain: initiation Cl₂ —hv→ 2Cl·; propagation Cl·+CH₄→HCl+CH₃·, then CH₃·+Cl₂→CH₃Cl+Cl·; termination occurs when radicals combine. Further substitution can form CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄. 1b) Cycloalkanes have carbon rings; cyclopropane’s 60° bond angles cause strong strain and bent bonds, making it unusually reactive; larger rings approach tetrahedral angles. 1c) Alkanes/cycloalkanes are largely nonpolar, insoluble in water and soluble in nonpolar solvents; boiling points rise with molecular size and branching generally lowers boiling point. Lower members are gases, intermediate members liquids, higher members solids; they burn and undergo substitution, while strained small rings can add reagents. 2a) Hydrocarbons contain only C and H: alkanes (single bonds), alkenes (C=C), alkynes (C≡C), alicyclic compounds and aromatic compounds. 2b) Ethene from ethanol: CH₃CH₂OH —conc. H₂SO₄, heat→ CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O. Ethene from ethyl bromide: CH₃CH₂Br + KOH(alc) → CH₂=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O. 2c) Alkene stability generally rises with substitution due to hyperconjugation/inductive effects; for geometrical isomers, trans is often more stable than cis due to less steric crowding; conjugation adds stabilization. 3a) Vicinal dihalide: add Br₂ to alkene; alcohol: acid-catalyzed hydration or hydroboration-oxidation; epoxide: epoxidize with a peroxyacid; aldehydes/ketones: ozonolysis followed by reductive workup. 3b) Terminal alkynes are weakly acidic because the sp-hybridized carbon stabilizes the conjugate base; they form acetylides with strong bases and precipitates with ammoniacal Ag⁺/Cu⁺, unlike internal alkynes. 4a) Benzene is a planar six-membered ring with six delocalized π electrons above/below the σ framework; molecular-orbital delocalization makes all C–C bonds equivalent and stabilizes the ring. 4b) Electrophilic aromatic substitution: generate E⁺; benzene attacks to form a resonance-stabilized arenium ion; a base removes H⁺ and aromaticity returns. 4c) From benzene: acetophenone by CH₃COCl/AlCl₃; toluene by CH₃Cl/AlCl₃; nitrobenzene by HNO₃/H₂SO₄; benzenesulfonic acid by SO₃/H₂SO₄; chlorobenzene by Cl₂/FeCl₃; cyclohexane by catalytic H₂/Ni or Pt at elevated conditions. 5a) Electron-donating groups (e.g. –OH, –NH₂, alkyl) usually activate and direct ortho/para; electron-withdrawing groups (e.g. –NO₂, –COOH, –CHO) deactivate and direct meta; halogens deactivate by induction but direct ortho/para by resonance. 5b) Names from the printed structures: (i) 3-methylpentane; (ii) 2-methyl-4-propyloctane (longest chain selected from the branch through to the far end); (iii) 2-ethylpent-1-ene; (iv) 3-ethylpent-2-ene; (v) 2,2,5-trimethylhept-3-yne; (vi) the low-resolution drawing appears to be propylcyclobutane; (vii) 3-bromo-1-chloro-2,6-dinitrobenzene; (viii) methyl-substituted benzene-1,3-diol (the source drawing’s exact locants are difficult to distinguish at this scan resolution); (ix) 1-ethyl-2,4-dimethylcyclohexane; (x) anthracene. Names in the separate draw-structure list: 3-ethyl-4-methyloctane; 4-isopropyldecane; sec-butylcycloheptane; 2,3-dimethyl-4-propylnonane; 2,4,4-trimethylhex-2-ene; (Z)-hex-3-ene for the stated cis form; hex-2-yne; 1,2-dimethyl-4-propylbenzene; cyclopropylbenzene. Condensed formulas can be drawn directly from the parent chain and substituent locants.

Project: Bromine decolorization tests for unsaturation. In a properly equipped teaching lab, add bromine solution dropwise to a small sample and compare with a known saturated control; rapid loss of bromine colour suggests an alkene/alkyne addition reaction. Use teacher supervision, a fume hood, eye protection and microscale quantities. Bromine and carbon tetrachloride are hazardous; modern safer solvent substitutions and institutional rules take precedence, and this experiment must not be done at home.`,source:"Checked against Unit 16 printed pp.158–161 (JPG 081_page_1.jpg through 082_page_2.jpg). Two structure drawings are low-resolution; their ambiguity is stated above."});
CHEM_12_DATA[3].sections.push({id:"16.exercise.structures",title:"Unit 16 Structure-drawing answers (condensed forms)",theory:`The exercise asks for these nine structures; these condensed formulas specify the connectivity (draw the indicated branches and rings):
1. 3-ethyl-4-methyloctane: CH₃–CH₂–CH(CH₂CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃.
2. 4-isopropyldecane: CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH[CH(CH₃)₂]–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃.
3. sec-butylcycloheptane: cycloheptane–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃ (ring attaches at the secondary carbon).
4. 2,3-dimethyl-4-propylnonane: CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₂CH₂CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃.
5. 2,4,4-trimethylhex-2-ene: CH₃–C(CH₃)=CH–C(CH₃)₂–CH₂–CH₃.
6. cis-hex-3-ene: cis-CH₃CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂CH₃ (both ethyl groups on the same side of the double bond).
7. Hex-2-yne: CH₃–C≡C–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃.
8. 1,2-dimethyl-4-propylbenzene: benzene ring with CH₃ at C1 and C2 and CH₂CH₂CH₃ at C4; formula C₆H₃(CH₃)₂(CH₂CH₂CH₃).
9. Cyclopropylbenzene: C₆H₅–(cyclopropyl), a benzene ring bonded directly to one carbon of a cyclopropane ring.`,source:"Structural prompts on printed p.161, checked against JPG 082_page_2.jpg."});
CHEM_12_DATA[4].sections.push({id:"17.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 17 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed pp.190–191): 1) a, tert-butyl alcohol: CH₃MgI adds CH₃⁻ equivalent to acetone, then hydrolysis gives (CH₃)₃COH. 2) a, C₂H₅Br: among the listed ethyl halides it reacts fastest in nucleophilic substitution because Br⁻ is the best leaving group here. 3) a, an electron-deficient species is an electrophile. 4) d, R–CH₂–X represents a primary alkyl halide (the carbon bearing X is bonded to one carbon; for R as an alkyl group). 5) a, a positively charged carbon bonded to three groups is a carbocation. 6) d, Grignard reagent is R–Mg–X. 7) c, ester addition with excess Grignard reagent followed by hydrolysis gives a tertiary alcohol. 8) b, an electron-pair donor is a nucleophile (Lewis base). 9) b, the C–X carbon in a secondary halide is attached to two other carbon atoms. 10) c, an SN1 reaction generally gives partial racemization because the planar carbocation can be attacked from either face, often with some ion-pair bias. 11) a, acetone is aprotic. 12) a, SN1 rate depends on substrate ionization/carbocation formation and solvent, but not on nucleophile identity; the textbook's intended exception among the listed choices is nature of nucleophile. 13) b, ethylmagnesium bromide + CO₂, then hydrolysis, gives propanoic acid (one carbon is added). 14) d, methyl chloride has tetrahedral geometry around carbon. 15) a, steric crowding around the alpha carbon favors elimination over substitution.

Q2. Short answers (printed p.191):
1) In nucleophilic substitution, a nucleophile replaces a leaving group at carbon (e.g. R–Br + OH⁻ → R–OH + Br⁻). In electrophilic substitution, an electrophile replaces a hydrogen or substituent, commonly on an aromatic ring; the ring regains aromaticity after substitution (e.g. benzene nitration).
2) Alkyl halides have a polarized C–X bond: carbon is electrophilic and can be attacked by nucleophiles, while benzene's delocalized pi cloud is electron-rich and reacts with electrophiles by substitution to preserve aromaticity.
3) Tertiary carbocations are stabilized by three alkyl groups through electron donation (inductive effect) and hyperconjugation; their usual stability order is 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl.
4) In polar protic solvent, a tertiary halide can ionize to a stable tertiary carbocation (SN1), while the same substrate can undergo E1 after ionization. Their rates can be comparable because both share the slow carbocation-forming step; temperature and base/nucleophile conditions shift the product ratio.
5) In polar protic solvents, F⁻ is strongly hydrogen-bond solvated, which suppresses its nucleophilicity. In polar aprotic solvents, it is less strongly solvated and becomes a much stronger nucleophile.
6) SN2 is a concerted backside attack: the nucleophile approaches opposite the leaving group, forming the C–Nu bond as C–X breaks. This minimizes repulsion and causes inversion of configuration.
7) C–F is very strong and F⁻ is a poor leaving group, so alkyl fluorides are least reactive. Alkyl iodides react fastest because C–I is weaker and I⁻ is a stable, polarizable leaving group.
8) In aqueous solution, secondary amines are often more basic than tertiary amines because their conjugate acids are well stabilized by alkyl donation and better hydration/less steric hindrance; tertiary ammonium ions are less effectively solvated.
9) CH₃⁺ has three C–H sigma bonds in a trigonal planar arrangement and an empty p orbital perpendicular to that plane; it lacks the fourth bond needed for a tetrahedral pyramidal shape.
10) A nucleophile donates an electron pair to an electron-poor atom to make a bond. A base accepts a proton. Species such as OH⁻ can do both; the reaction context determines which description applies.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.191–193):
1(a) Preparation of alkyl halides: (i) replace alcohol –OH using HX, PCl₃/PCl₅ or SOCl₂, e.g. R–OH + HBr → R–Br + H₂O; (ii) radical halogenation of alkanes, R–H + X₂ —hv→ R–X + HX (mixtures may form); (iii) addition of HX to alkenes, following Markovnikov orientation unless radical conditions with HBr apply; (iv) addition of X₂ to alkenes gives vicinal dihalides; (v) halide exchange such as Finkelstein reaction, R–Cl/R–Br + NaI (acetone) → R–I + NaCl/NaBr. Elimination: alcoholic base and heat remove a beta hydrogen and the halide from adjacent carbons, producing an alkene: CH₃CH₂Br + KOH(alc) → CH₂=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O. E2 is concerted; E1 proceeds through a carbocation and competes with SN1.
1(b) In elimination, two sigma bonds (C–H and C–X) break and a C=C pi bond forms. The major alkene often follows Zaitsev's rule (more substituted alkene) where applicable; stereochemistry and substrate structure can affect the outcome.
2(a) SN1: slow ionization R–X → R⁺ + X⁻, followed by rapid nucleophile attack; first-order rate k[R–X], favored by tertiary substrates and polar protic solvents; planar carbocation permits racemization/rearrangement. SN2: one-step backside attack Nu⁻ + R–X → R–Nu + X⁻; rate k[Nu⁻][R–X], favored by methyl/primary substrates, strong nucleophiles and polar aprotic solvents; gives inversion and no carbocation rearrangement.
2(b) Substrate: molecule containing the carbon–leaving-group bond that reacts. Leaving group: atom/group that departs with the bonding electron pair, such as Br⁻. Carbocation: positively charged carbon species with three bonds and an empty orbital.
3(a) Prepare a Grignard reagent by reacting an alkyl/aryl halide with magnesium turnings in rigorously dry ether: R–X + Mg → R–MgX. Water/protic impurities destroy the reagent, so glassware and solvent must be dry.
3(b) Grignard reactions after acidic work-up: (i) with formaldehyde gives a primary alcohol, RMgX + HCHO → RCH₂OH; with another aldehyde gives a secondary alcohol; (ii) ketone gives a tertiary alcohol; (iii) ester reacts twice with Grignard reagent and gives a tertiary alcohol (methanoate esters are a special case); (iv) CO₂ gives a carboxylic acid with one added carbon, RMgX + CO₂ then H₃O⁺ → RCO₂H.
4(a) Amines are prepared by reduction of nitro compounds/nitriles/amides, ammonolysis of alkyl halides, or Gabriel synthesis for primary aliphatic amines. Their nitrogen is approximately trigonal pyramidal with a lone pair; this lone pair accepts H⁺, so amines are bases. Alkyl groups increase electron density, but aqueous basicity also depends on solvation and steric effects.
4(b) Common reactions: salt formation with acids (RNH₂ + HCl → RNH₃⁺Cl⁻), alkylation, acylation of primary/secondary amines, and reaction with nitrous acid (class-dependent products). Primary amines also form sulfonamides in Hinsberg's test; the supplied project describes distinguishing primary, secondary and tertiary amines by product formation/solubility.
5(a) Carbocations are positively charged carbon intermediates, commonly formed by heterolytic C–X bond cleavage or protonation/addition to pi bonds. Formation examples: SN1 ionization R–X → R⁺ + X⁻; alkene + H⁺ → carbocation.
5(b) Stability: benzylic/allylic resonance-stabilized carbocations are especially stable; among simple alkyl cations, 3° > 2° > 1° > CH₃⁺ due to inductive donation and hyperconjugation. Vinylic/aryl carbocations are very unstable.
5(c) Systematic names from the displayed structures: (i) 3-iodo-3-methylhexane (choose the longest six-carbon chain; tie gives lowest locants to alphabetically first substituent); (ii) 4-bromo-4-ethyl-3-methyl-2-chlorohexane, written systematically as 4-bromo-2-chloro-4-ethyl-3-methylhexane; (iii) 5-bromo-4-fluoro-5-methylhept-1-ene (number from the terminal double bond; check the image's halogen labels: the leftmost substituted carbon bears Br, central bears Br and F); (iv) 4-bromo-3-ethyl-2,3,4-trimethylhexane; (v) 4-methylhexan-4-amine. For (ii), longest chain includes the ethyl branch; alphabetical order and locants yield the listed name.
5(d) Condensed structural formulas: (i) 3-methylpentane-2,3-diamine: CH₃–CH(NH₂)–C(NH₂)(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃. (ii) 2,3-dimethylhexane-2,3-diamine: CH₃–C(NH₂)(CH₃)–C(NH₂)(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃. (iii) 2-bromo-3-fluoro-2,3-dimethylpentane: CH₃–C(Br)(CH₃)–C(F)(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃. (iv) 4-bromo-4-chloro-3-fluoro-2,3-dimethylhexane: CH₃–CH(CH₃)–C(F)(CH₃)–C(Br)(Cl)–CH₂–CH₃. (v) 2-chloro-3,3-dimethylpentan-2-amine: CH₃–C(Cl)(NH₂)–C(CH₃)₂–CH₂–CH₃.

Project, Hinsberg test (printed p.193): Add aqueous NaOH and benzenesulfonyl chloride to the amine sample and shake (teacher-supervised microscale lab). A primary amine forms a sulfonamide with an N–H that deprotonates and dissolves in base; a secondary amine forms an N,N-disubstituted sulfonamide that remains insoluble; tertiary amines do not form sulfonamides and remain as salts in acid. Follow a validated lab protocol and use proper ventilation/PPE; the book’s abbreviated procedure is not a home experiment.`,source:"Checked against the supplied JPG scans for printed pp.190–193 (097_page_1.jpg through 098_page_2.jpg). Structure (iii) locants were read from the scan; verify the drawn chain if a higher-resolution original is available."});
CHEM_12_DATA[5].sections.push({id:"18.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 18 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed pp.222–223): 1) b, isopropyl alcohol is secondary. 2) c, n-propyl alcohol has the highest boiling point among these choices because it forms hydrogen bonds and is larger than ethanol; ether cannot donate hydrogen bonds. 3) d, trimethylene glycol (propane-1,3-diol) is neither vicinal nor geminal: its OH groups are separated by one carbon. 4) a, periodic-acid cleavage of ethylene glycol yields two molecules of formaldehyde. 5) b, an oxygen carrying positive charge and three bonds is an oxonium ion. 6) a, acidified dichromate is a standard oxidant for primary alcohols; PCC can stop at an aldehyde under controlled conditions. 7) c, benzyl alcohol is an aromatic alcohol (OH is on the side chain); p-cresol is a phenol. 8) b, ethanethiol is added as an odorant to detect natural-gas leaks. 9) c, 2-methyl-2-propanol is tertiary and reacts fastest with Lucas reagent. 10) b, ethoxyethane is protonated by concentrated sulfuric acid to an oxonium ion. 11) b, Lucas reagent is ZnCl₂ + HCl. 12) c, alcohol + sodium gives an alkoxide and hydrogen. 13) a, hydrolysis of benzene diazonium salt yields phenol. 14) d, oxidation of a secondary alcohol gives a ketone. 15) a, Lucas-test cloudiness is due to poorly soluble alkyl chloride.

Q2. Short answers (printed p.223):
i) Alcohols have –OH bonded to an aliphatic sp³ carbon (R–OH); phenols have –OH directly bonded to an aromatic ring (Ar–OH). Phenoxide is resonance-stabilized, making phenols appreciably more acidic.
ii) Four structural alcohol isomers of C₄H₁₀O: butan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH; butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃; 2-methylpropan-1-ol, (CH₃)₂CHCH₂OH; 2-methylpropan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃COH.
iii) Lucas test uses concentrated HCl and anhydrous ZnCl₂ to convert alcohols to alkyl chlorides. Tertiary alcohols turn cloudy immediately, secondary in minutes, primary only very slowly/on heating, because the rates roughly follow carbocation stability under these conditions.
iv) Phenol is more acidic because its conjugate base, phenoxide, is stabilized by resonance over the aromatic ring; an alkoxide has its negative charge largely localized on oxygen and alkyl groups donate electron density.
v) Primary alcohol: the OH-bearing carbon is attached to one carbon (or none for methanol); secondary: attached to two; tertiary: attached to three.
vi) Alcohol oxygen has lone pairs and is protonated by acids to an oxonium ion; phenol is less basic because its oxygen lone pair is delocalized into the ring, reducing availability for protonation.
vii) Intermolecular dehydration of a single unsymmetrical alcohol can give mixtures of ethers (R–O–R and R'–O–R'), and dehydration conditions can also yield alkenes. Williamson synthesis gives controlled ether products by SN2 reaction of an alkoxide with a suitable primary alkyl halide.
viii) Bromine water: an alkene rapidly decolorizes Br₂ by addition across C=C and does not normally give a white precipitate. Phenol rapidly decolorizes it and forms white 2,4,6-tribromophenol precipitate.
ix) Aryl diazonium salts lose nitrogen readily on warming with water; N₂ is a very stable gas and its loss drives hydrolysis to phenol.
x) Tertiary alcohols lack a hydrogen on the carbon bearing OH (no alpha hydrogen), so ordinary oxidation cannot form a carbonyl without breaking a C–C bond.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.223–224):
1(a) Diethyl ether by acid dehydration: 2 CH₃CH₂OH —conc. H₂SO₄, about 413 K→ CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃ + H₂O. At higher temperature, elimination to ethene competes. Williamson route: CH₃CH₂ONa + CH₃CH₂Br → CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃ + NaBr (SN2).
1(b) Diethyl ether is a colorless, volatile, flammable liquid with a characteristic odor; it has a lower boiling point than alcohols of similar molar mass because it cannot hydrogen-bond to itself, is only slightly soluble in water, and is a good solvent for many organic substances. Vapors can form explosive mixtures; peroxides may form on storage.
1(c) Two reactions: cleavage by HI, CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃ + HI → CH₃CH₂I + CH₃CH₂OH (excess HI/heat can convert the alcohol further); combustion, C₄H₁₀O + 6 O₂ → 4 CO₂ + 5 H₂O. Ethers also form oxonium salts with strong acids.
2(a) Phenols are compounds with an –OH group directly attached to an aromatic ring (ArOH). Preparation methods include hydrolysis of benzene diazonium salts (ArN₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O → ArOH + N₂ + HCl), fusion of sodium benzenesulfonate with NaOH followed by acidification, and the industrial cumene process.
2(b) Phenol is weakly acidic: C₆H₅OH ⇌ H⁺ + C₆H₅O⁻. It reacts with Na and NaOH to form phenoxide; it is generally not strong enough to react with NaHCO₃. Bromination in water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol; nitration and electrophilic substitution occur readily because –OH activates the ring and directs ortho/para.
3) Phenol structure: oxygen is attached directly to an sp² aromatic carbon; one lone pair conjugates with the ring, giving resonance contributors and stabilizing phenoxide after deprotonation. Chemical properties: weak acidity and phenoxide formation; electrophilic substitution (bromination, nitration, sulfonation); ferric-chloride colour test; esterification/acylation at oxygen; oxidation under stronger conditions. Ortho/para activation reflects resonance donation by –OH.
4(a) Alcohol structure: R–OH with a polar C–O and O–H bond; oxygen has two lone pairs and the local geometry is bent. Preparation: hydration of alkenes; substitution/hydrolysis of alkyl halides; reduction of aldehydes, ketones, acids or esters; fermentation for ethanol; and Grignard addition followed by hydrolysis.
4(b) Alcohols are only weakly acidic because alkoxide is not strongly stabilized; they react with active metals such as Na to release H₂ and form alkoxides. Phenols are more acidic because phenoxide is resonance stabilized; phenols react with NaOH, while ordinary alcohols do not. Both can donate a proton to sufficiently strong bases.
5(a) Grignard reagent RMgX adds its carbon nucleophile to carbonyl carbon; acid work-up protonates the alkoxide. Formaldehyde gives a primary alcohol; another aldehyde gives a secondary alcohol; a ketone gives a tertiary alcohol.
5(b) From an alcohol: (i) ethene by dehydration, CH₃CH₂OH —conc. H₂SO₄, heat→ CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O; (ii) ethyl acetate by esterification, CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O (H⁺); (iii) acetone from propan-2-ol oxidation, CH₃CHOHCH₃ + [O] → CH₃COCH₃ + H₂O; (iv) formaldehyde from controlled oxidation/dehydrogenation of methanol, CH₃OH + [O] → HCHO + H₂O.
5(c) Names from structures: (i) 2-methylbutan-2-ol; (ii) 2-methylpentane-2,3-diol; (iii) 2-methylpent-4-en-2-ol; (iv) 2-bromo-3-fluoro-4-methylphenol (OH carbon is ring C1; number in the direction giving Br/fluoro lowest locants); (v) 2,3-dimethoxybutane.
5(d) Condensed answers: (i) pentan-2-ol: CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃. (ii) 3,4-dimethylhexan-2-ol: CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃. (iii) 4-methylpent-3-en-2-ol: CH₃CH(OH)CH=C(CH₃)CH₃. (iv) 3,4-dimethyl-2-nitrophenol: phenol ring with NO₂ at C2 and CH₃ at C3 and C4. (v) 2,3-dimethoxy-4-methylpentane: CH₃CH(OCH₃)CH(OCH₃)CH(CH₃)CH₃.

Project, bromine-water phenol test (printed p.224): Dissolve a small phenol sample in ethanol or methanol, then add bromine water dropwise with stirring. Rapid decolorization and a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol indicate phenol. Bromine and phenol are hazardous; conduct only as a teacher-supervised microscale lab with ventilation and PPE, never at home.`,source:"Checked against printed pp.222–224 (JPG 113_page_1.jpg through 114_page_1.jpg); page 224 begins Unit 19 after the exercise/project."});
CHEM_12_DATA[6].sections.push({id:"19.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 19 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed pp.245–246): 1–9 are on the preceding exercise page; see the source exercise entry already present in this unit if available. Answers for the scanned items: 10) a, hydration of an aldehyde gives a geminal diol. 11) a, Schiff’s base (imine) is formed by a primary amine. 12) a, a hemiacetal has alcohol and aldehyde-derived functional character (one –OH and one –OR on the same carbon). 13) a, formaldehyde is most reactive toward nucleophilic addition because it has the least steric hindrance and no electron-donating alkyl groups. 14) d, acetal formation proceeds through nucleophilic addition to the carbonyl followed by substitution/dehydration steps. 15) b, oxidation of a primary alcohol does not give a ketone; secondary alcohol oxidation does. MCQs 1–9 were not visible in the supplied scans reviewed for this section and must not be guessed.

Q2. Short answers (printed pp.246–247):
i) Aldehydes have at least one hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon (R–CHO); ketones have two carbon groups attached (R–CO–R′).
ii) Acid protonates carbonyl oxygen, increasing polarization and making carbonyl carbon more electrophilic, so nucleophilic attack is faster.
iii) The C=O pi bond is polarized toward oxygen, so nucleophiles attack the carbonyl carbon. Alkene C=C is relatively electron-rich and commonly reacts with electrophiles.
iv) Aldehydes have one H and only one alkyl group (formaldehyde has none), so they are less sterically hindered and more electrophilic than ketones, which have two electron-donating alkyl groups.
v) Acid catalysis activates carbonyl oxygen and allows water attack; base catalysis generates the stronger nucleophile OH⁻. Cyanide is already a strong nucleophile and HCN is weakly ionized; acid would protonate CN⁻ and suppress its concentration, so cyanohydrin addition is base/nucleophile promoted.
vi) Grignard carbon is strongly nucleophilic/basic and adds directly to the electrophilic carbonyl carbon; an acid catalyst would protonate and destroy RMgX.
vii) Two alkyl groups donate electron density to the carbonyl carbon and create steric hindrance, so ketones are less electrophilic and less accessible than aldehydes.
viii) Their oxygen can accept hydrogen bonds from water, making the lower members soluble despite no acidic O–H hydrogen.
ix) Oxidation of a ketone usually cleaves a C–C bond next to the carbonyl. The resulting carboxylic-acid fragments have fewer carbon atoms than the parent ketone.
x) Tollens’ reagent: warm the sample gently with freshly prepared ammoniacal silver nitrate. Aldehydes reduce Ag(I) to metallic silver, producing a silver mirror/grey deposit; ordinary ketones do not. Use only supervised microscale quantities; never store Tollens reagent.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.247–248):
1(a) Acyclic saturated aldehyde/ketone constitutional isomers for C₅H₁₀O: aldehydes: pentanal CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CHO; 2-methylbutanal CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CHO; 3-methylbutanal (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CHO; 2,2-dimethylpropanal (CH₃)₃CCHO. Ketones: pentan-2-one CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃; pentan-3-one CH₃CH₂COCH₂CH₃; 3-methylbutan-2-one CH₃COCH(CH₃)CH₃.
1(b) Carbonyl carbon is trigonal planar, sp²-hybridized; C=O is polar, with δ+ carbon and δ− oxygen. Nucleophiles attack the carbonyl carbon, shifting pi electrons to oxygen; protonation gives addition products. Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones due to reduced steric and electron donation. Under acid, oxygen protonation enhances electrophilicity; under base, nucleophile attack forms an alkoxide intermediate.
1(c) Example: CH₃CHO + HCN ⇌ CH₃CH(OH)CN. CN⁻ attacks carbonyl carbon; the alkoxide is protonated by HCN. The product is a cyanohydrin.
2(a) Carbonyl derivatives: cyanohydrin: R₂C=O + HCN → R₂C(OH)CN; oxime: R₂C=O + NH₂OH → R₂C=NOH + H₂O; imine: R₂C=O + R′NH₂ ⇌ R₂C=NR′ + H₂O; hydrazone: R₂C=O + H₂NNH₂ → R₂C=NNH₂ + H₂O; acetal: RCHO + 2ROH ⇌ RCH(OR)₂ + H₂O under acid (ketones form ketals, also called acetals in common usage).
2(b) Select RMgX and carbonyl, then hydrolyze: (i) butan-1-ol: HCHO + n-propyl-MgX, then H₃O⁺; (ii) ethanol: HCHO + CH₃MgX, then H₃O⁺; (iii) butan-2-ol: CH₃CHO + C₂H₅MgX, then H₃O⁺; (iv) 2-methylbutan-2-ol: acetone + C₂H₅MgX, then H₃O⁺.
3(a) Aldehyde preparation: controlled oxidation/dehydrogenation of primary alcohols; ozonolysis of alkenes where one fragment is aldehyde; partial reduction of acid chlorides (Rosenmund); reduction of nitriles to aldehydes (Stephen, followed by hydrolysis); hydroboration-oxidation of terminal alkynes gives aldehydes.
3(b) Acetaldehyde reactions: (i) ethylmagnesium iodide then hydrolysis gives butan-2-ol: CH₃CHO + C₂H₅MgI → CH₃CH(OMgI)C₂H₅ → H₃O⁺ CH₃CH(OH)C₂H₅. (ii) Zn(Hg)/HCl Clemmensen reduction gives ethane, CH₃CHO → CH₃CH₃. (iii) LiAlH₄ then water gives ethanol, CH₃CHO → CH₃CH₂OH. (iv) acidified dichromate oxidizes it to ethanoic acid, CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH.
4(a) Aromatic ketones are prepared by Friedel–Crafts acylation: ArH + RCOCl —AlCl₃→ ArCOR + HCl (or acid anhydride/AlCl₃). The acylium ion RCO⁺ is electrophile; aromatic substitution restores ring aromaticity.
4(b) Starting from acetone: (i) propane by Clemmensen or Wolff–Kishner reduction; (ii) propan-2-ol by NaBH₄/LiAlH₄; (iii) acetone cyanohydrin by HCN/KCN; (iv) acetone hydrazone by NH₂NH₂; (v) acetic acid by oxidative cleavage of acetone under strong oxidation (with the other carbon fragment oxidized to CO₂; do not imply a clean selective one-product conversion).
5(a) Acid catalysis: protonate O, nucleophile attacks carbonyl C, deprotonate to product. Base catalysis: nucleophile attacks C=O, pi electrons move to O, then proton transfer. Both pathways pass through a tetrahedral intermediate; acid and base conditions alter the nucleophile and proton-transfer steps.
5(b) Lower aldehydes/ketones are colorless, volatile liquids with characteristic odors; carbonyl dipole gives higher boiling points than similar hydrocarbons/ethers, but they cannot self hydrogen-bond as donors, so lower boiling points than corresponding alcohols. Small members dissolve in water by accepting H-bonds; solubility falls as hydrocarbon chain grows. They are flammable and undergo nucleophilic addition; aldehydes oxidize readily, ketones resist mild oxidation.
5(c) Names from the displayed structures: (i) pent-3-en-2-one; (ii) 4,5-dimethylhexan-3-one; (iii) 2,3-dimethylbutanal; (iv) 2-bromo-3-methylbutane-1,4-dial; (v) 2-methylbut-2-ene-1,4-dial.
5(d) Condensed structures: (i) 2,4-dimethylpent-2-enal: OHC–C(CH₃)=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH₃. (ii) 2,2-dimethylbutanal: OHC–C(CH₃)₂–CH₂–CH₃. (iii) 3,3-dimethylhex-4-en-2-one: CH₃CO–C(CH₃)₂–CH=CH–CH₃. (iv) 4-methylhex-5-en-3-one: CH₃CH₂COCH(CH₃)CH=CH₂. (v) pent-4-yn-2-one: CH₃COCH₂C≡CH.

Project, Tollens test (printed p.248): Freshly prepare Tollens reagent from AgNO₃ and dilute NH₃ until precipitate just dissolves, then add sample and warm gently. Aldehydes form silver mirror/deposit; ketones normally do not. The textbook quantities should be scaled down and performed only in a supervised lab. Tollens reagent can form dangerous residues if stored; prepare fresh and dispose promptly under institutional procedure.`,source:"Checked against printed pp.246–248 (JPG 125_page_1.jpg through 126_page_1.jpg). Exercise MCQs 1–9 are on the previous printed page and were not included in this scan pass; they are explicitly marked unavailable rather than inferred."});
CHEM_12_DATA[6].sections.push({id:"19.exercise.mcq-correction",title:"Unit 19 Exercise — MCQs 1–9 from printed p.245",theory:`Source scan correction: the previously scanned pp.246–248 omitted printed p.245. The first nine MCQs are: 1) b, carbonyl carbon is sp²-hybridized. 2) c, Clemmensen reduction converts carbonyl to methylene. 3) d, formaldehyde + CH₃MgI then hydrolysis gives ethanol. 4) d, ethanal gives Tollens silver mirror. 5) b, ethanal reduces Fehling’s solution but does not react with sodium metal; ethanol reacts with sodium but does not reduce Fehling’s solution. 6) b, acetone is propanone. 7) c, formalin is conventionally about 40% aqueous formaldehyde. 8) c, symmetrical but-2-ene gives only one aldehyde product type on ozonolysis: ethanal. 9) a, acetylene hydration gives an aldehyde (ethanal), while the listed higher alkynes yield ketones.`,source:"Printed p.245, JPG 124_page_2.jpg."});
CHEM_12_DATA[7].sections.push({id:"20.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 20 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed p.269): 1) c, C₆H₅COOH is aromatic carboxylic acid. 2) c, butyric acid is C₄H₈O₂. 3) a, acetic acid has the highest boiling point among listed because carboxylic acids hydrogen-bond and dimerize. 4) b, methanol's carbon is sp³; carbonyl carbons in the others are sp². 5) d, fluoroacetic acid is strongest among these by the strong –I effect of fluorine. 6) a, hydrolysis of methyl cyanide (acetonitrile) yields acetic acid. 7) b, Fischer esterification is acid + alcohol. 8) d, thionyl chloride converts carboxylic acids to acyl chlorides. 9) a, alkaline ester hydrolysis is saponification. 10) d, acid derivatives hydrolyze back to carboxylic acids.

Q2. Short answers (printed p.270):
i) Pure, water-free acetic acid freezes near room temperature (about 16.6°C) to ice-like crystals, hence “glacial.”
ii) Carboxylic acids form strong hydrogen-bonded dimers; this raises their boiling points above comparable alcohols, which generally do not form equally stable cyclic dimers.
iii) Carboxylate conjugate bases are resonance-stabilized over two oxygens, unlike phenoxide (less effective stabilization for this ionization) and alkoxide/water conjugate bases; this makes carboxylic acids more acidic.
iv) Methanoate is less electron-donating than methyl, so HCOOH's conjugate base is less destabilized; methanoic acid is stronger than ethanoic acid.
v) Chlorine withdraws electron density inductively and stabilizes the carboxylate anion, increasing acidity relative to acetic acid.
vi) Oxidation of a primary alcohol gives an aldehyde, then further oxidation gives the carboxylic acid: RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH.
vii) In carboxylic-acid derivatives, the carbonyl carbon is less electrophilic because the attached heteroatom donates a lone pair by resonance; aldehyde carbonyl has no such donor and less steric hindrance.
viii) Amide nitrogen donates strongly by resonance into C=O, making the carbonyl carbon least electrophilic and NH₂⁻ a very poor leaving group; amides are therefore least reactive toward nucleophilic acyl substitution.
ix) Base hydrolysis of an ester produces a carboxylate salt, which is not readily re-esterified under the basic conditions; acidification gives the acid. This effectively drives the reaction forward, hence saponification.
x) Acyl halides are most reactive because halide is a weak base/good leaving group and contributes little resonance stabilization; nucleophilic acyl substitution is therefore rapid.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.270–271):
1) Carboxylic acids are weak Brønsted acids: RCOOH ⇌ RCOO⁻ + H⁺. The carboxylate has two equivalent resonance contributors. Electron-withdrawing substituents stabilize RCOO⁻ and increase acidity; electron-donating groups destabilize it and reduce acidity. Effects are stronger when substituents are nearer the –COOH and when more are present.
2) Acetic acid preparation: (i) nitrile hydrolysis: CH₃CN + 2H₂O + H⁺ → CH₃COOH + NH₄⁺ (or alkaline hydrolysis then acidification); (ii) Grignard carbonation: CH₃MgCl + CO₂ then H₃O⁺ → CH₃COOH; (iii) oxidize ethanol/ethanal: CH₃CH₂OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O; (iv) oxidize ethanal: CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH; (v) hydrolyze acetyl chloride: CH₃COCl + H₂O → CH₃COOH + HCl.
3) From acetic acid: (i) acetyl chloride: CH₃COOH + SOCl₂ → CH₃COCl + SO₂ + HCl; (ii) acetic anhydride: 2 CH₃COOH —P₂O₅, heat→ (CH₃CO)₂O + H₂O; (iii) acetamide: CH₃COOH + NH₃ → CH₃COONH₄ —heat→ CH₃CONH₂ + H₂O; (iv) ethyl acetate: CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O (H⁺); (v) ethyl alcohol: reduce CH₃COOH with LiAlH₄ then water → CH₃CH₂OH.
4) Equations: (i) CH₃COCl + H₂O → CH₃COOH + HCl. (ii) C₆H₅COCl + CH₃OH → C₆H₅COOCH₃ + HCl. (iii) CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH + (CH₃CO)₂O → CH₃CH₂CH₂COOCOCH₃ + CH₃COOH (mixed anhydride; textbook wording can also be interpreted as esterification with acetic anhydride, giving butyl acetate). (iv) CH₃CH₂CH₂COCl + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂CH₂CONH₂ + NH₄Cl. (v) CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃CH₂CH₂COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O (H⁺).
5(a) Three preparations: oxidation of primary alcohols/aldehydes; hydrolysis of nitriles; carbonation of Grignard reagents. Other routes include hydrolysis of acyl derivatives and oxidation of alkylbenzenes.
5(b) Systematic names: (i) 2-methylbutanedioic acid; (ii) 3-methylhexanoic acid; (iii) ethyl 3-methylpentanoate; (iv) pent-4-enamide; (v) pent-4-ynoic acid.
5(c) Condensed structures: (i) 3-ethyl-4-methylpentanoic acid: HOOC–CH₂–CH(CH₂CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH₃. (ii) ethyl 3-methylbutanoate: CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂COOCH₂CH₃. (iii) 2-chloropropanoyl bromide: CH₃CH(Cl)COBr. (iv) ethanoic propanoic anhydride: CH₃CO–O–COCH₂CH₃. (v) 3,3-dimethylbutanenitrile: (CH₃)₃CCH₂CN (nitrile carbon is C1).
5(d) Complete reactions: (i) CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH + PCl₅ → CH₃CH₂CH₂COCl + POCl₃ + HCl. (ii) CH₃CH₂COOH + SOCl₂ → CH₃CH₂COCl + SO₂ + HCl. (iii) CH₃CH₂CONH₂ + LiAlH₄, then H₂O → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂. (iv) CH₃COOCH₃ + NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + CH₃OH. (v) CH₃CH₂COOCH₃ + NaOH → CH₃CH₂COONa + CH₃OH. (vi) CH₃CH₂COONa + NaOH —CaO, heat→ CH₃CH₃ + Na₂CO₃. (vii) CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅ + LiAlH₄, then H₂O → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + C₂H₅OH. (viii) CH₃CH₂MgBr + CH₃CH₂CN, then hydrolysis → CH₃CH₂COCH₂CH₃ (pentan-3-one; nitrile + Grignard gives ketone after hydrolysis).

Project: Add a small amount of carboxylic acid to aqueous NaHCO₃. Effervescence of CO₂ indicates acid: RCOOH + NaHCO₃ → RCOONa + CO₂ + H₂O. Use dilute microscale quantities with eye protection.`,source:"Checked against printed pp.269–271 (JPG 135_page_2.jpg through 136_page_2.jpg)."});
CHEM_12_DATA[8].sections.push({id:"21.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 21 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed pp.306–307): 1) c, pepsin works best near pH 2. 2) a, cellulose is the most abundant natural carbohydrate. 3) a, a glycosidic bond links monosaccharide units such as glucose. 4) c, steroids are lipids. 5) d, hemoglobin has quaternary structure. 6) a, deoxyribose does not fit Cₙ(H₂O)ₙ. 7) d, starch is converted to ethanol by fermentation (after hydrolysis to sugars). 8) b, humans cannot digest cellulose because they lack cellulase. 9) b, a ketone is not a feature of an aldohexose. 10) c, zinc deficiency can delay wound healing. 11) b, RNA is mainly found in cytoplasm (including ribosomes), though it also occurs in the nucleus. 12) a, maltose contains two glucose units. 13) d, a competitive inhibitor competes with substrate for the enzyme active site. 14) c, adenine pairs with thymine in DNA. 15) b, proteins are polymers of amino acids joined by peptide bonds.

Q2. Short answers (printed p.307):
i) Vegetable oils contain unsaturated fatty-acid chains with C=C bonds. Oxygen can react at these sites to form hydroperoxides and breakdown products with rancid odors/flavors; heat/light accelerate it.
ii) Saturated fatty-acid chains are straighter and pack closely, strengthening dispersion forces and raising melting point. Cis double bonds kink unsaturated chains, reduce packing and lower melting point.
iii) Glucose and fructose: both C₆H₁₂O₆ monosaccharides, but glucose is an aldohexose and fructose a ketohexose. Sucrose and maltose: sucrose = glucose + fructose, nonreducing; maltose = glucose + glucose, reducing. Cellulose and starch: both glucose polymers; cellulose has beta-1,4 links and structural role, starch has alpha links and energy storage.
iv) Nucleic acids store, transmit and express genetic information; DNA carries hereditary instructions, RNA participates in transcription/translation, and some RNAs regulate genes or catalyze reactions.
v) Sucrose hydrolysis: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O → glucose + fructose (acid or sucrase/invertase); products are equimolar monosaccharides.
vi) Denaturation disrupts a protein's secondary/tertiary (and often quaternary) structure without normally breaking peptide bonds; it loses its native shape and biological activity.
vii) Nucleic acids consist of nucleotide units. Each nucleotide has a phosphate group, pentose sugar (ribose or deoxyribose), and nitrogenous base (A,G,C plus T in DNA or U in RNA).
viii) Primary: amino-acid sequence. Secondary: local alpha helices/beta sheets stabilized mainly by backbone hydrogen bonds. Tertiary: full three-dimensional fold stabilized by hydrophobic interactions, hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions and disulfide bonds. Quaternary: association of multiple polypeptide subunits.
ix) Competitive inhibitor binds the active site and competes with substrate; adding substrate can reduce inhibition. Noncompetitive inhibitor binds elsewhere and changes activity; increasing substrate does not fully overcome it. Uncompetitive inhibitor binds enzyme–substrate complex and lowers apparent Vmax and Km.
x) Lipids are largely hydrophobic, insoluble in water and soluble in nonpolar solvents; many are less dense than water. They include fats/oils, phospholipids, steroids and waxes; hydrolysis of many lipids yields fatty acids and alcohols. Unsaturated oils can undergo oxidation and hydrogenation.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.307–308):
1(a) Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes/ketones or substances that yield them on hydrolysis. They are classified as monosaccharides (one unit; glucose, fructose), oligosaccharides (2–10 units; disaccharides sucrose/maltose/lactose), and polysaccharides (many units; starch, glycogen, cellulose). They also classify as aldoses/ketoses and by carbon count. Monosaccharides provide immediate energy; glucose is cellular fuel. Starch/glycogen store energy; cellulose provides plant structure and dietary fiber. Excess refined sugar can contribute to dental caries/metabolic disease, while adequate complex carbohydrate supports nutrition.
1(b) Carbohydrates provide about 4 kcal/g and are a primary energy source; glucose fuels cells, glycogen stores glucose in animals, and starch stores it in plants. Fiber (cellulose and related material) supports bowel function and helps regulate glycemic response. Ribose/deoxyribose are components of RNA/DNA; carbohydrate chains also participate in cell recognition. Diet should favor fiber-rich whole foods over excess added sugars.
2(a) Proteins are amino-acid polymers joined by peptide bonds. Classes include fibrous (collagen, keratin), globular (enzymes, hemoglobin), simple (yield amino acids on hydrolysis), conjugated (protein plus prosthetic group, e.g. hemoglobin), and derived proteins formed by partial breakdown/denaturation. Examples and functions: enzymes catalyze reactions; antibodies defend; actin/myosin enable movement; collagen supports tissues; hemoglobin transports oxygen; albumin transports substances.
2(b) Properties: high molecular mass; amphoteric behavior due to amino/carboxyl groups; characteristic isoelectric point; colloidal behavior; solubility varies by pH, salt and structure; hydrolysis yields peptides/amino acids; heat, pH or chemicals can denature them; color tests include Biuret (peptide bonds) and xanthoproteic (aromatic residues).
3(a) Lipids are water-insoluble biomolecules, commonly esters of fatty acids and alcohols. Classification: simple (fats/oils, waxes), compound (phospholipids, glycolipids, lipoproteins) and derived (fatty acids, glycerol, steroids, fat-soluble vitamins). Triglyceride structure: glycerol esterified with three fatty acids; phospholipid has two fatty acids plus phosphate-containing head.
3(b) Lipids store concentrated energy (~9 kcal/g), form membranes, insulate and cushion organs, provide essential fatty acids, aid absorption of vitamins A,D,E,K, and act as steroid-hormone/signaling precursors. Excess saturated/trans fats can increase cardiovascular risk; unsaturated fats are important in balanced diets.
4(a) DNA nucleotide: deoxyribose, phosphate, and base A/T/G/C; RNA nucleotide uses ribose and U instead of T. Nucleotides connect by 3′–5′ phosphodiester bonds. DNA generally forms antiparallel double helix with A–T and G–C pairing; RNA is usually single-stranded and can fold into structures. DNA stores hereditary information; RNA includes mRNA, tRNA, rRNA and regulatory/catalytic types.
4(b) DNA replication/transcription/translation transfer sequence information: DNA is transcribed to RNA; ribosomes read mRNA codons, tRNA brings amino acids, and peptide bonds form a protein. DNA sequence changes can alter codons and protein sequence/expression.
5(a) Enzymes are biological catalysts, mostly proteins (some RNA), lowering activation energy without being consumed. Active-site shape/chemistry binds substrate; induced fit can assist catalysis. Enzyme action is affected by temperature, pH, substrate/enzyme concentration, cofactors and inhibitors. Each enzyme has an optimum range; high heat or extreme pH can denature it. Competitive inhibitors occupy active sites; noncompetitive inhibitors bind elsewhere; irreversible inhibitors permanently inactivate enzyme.
5(b) Temperature raises reaction rate until an optimum, after which unfolding lowers activity; pH changes ionization and structure, with each enzyme having an optimum. Low substrate concentration increases rate with substrate; at saturation, rate approaches Vmax. Inhibitors reduce rate; cofactors/coenzymes may be required. For digestion, amylase acts on starch, proteases on proteins, and lipases on fats.
6(a) Zinc supports enzymes, immunity, growth, DNA/protein synthesis, taste and wound healing; sources include meat, seafood, legumes, nuts and whole grains. Deficiency can impair growth, immunity and healing.
6(b) Calcium/phosphorus imbalance can cause weak or poorly mineralized bones/teeth (rickets/osteomalacia), osteoporosis, cramps or abnormal bone metabolism. Calcium is essential for bone, muscle contraction, nerve signaling and clotting; phosphorus is in bone, ATP, phospholipids and nucleic acids. Diagnosis/treatment requires clinical assessment.

Project: Acid curdling of milk. Warming milk and adding lemon juice/vinegar lowers pH and denatures/coagulates casein; curds separate from whey (physical separation accompanying protein aggregation). The curd is paneer-like and supplies protein, fat and minerals, depending on milk and processing. It is used in paneer, cheese, desserts and cooked dishes. Maintain hygienic food handling; do not boil as the project directs.`,source:"Checked against printed pp.306–308 (JPG 154_page_1.jpg through 155_page_1.jpg)."});
CHEM_12_DATA[9].sections.push({id:"22.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 22 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed pp.332–333): 1) a, Nylon 6,6 forms from hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid. 2) d, DDT is non-biodegradable. 3) b, “black gold” means petroleum. 4) c, cellulose is not a petrochemical. 5) c, multipart/two-part adhesives cure by chemical reaction. 6) b, nail-polish remover commonly contains propanone (acetone). 7) c, cracking breaks large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller molecules. 8) a, Rexine is a plasticized PVC product. 9) d, vinyl chloride is not obtained from benzene. 10) c, nylon is a condensation polymer. 11) c, the shown polyamide repeat unit is Nylon 6,6, a condensation polymer. 12) d, polythene is synthetic. 13) b, hair dyes are commonly grouped into four types (temporary, semi-permanent, demi-permanent and permanent). 14) a, casein glue is not synthetic. 15) b, acrylate-based adhesives are commonly pressure-sensitive.

Q2. Short answers (printed pp.333–334):
i) Reactive adhesives chemically cure (polymerize/cross-link) after application; non-reactive adhesives set by solvent evaporation, cooling, pressure or water loss without a chemical cure.
ii) Nail-polish remover dissolves the lacquer polymer/resin and releases it from the nail; propanone or alternative solvents evaporate after wiping.
iii) Temporary dyes mainly coat the hair surface and are water-soluble, so shampoo removes them; they do not penetrate/covalently alter the hair shaft as permanent dyes do.
iv) PVC uses: water/drain pipes, cable insulation, flooring, window frames, synthetic leather/rainwear, packaging and medical tubing (formulation-dependent).
v) Petrochemicals are basic chemicals made from petroleum/natural-gas feedstocks (e.g. ethylene, propylene, benzene); chemicals derived from them are downstream products such as plastics, solvents, fibers, detergents and medicines.
vi) Addition polymerization joins unsaturated monomers without eliminating a small molecule (e.g. ethene → polyethylene). Condensation polymerization joins bifunctional monomers while eliminating small molecules such as water/HCl (e.g. nylon-6,6).
vii) Non-acetone removers can be less drying/irritating for some users and may be preferable for artificial nails, depending on formulation; they still contain solvents and should be ventilated and kept away from ignition. Acetone is effective and evaporates quickly but can dry skin/nails and damage some artificial nail materials.
viii) Safety protects workers and the public from fire, toxic exposure, pressure/temperature hazards, spills and equipment failure; use training, PPE, ventilation, guards, monitoring, emergency procedures and safe storage.
ix) Petroleum refining separates crude oil into useful fractions and converts them to meet demand and quality specifications, improving the value and usability of crude.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.333–334):
1(a) Petrochemical process technology converts petroleum/natural-gas feedstocks into basic chemicals and products. Refinery gases/naphtha are separated; steam cracking produces ethylene, propylene and C₄ streams; catalytic reforming produces aromatics (BTX); fractionation, extraction, compression and purification prepare feedstocks. These undergo polymerization, oxidation, alkylation, hydrogenation or other reactions, followed by separation and product finishing. Process design controls heat/mass transfer, catalysts, pressure, safety and emissions.
1(b) Cracking converts heavy hydrocarbons to lighter, more valuable products. Thermal cracking uses high temperature/pressure; steam cracking uses dilution steam and very high temperature with short residence time to produce olefins; catalytic cracking uses acid catalysts to make gasoline-range hydrocarbons; hydrocracking uses hydrogen and catalyst to produce cleaner fuels and lighter fractions. Products depend on feed, catalyst and conditions.
2(a) Lipstick manufacture: melt waxes (e.g. beeswax/carnauba), oils/emollients and fats; disperse pigments/lakes in oil; blend with antioxidants, fragrance and permitted additives; homogenize, pour into molds, cool, demold and package. It provides color, lubrication and a protective film. Ingredient selection and hygiene must meet cosmetic regulations.
2(b) Nail-polish composition: film-forming polymer (often nitrocellulose), resins for adhesion/gloss, plasticizers for flexibility, volatile solvents (e.g. ethyl acetate/butyl acetate), pigments/pearlescent agents and stabilizers/thickeners. It is mixed, filtered and filled. Removers dissolve the film. Solvent vapors are flammable; manufacture/use require ventilation and controlled storage.
3(a) Adhesives are materials that join surfaces by interfacial adhesion and internal cohesion. By origin: natural (plant starch/dextrin, animal protein/casein, natural rubber) and synthetic (epoxy, polyurethane, acrylic, cyanoacrylate, polyvinyl acetate). By reactivity/set: reactive (epoxy, polyurethane, cyanoacrylate cure chemically), non-reactive (pressure-sensitive tapes, solvent/water-based, hot-melt thermoplastics). Selection depends on substrate, strength, temperature, moisture and cure time.
3(b) Adhesives are used in furniture, construction, packaging, footwear, vehicles, electronics, medical products, books, household repairs and composite materials; they distribute loads and can join dissimilar materials without drilling or welding.
4(a) Raw materials in Pakistan include natural gas, petroleum, rock salt, limestone, gypsum, coal, sulfur, silica, phosphate rock, soda ash, cotton, sugarcane/molasses and agricultural biomass. These support fertilizer, cement, glass, chlor-alkali, petrochemical, textile, sugar, paper and pharmaceutical industries. Availability and quality vary by location and import needs.
4(b) Process-industry safety: identify hazards and assess risk; design containment/relief and safe access; train workers; use task-appropriate PPE, ventilation and gas detection; manage flammable/toxic/reactive materials with labeling and segregation; inspect pressure vessels, electrical systems and machinery; apply lockout/tagout and permit-to-work; plan fire/spill/emergency response; monitor emissions and waste; report incidents and maintain equipment. Site-specific laws and procedures govern.
5(a) Fractional distillation heats crude oil and feeds vapor into a fractionating column. Components condense at different heights according to boiling range: refinery gases at top, then gasoline/naphtha, kerosene, diesel/gas oil, lubricating/heavy oils and residue/bitumen lower down. The fractions are mixtures, not pure compounds; conversion processes such as cracking and reforming improve yields and quality.
5(b) Chemical industries supply fertilizers, cement, pharmaceuticals, plastics, textiles, glass, paints, detergents and fuels; they create jobs, exports, infrastructure and inputs for agriculture/manufacturing. Their economic benefit depends on efficient production, safe working conditions, reliable feedstock and pollution control.
Project: Petroleum formed over geologic time from buried organic matter transformed by heat, pressure and microbial processes. Refining begins with desalting and fractional distillation, followed by conversion (cracking/reforming), treating and blending. Fractions include gases/LPG, gasoline/naphtha, kerosene, diesel, lubricants, fuel oil and bitumen. Major petrochemicals include ethylene, propylene, butadiene, benzene, toluene and xylenes; they are building blocks for plastics, synthetic rubber, fibers, solvents, detergents and many consumer/industrial goods. Their importance comes from enabling modern materials, while extraction, combustion, waste and emissions require careful management.`,source:"Checked against printed pp.332–334 (JPG 167_page_1.jpg through 168_page_1.jpg). The first short answer appears on p.331, outside this scan batch; the textbook sequence starts at item ii on p.333, and item i is supplied here as reactive versus non-reactive adhesive distinction."});
CHEM_12_DATA[10].sections.push({id:"23.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 23 Exercise — complete worked answers",theory:`Q1. MCQs (printed pp.355–356): 1) a, ozone is not a secondary pollutant in the general sense; ground-level ozone is secondary, but the intended answer is O₃ among the list of primary substances? The item is ambiguous because ozone is a secondary pollutant while H₂CO₃/H₂SO₄ form in the environment and CO₂ is emitted directly. The textbook likely expects d, CO₂, as a non-secondary pollutant. 2) d, mesosphere is the coldest atmospheric region. 3) a, CFCs destroy stratospheric ozone. 4) a, sewage has higher BOD than clean water. 5) c, photochemical smog involves ozone; NOx and VOC sunlight chemistry forms it. 6) d, ozone depletion lets more UV reach Earth. 7) d, acetic acid is not a principal acid-rain acid. 8) a, PAN forms through NOx/hydrocarbon photochemistry in sunlight. 9) c, wood combustion produces CO₂ and water vapor (among other products). 10) a, textbook table gives troposphere temperature from about 15°C to −56°C. 11) b, most atmospheric ozone is in the stratosphere. 12) a, CFCs are highly damaging to ozone. 13) d, DO, BOD and COD all help assess water quality. 14) a, alum (hydrated aluminum sulfate) is a common flocculant. 15) d, green chemistry includes waste prevention, atom economy and safer chemicals.

Q2. Short answers (printed p.356):
i) Acid rain dissolves/reacts with carbonate stone (marble/limestone), weakening and eroding historic buildings and monuments; it also corrodes metals and damages paint.
ii) Contaminant: an unwanted substance present in a material, not necessarily harmful. Pollutant: contaminant at a level or form that causes adverse effects. Acid rain: precipitation with pH below about 5.6 due mainly to sulfuric/nitric acids. Smog: polluted air mixture; industrial smog includes smoke/SO₂, photochemical smog forms from NOx and VOCs in sunlight. BOD: oxygen microorganisms need to decompose organic matter in water.
iii) Dissolved oxygen is needed by aquatic organisms and reflects water's ability to support life; low DO can signal organic pollution/eutrophication.
iv) Reduce SO₂ by low-sulfur fuels, fuel desulfurization, flue-gas scrubbers/limestone treatment, efficient combustion and replacing sulfur-rich fuels with cleaner energy.
v) Four Green Chemistry principles: prevent waste; maximize atom economy; use less hazardous syntheses; design safer chemicals. Other principles include safer solvents, energy efficiency, renewable feedstocks, catalysis and design for degradation.
vi) Four greenhouse gases: CO₂, CH₄, N₂O and water vapor (also ozone and fluorinated gases).
vii) Per molecule, many CFCs have much greater global-warming potential than CO₂, but CO₂'s total climate contribution is larger because emissions are vastly greater and it persists for a long time. The question's “higher potential” must specify per-mass horizon; the answer is CFCs on a per-molecule/per-mass basis.
viii) Rainwater dissolves atmospheric CO₂ to form weak carbonic acid, producing natural pH near 5.6. It becomes acid rain when sulfuric/nitric acid pollutants lower pH further, commonly below 5.6.
ix) A three-way catalytic converter reduces NOx to N₂ and oxidizes CO and unburned hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O.

Q3. Long answers (printed pp.356–357):
1(i) Green chemistry designs chemical products/processes to reduce or eliminate hazardous substances. It protects people and ecosystems, reduces waste and energy/resource use, and prevents pollution at source. Principles include prevention, atom economy, safer synthesis/products/solvents, energy efficiency, renewable feedstocks, catalysis, degradation and real-time monitoring. It can lower treatment costs and improve process efficiency, though alternatives still require life-cycle and hazard assessment.
1(ii) Greenhouse effect/global warming: greenhouse gases absorb outgoing infrared radiation and re-emit it, warming the lower atmosphere. Human increases in CO₂ (fossil fuels/land-use change), CH₄, N₂O and fluorinated gases intensify this effect, shifting climate patterns, raising average temperatures and contributing to sea-level rise and heat extremes. Mitigation includes clean energy, efficiency, methane control, forest protection and lower-emission transport/industry.
2(i) Atmosphere is the gas envelope around Earth, held by gravity. Troposphere: weather and most water vapor; temperature generally falls with altitude. Stratosphere: contains ozone layer; temperature rises with height through UV absorption. Mesosphere: meteors burn; coldest region. Thermosphere: very thin, high-energy gases and auroras; temperature rises strongly. Exosphere is the outer transition to space. Boundaries/heights vary by source and conditions.
2(ii) Air pollution is harmful gases/particles at concentrations that damage health, ecosystems or materials. Sources include vehicles, power plants, industry, burning and dust. Effects include respiratory/cardiovascular illness, reduced visibility, acid deposition, smog, crop damage, climate forcing and material corrosion. Controls: emission standards, cleaner fuels/energy, filters/scrubbers, catalytic converters, public transport and monitoring.
3(i) Stratospheric ozone absorbs much harmful UV-B/UV-C radiation, protecting organisms from DNA damage, skin/eye harm and ecological effects.
3(ii) Without the ozone layer, much more UV would reach the surface, increasing cancers/cataracts, damaging crops and phytoplankton and disrupting ecosystems.
4) Water pollution is harmful change in water's physical, chemical or biological quality. Sources: untreated sewage, industrial effluent, fertilizers/pesticides, oil, mining, plastics, pathogens, thermal discharges and sediment. Effects include disease, toxicity, eutrophication, oxygen depletion and ecosystem loss. Prevent at source, treat wastewater, control runoff and enforce discharge limits.
5(a) Acid rain: SO₂/NOx oxidize in air and form H₂SO₄/HNO₃; deposition acidifies lakes/soil and damages vegetation/buildings. Control SO₂/NOx emissions and use cleaner energy.
5(b) Smog: industrial (smoke + SO₂, often cool/humid) and photochemical (sunlight acts on NOx + VOCs to make ozone/PAN and oxidants). Reduce precursor emissions and vehicle/industrial pollution.
5(c) Water treatment: screening removes debris; coagulation/flocculation (often alum) aggregates fine particles; sedimentation and filtration remove solids; disinfection (chlorine, ozone or UV) inactivates pathogens. Advanced treatment may remove dissolved chemicals. A sand/cotton filter only removes suspended matter and does not make unsafe water potable.
5(d) Chemistry of stratosphere: UV photolyzes O₂ to O atoms; O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M, while O₃ + UV → O₂ + O. This natural cycle absorbs UV. CFC-derived chlorine radicals catalytically destroy ozone; international controls have reduced emissions, while recovery is gradual.

Project: A bottle filter with cotton/sand can clarify muddy water by straining and settling, but it does not reliably remove dissolved chemicals or pathogens. Label the output “not potable”; do not drink it without validated disinfection and testing.`,source:"Checked against printed pp.355–357 (JPG 178_page_2.jpg through 179_page_2.jpg). MCQ 1 wording is ambiguous; the likely intended textbook answer is identified with caveat."});

