      "theory": "Table 14.2 No. of Unpaired Electrons in the 1ˢᵗ Transition Series\n\nTherefore, binding energy increases upto Group –VIB and then progressively decreases upto Group – IIB. For example, in 3d transition series binding energy increases upto vanadium and then decrease upto zinc.\n\n14.1.4 Variable Oxidation States\n\nMost of the transition metals show variable oxidation states and form compounds showing more than one (variable) oxidation states because energy levels of ns and (n–1)d are very close. The involvement of the d-electrons in addition to ‘ns’ electrons in bond formation also take place. The oxidation state of 3d series is from +2 to +7 (except Cr and Cu).\n\nTidbit\n\nValency\n\nThe combining capacity of an atom is known as its valency.\n\nVariable Valency\n\nSome elements differ in their capacity to combine with other elements depending on the nature of the reaction; this property is called variable valency. For example, the iron in ferrous oxide shows a valency of +2, whereas in ferric oxide, it has a valency of +3.",
