      "content": "<p>Exercise</p><p>Q I. Choose the correct one from the given options.</p><p>1. Isopropyl alcohol is a type of</p><p>a. primary alcohol.   b. secondary alcohol.   c. tertiary alcohol.   d. none of these.</p><p>2. Which of the following has the highest boiling point?</p><p>a. Dimethyl ether   b. Ethyl alcohol   c. n–propyl alcohol   d. Isopropyl alcohol</p><p>3. Trimethylene glycol is a</p><p>a. vic–diol.   b. gem–diol.   c. triol.   d. none of these.</p><p>4. The final product of oxidative cleavage of ethylene glycol with periodic acid is</p><p>a. formaldehyde.   b. acetaldehyde.   c. formic acid.   d. mixture of formaldehyde and acetaldehyde.</p><p>5. Oxygen carrying positive charge and bonded to three other atoms is called</p><p>a. oxide ion.   b. oxonium ion.   c. carbocation.   d. carbanion.</p><p>6. Which of the proper reagent used for oxidation of a primary alcohol to aldehyde?</p><p>a. Acidified dichromate   b. Acidified chromate   c. Acidified permanganate   d. PCC</p><p>7. Which one is an aromatic alcohol?</p><p>a. methyl alcohol   b. ethyl alcohol   c. benzyl alcohol   d. p–cresol</p><p>8. Compound which is added to natural gas to make gas leakage detectable?</p><p>a. phenol   b. ethanthiol   c. methanol   d. hydrogen sulphide</p><p>9. Which of the following is more reactive toward Lucas Reagent?</p><p>a. methanol   b. ethanol   c. 2–methyl–2–propanol   d. 2–butanol</p><p>10. Ethoxyethane when treated with conc. H₂SO₄ produces</p><p>a. carbocation   b. oxonium ion   c. carbanion   d. oxalate ion</p><p>11. Lucas Reagent is</p><p>a. Zn–Hg/HCl   b. ZnCl₂ + HCl   c. Zn + H₂O   d. Na + liq.NH₃</p><p>12. The reaction of an alcohol with sodium produces</p><p>a. ethane   b. ethene   c. alkoxide   d. aldehyde</p><p>13. Benzene diazonium salt is a reactive salt and upon hydrolysis yields</p><p>a. phenol.   b. benzene.   c. diazobenzene.   d. benzoic acid.</p><p>14. Oxidation of secondary alcohol gives</p><p>a. carboxylic acid   b. ether   c. aldehyde   d. ketone</p><p>15. The appearance of cloudiness in the Lucas test for alcohol is due to formation of</p><p>a. alkyl chloride.   b. acid chlorides.   c. aldehydes.   d. ketones.</p><p>Q2. Write short answers of the following questions.</p><p>i. What is the basic difference between an alcohol and phenol, although both have same functional group?</p><p>ii. How many isomeric alcohols are possible for C₄H₉OH? Draw their structural formulae and write both common and IUPAC names.</p><p>iii. What is Lucas test? How Lucas test is used to distinguish alcohols?</p><p>iv. Why phenol is more acidic than alcohols?</p><p>v. What is meant by primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols?</p><p>vi. Why alcohol and ethers are easily protonated unlike phenol?</p><p>vii. Why intermolecular dehydration of alcohol is not a suitable method for synthesis of asymmetric ethers?</p><p>viii. How bromine water test in alkene differ from bromine water test for phenol?</p><p>ix. Why benzene diazonium salt is not much stable and hydrolyse to phenol easily?</p><p>x. Why tertiary alcohol can not be oxidized?</p><p>Q3. Write detail answers of the following questions.</p><p>1. (a) Describe the reaction for preparation of diethyl ether, by dehydration of alcohol and Williamson’s synthesis.</p><p>(b) Write two chemical reactions of diethyl ether.</p><p>(c) Enlist the physical properties of ether.</p><p>2. (a) What are phenols? Give method of preparation of phenol.</p><p>(b) Discuss the acidic behaviour of phenol.</p>",
