          "text": "The acidities of the carboxylic acids vary considerably with the nature of the substituents present in the molecule. Any substituent that stabilizes the negatively charged carboxylate ion promotes dissociation and vice versa. Electron withdrawing substituents (e.g. –X, –NO₂ etc.) enhance the strength of an acid by withdrawing electron density from the carboxylate ion. This inductive effect can be quite large if one or more strongly electron–withdrawing groups are present on the carbon atom adjacent to carboxyl carbon. Whereas an electron donating substituent (e.g. alkyl groups) will tend to destabilize the carboxylate ion and thus decreases the acidity of the acid. (Table 20.2) lists values of pKa for some substituted carboxylic acids, showing how electron-withdrawing groups and electron-donating groups affect the strength of an acid.",
