      "theory": "The substances, which decrease the activity of an enzyme, known as enzyme\ninhibitor and this process is known as enzyme inhibition.\nLipids have three major subclasses: simple lipids, compound lipids and derived\nlipids.\nThe monomer that makes up a nucleic acid is called a nucleotide.\nNucleotide has three parts: an inorganic phosphate group, a five-carbon monosaccharide sugar, and a nitrogen containing structure known as a nitrogenous\nbase.\nDNA contains genetic information in the form of codes.\nMinerals essential for life are the inorganic nutrients.\nIron is a part of haemoglobin, a carrier of oxygen in the body. Calcium is the main constituent of bones and teeth. Living organism cannot synthesized the essential minerals.\nExercise\nQI. Choose the correct one from the given options.\n1. The optimum pH of pepsin is around\na. 3.0\nc. 2.0\nd. 2.5\n2. The most abundant carbohydrate found in nature is\na. cellulose\nb. 3.5\nb. glycogen\nc. pectin\n3. The glycosidic linkage is present between the molecules of\na. glucose\nc. fatty acid\n4. Steroid belong to family of\na. carbohydrates\nc. lipids\nd. chitin\nFor Z\nb. glycine d. none of these\n&amp; non\nb, proteins\nd. enzymes\n5. Protein present in haemoglobin has the structure known as\na. primary structure.\nc. tertiary structure.\nb. secondary structure.\nd. quaternary structure.\n6. Which carbohydrate does not conform the general formula C,(H,O),?\na. Deoxyribose b. Ribose\nc. Glucose\n7. Ethanol is produced from starch by the process of\na. hydrolysis\nc. decomposition\nb. hydrogenation.\nd. Fructose\nin\nd. fermentation\n306\n8. Human are unable to digest\na. glucose\nc. starch\nb. cellulose\nNOT FOR SALE\nd. denatured protein\n9. Which one of the following term is not related to term aldohexose?\na. Aldehyde\nb. Ketone\nc. Six\n10. Slow healing of wounds is due the deficiency of\na. Ca\nb. P\n11. RNA is primarily seen in\na. nucleus\nc. cell wall\nd. Sugar\nc. Zn\nd. Fe\nb. cytoplasm\nd. cell membrane\n12. In which of following disaccharides both the basic structural units are the same?\na. Maltose\nb. Lactose\n13. In competitive inhibition, the inhibitor\na. competes with enzyme.\nc. binds with substrate.\nc. Sucrose\nd. All of these\nb. irreversibly binds with the enzyme. d. competes with substrate.\n14. In DNA, adenine forms a complementary pair with which other nitrogen base?\na. Uracil\nb. Cytosine\n15. Proteins are polymers of\na. glucose\nc. amino acids\nc. Thymine d. Guanine\nb. disulphide bridges\nd. peptide bonds\n2. Write short answers of the following questions.\nWhy vegetable oil is sensitive to rancidity?\nWhy melting point of saturated fatty acid is higher than unsaturated fatty acid? iii. Make at least one point difference in each compound in the given pairs.\nDifferentiate between the following compounds.\na. Glucose and fructose\nc. Cellulose and starch\nb. Sucrose and maltose\niv. Write down at least three important functions of nucleic acids. V. Write down the products of hydrolysis of sucrose with reaction. vi. What happens when protein is denatured?\nvii. What are chemical components that compose the nucleic acid?\nviii. Describe briefly the primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure of protein. ix. Differentiate between non-competitive and uncompetitive inhibitions.\nx. Write down the physical and chemical properties of lipids.\nWrite detail answers of the following questions.\n1. (a).\n(b). How would\nclassification of carbohydrate.\n2. (a). Describe various classes of protein along with examples.\n(b). Enlist the properties of protein.\nyou evaluate the role of various carbohydrates in health and diseases?\n307\nNOT FOR SALE\n3. (a). What are lipids? Explain the basis of classification of lipids.\n(b). Discuss in detail the structure and nutritional and biological importance of lipids.\n(a). Explain the role of inhibitors of enzyme catalysed reactions.\n(b). Identify the different structural components of DNA and RNA.\n5. (a). Assess the role of enzyme as biocatalyst and relate this role to various functions\nsuch as digestion of food.\n(b). Evaluate the factors that affect enzyme activity such as effect of temperature\nand pH.\n6. (a). Why zinc is important for human being?\n(b). State the medical problems that may relate to calcium and phosphorus.\nProject\nTake 250cm3 milk in a beaker. Gently warm the milk (not boil), add small amount of lemon juice or vinegar (acetic acid) in it and observe it. Report your observation by answering the following questions.\n1. What happen to the state of the milk? 2. Is there any chemical change occur in it? 3. Does the change in pH affect the milk? 4. Name the product obtained during the process. 5. Is this product has any nutritional importance?\n6. Name some products in which it is used.\nawaz e in\n308\n26\nNOT FORSALE\nUnit 22\nB",
