      "content": "<p>Figure 24.5 Different type of bond vibrations in methylene group.</p><p>The Infrared Spectrum</p><p>The plot of wavenumber, cm⁻¹ (or sometimes wavelength in micron, μm) versus percent transmittance (%T) is called Infrared spectrum. This is recorded as percent transmittance because the spectrometer records the ratio of the intensities of the two beams.</p><p>Where Iₛ is the intensity of the sample beam, and Iᵣ is the intensity of the reference beam. In many parts of the spectrum, the transmittance is nearly 100%, meaning that the sample is nearly transparent (no absorbance) to radiation of that wavenumber.</p><p>In using infrared spectroscopy for structure determination, peaks in the range 4000–1500 cm⁻¹ are usually emphasized because this is the region in which the vibrations characteristic of particular functional groups are found and therefore called functional groups region. The region 1500–625 cm⁻¹ is known as the fingerprint region. It is highly unlikely that the IR spectra of two different compounds (except enantiomers) will show the same frequencies for all of their various complex vibrations. Hence, this region of the infrared spectrum provides a “fingerprint” of a molecule. The IR spectra of ethanol and toluene are shown in Figure 24.6 and 24.7.</p><p>Figure 24.6 IR spectrum of ethanol.</p><p>Figure 24.7 IR spectrum of toluene.</p><p>Applications of Infrared Spectroscopy</p><p>i. Infrared spectroscopy gives information about presence of different organic functional groups in a molecule.</p><p>ii. The identity of a compound can also be established by comparing the finger print region of infrared spectrum with that of a known compound.</p><p>iii. The presence of some extra absorption bands, where the compounds does not absorb, shows the presence of impurities. For example the presence of cyclohexanone impurity in cyclohexanol can be readily detected by the intense carbonyl band at 1715 cm⁻¹ in spectrum of cyclohexanol.</p><p>iv. The progress of most of the reactions can also be monitored using infrared spectroscopy, by taking the spectra of small samples of reaction mixture continuously at suitable intervals of time. For example, in oxidation of cyclohexanol to that of cyclohexanone is accompanied by the disappearance of O–H band near 3600 cm⁻¹, and the appearance of C=O band at 1715 cm⁻¹</p>",
